WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.760
- You cannot control what
a great military event

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.000
can set loose. More battle
casualties than US forces had

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.880
suffered in every war combined
down to that point happened in two

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:15.740
days. Two and a half months after
that, George B. McClellan's army

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:20.150
engaged with Lee's army in what came to be
called the Seven Days Battles at Richmond,

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:24.240
36,000 casualties. So
those two together just

00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:27.470
showed that this was gonna
be on a totally different

00:00:27.500 --> 00:00:31.490
scale. He added a
psychological twist. What,

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.580
what Grant envisioned his doing, and
Sheridan actually did it in the Shenandoah

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:39.120
Valley before Sherman did it
in Georgia, is go into an

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.100
agriculturally productive area,
burn the mills, kill the livestock,

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:48.900
destroy the crops, lay waste to it so that
it can't be a logistical support of the

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.900
Confederacy anymore. Sheridan did it in
the Shenandoah Valley, and then Sherman

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.540
did it in Georgia. But Sherman said,
"But I'm also going to break them

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:00.420
psychologically. I'm gonna show them that
my army can go wherever it wants to go

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:04.540
whenever it wants to go, and there's nothing
Jefferson Davis can do about it." The great

00:01:04.599 --> 00:01:08.460
forces are all that matters and individuals
don't really matter notion of history I

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.340
think is ridiculous. Individuals
really matter. For the

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.810
United States, Lincoln and Grant
matter beyond calculation-

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.800
... in this war. And on the Confederate side,
if you take Lee out of the picture, the

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:24.450
war doesn't last nearly as long as it
does. In terms of causation, if you take

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.180
slavery out of the picture,
there is no Civil War, period.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.000
- The following is a conversation
with Gary Gallagher, a

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.740
historian of the American Civil War.

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:40.840
This is the Lex Fridman Podcast. To
support it, please check out our sponsors

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:44.600
in the description where you can
also find links to contact me,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:48.500
ask questions, give feedback,
and so on. And now, dear

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:56.520
friends, here's Gary Gallagher. The
American Civil War broke out in 1861.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:01.380
What were the causes of the
Civil War? Could it have been

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:03.560
avoided, or was it inevitable?

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.240
- I don't know if anything is inevitable in
history, but it's certainly safe to say

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:11.740
that the conditions were in
place to make it possible-

00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:15.790
... by the winter of 1860, '61, and
the conditions that made it possible

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:19.840
were all related to the
institution of slavery and

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.900
its effects both in the states where
slavery was allowed and the states

00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.900
where it was not allowed. And there
had been a series of tensions

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:30.750
that grew in the decade
preceding the 1850s.

00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:35.680
It's not that one side wants to get
rid of slavery and the other side

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:39.640
wants to protect slavery. I'm talking about
the white people in the United States now

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:42.240
who would be making the
political decisions.

00:02:43.260 --> 00:02:47.160
It's a question of whether slavery was
going to be able to expand into federal

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.240
territories. That is what really created

00:02:51.380 --> 00:02:55.100
the starkest difference between
the two sections in the

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:01.400
1850s, even in the late 1840s.
The issues that came to most, uh,

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:06.200
perilous a point in the view of many people
then had to do with whether slavery would

00:03:06.260 --> 00:03:09.840
be allowed only where it already
existed, or would it be allowed to

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:13.740
expand. If it could expand,
then it seemed that it might

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:17.180
be conceivable at some point even
that the slaveholding states

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:21.450
could end up with a majority.
They were in a minority by 1860,

00:03:21.549 --> 00:03:25.460
'61 and feeling beleaguered, feeling
threatened. They'd long since lost

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:27.670
the population race to the free states.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:32.580
Early in the nation, the most powerful states
were slaveholding states. Virginia was by

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.020
far the most important state.
Four of the first five

00:03:36.200 --> 00:03:39.880
presidents were from Virginia,
all slaveholders, all prominent

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:44.740
members of the slaveholding class. Those
days were long gone by the late 1850s.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.000
- So that's an important thing to
say. It wasn't before the Civil

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.840
War a discussion about abolishing
slavery. It's about whether

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.900
slavery, the institution of
slavery should be expanded.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:59.900
- Allowed to expand into federal
territories. There, there is a portion

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:04.900
of the white population that
is adamantly in favor of

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:08.880
getting rid of slavery immediately. The abolitionists,
they... There was even a political party

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:14.720
called the Liberty Party, that ran a
candidate in 1840 and they got a handful of

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:18.540
thousand votes. They reached their
high point in 1844. They got about

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:20.060
two and a half percent of the vote.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:24.720
The abolitionists are a tiny minority
among the— even in the North, tiny

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:28.740
minority among the white people
of the United States. The African

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:32.240
American population, nearly four
million of them by 1860, of

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.130
course, would have embraced it
instantly and yearned for it. But in

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:41.440
the white population, the abolitionists are
a tiny minority. They are much despised

00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:45.460
even across much of the North
because they were seen as agitators

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:49.360
who roiled the Union and
deepened rifts that might

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:51.260
bring a significant political break.

00:04:52.100 --> 00:04:55.730
- So how did this escalate
to a civil war, this

00:04:55.780 --> 00:04:59.620
almost policy discussion about the
institution of slavery? How did it

00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:00.480
actually escalate?

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:04.540
- It escalated into a civil war because
in the presidential election of

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:08.540
1860... There'd been two party systems
in the United States. The first

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.740
party system, the Republicans, the Jeffersonian
Republicans versus the Federalists.

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:18.060
After that, there was, the two parties
were the Whig Party against the

00:05:18.100 --> 00:05:22.060
Democratic Party. The Democratic Party grew
out of the Jeffersonian Republican Party.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:22.979
It's the same party.

00:05:23.900 --> 00:05:28.600
The Whig Party foundered to a significant
degree on the issue of slavery.

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.440
How do you maintain a national party
that has a wing in the Southern

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:34.680
states and has a wing

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:39.350
in the Northern free states that
can handle the issue of slavery?

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:43.219
The Democrats managed to do that. The
Whigs could not do it, and so the Whig

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:46.780
Party ran their last candidate
in 1852, presidential candidate,

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:50.580
significant candidate. And the
Republican Party emerged in the

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:54.400
mid-1850s, took most of the
old Whigs into the Republican

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:58.200
ranks and one of the Republican,
the key Republican platform plank

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:01.820
was no expansion of slavery
into the territories.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.340
Not abolish slavery, no
expansion. That was the key in

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:09.260
1856 when they ran their first
candidate. In 1860, that

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.900
is again the key plank in the Republican
platform. The Republican platform in

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:19.644
1860 says where slavery exists it will
continue, but it cannot expand into the

00:06:19.684 --> 00:06:23.524
territories. When white Southerners
saw that, that meant that

00:06:23.564 --> 00:06:26.744
they were doomed to become an increasingly

00:06:27.084 --> 00:06:30.924
minority section in the nation,
and you would have a federal

00:06:31.024 --> 00:06:34.704
government increasingly dominated by the
free states that could then do the one

00:06:34.824 --> 00:06:38.624
thing that the white South could
not countenance, and that is

00:06:38.684 --> 00:06:41.984
tamper with the institution
of slavery in the South.

00:06:43.544 --> 00:06:47.014
The presidential election of 1860
brought the destruction of the

00:06:47.164 --> 00:06:51.044
last national party, which was
the Democrats. They couldn't even

00:06:51.204 --> 00:06:54.914
settle on a candidate, so you end up with
a Northern Democratic candidate running,

00:06:55.024 --> 00:06:58.604
Stephen A. Douglas, and a
Southern candidate running. The

00:06:58.664 --> 00:07:02.264
convention fractured and John C.
Breckinridge ran on the, on the Southern

00:07:02.324 --> 00:07:06.134
Democrat platform. You had a
Unionist candidate running who just

00:07:06.134 --> 00:07:09.404
didn't wanna talk about slavery, let's
talk about the Union, and you had Lincoln

00:07:10.064 --> 00:07:13.924
running as a Republican. When Lincoln
won, the white South looked at Lincoln

00:07:14.004 --> 00:07:17.864
winning. Here's a guy who
has said that he doesn't

00:07:17.874 --> 00:07:21.764
think slavery would expand, but
he'd said more than that. He

00:07:21.884 --> 00:07:25.604
scared the white South more because
in 1858, both Lincoln and William

00:07:25.624 --> 00:07:28.934
Henry Seward, these are two
prominent, prominent Republicans,

00:07:29.544 --> 00:07:33.484
had said basically in separate
speeches, the House Divided Speech

00:07:33.544 --> 00:07:37.404
on Lincoln's part, the Irrepressible
Conflict Speech on Seward's part,

00:07:37.564 --> 00:07:41.384
that the nation could not continue in
the long term half slave and half free.

00:07:42.044 --> 00:07:45.904
And everybody in the South knew
which way they wanted it to be. They

00:07:45.924 --> 00:07:49.184
would want it to be no slavery.
And so Lincoln's election

00:07:51.044 --> 00:07:54.584
scared the white South enough
so that seven of the 15

00:07:54.664 --> 00:07:58.544
slave-holding states seceded in
response to Lincoln's election.

00:07:58.584 --> 00:08:01.703
They seceded between December 20th,
when South Carolina went out,

00:08:02.644 --> 00:08:05.724
and the end of February. You
had the deep South went out.

00:08:06.244 --> 00:08:10.174
- What was the general view amongst
the populace towards slavery? Let's

00:08:10.204 --> 00:08:15.414
kind of lay it out. Let's you know,
for the audience looking back,

00:08:16.704 --> 00:08:20.454
let's put ourselves in the
mind space of how people

00:08:20.544 --> 00:08:24.883
saw the world, how people saw slavery,
how people saw African Americans.

00:08:24.924 --> 00:08:28.474
- Well, the baseline among the
white Americans, there were about

00:08:28.624 --> 00:08:34.633
31, shade more than 31 million Americans
in 1860 for the census of 1860.

00:08:35.264 --> 00:08:38.664
About four million of them
were African Americans,

00:08:39.144 --> 00:08:43.024
three and a half million of those
enslaved in the South. Anyway,

00:08:44.184 --> 00:08:45.804
among the white population,

00:08:47.304 --> 00:08:50.544
the average attitude toward Black
people is what we would call

00:08:50.684 --> 00:08:54.584
profoundly prejudiced. Doesn't matter whether
you... A lot of people think this is a

00:08:54.644 --> 00:08:58.224
Southern white thing. They're the only ones
who were prejudiced. By our standards, the

00:08:58.304 --> 00:09:01.924
overwhelming majority of
people in the free states

00:09:02.204 --> 00:09:06.044
were also deeply prejudiced.
It's important to

00:09:06.124 --> 00:09:09.944
keep in mind that attitudes towards
slavery and attitudes toward race

00:09:10.384 --> 00:09:14.244
do not, they're not necessarily the
same. I mean, you could be against

00:09:14.264 --> 00:09:17.544
slavery and still be prejudiced against
Black people. By our standards, many

00:09:17.624 --> 00:09:20.633
abolitionists were prejudiced
against Black people. They...

00:09:21.564 --> 00:09:24.164
But the overwhelming
majority of the white North,

00:09:25.604 --> 00:09:29.554
in the North, often had a hard time getting
my students to understand the demographics

00:09:29.584 --> 00:09:34.934
in 1860. The free states in
1860 were more than 98% white.

00:09:35.864 --> 00:09:39.584
98% white. African Americans are a tiny

00:09:39.644 --> 00:09:43.464
minority in the free states. Black
people lived in the 15 slaveholding

00:09:43.484 --> 00:09:47.184
states in 1860. Most of
the white North, by our

00:09:47.264 --> 00:09:51.244
standards, was very prejudiced.
They were against the expansion of

00:09:51.284 --> 00:09:54.644
slavery into the territories because
they thought that would circumscribe

00:09:54.684 --> 00:09:59.504
white opportunities to make an economic
life in these federal territories,

00:10:01.144 --> 00:10:04.924
and they didn't want that to happen.
About 45% of the people who lived

00:10:05.104 --> 00:10:08.724
in the free states were Democrats.
Most Democrats had no problem with

00:10:08.764 --> 00:10:12.254
slavery. I mean, absolutely no
problem with the institution of

00:10:12.344 --> 00:10:15.664
slavery. There would be more
problems among Republicans,

00:10:16.424 --> 00:10:20.404
especially with extension of slavery,
although many Democrats, white

00:10:20.484 --> 00:10:22.564
Democrats in the North
were against that as well.

00:10:23.764 --> 00:10:27.624
The white South was overwhelmingly
in favor of slavery. You

00:10:27.704 --> 00:10:31.074
often read the argument, the kind
of neo-Confederate argument that

00:10:31.724 --> 00:10:34.904
they'll talk about how most Confederate
soldiers didn't own slaves. That's true.

00:10:35.144 --> 00:10:38.994
The a large majority of families in
the South did not own a single slave.

00:10:39.484 --> 00:10:43.314
But all white Southerners had a stake
in slavery. They had a stake in

00:10:43.344 --> 00:10:45.184
it as a form of labor control,

00:10:46.144 --> 00:10:49.764
but more important than that, they had
a stake in it as a means of social

00:10:49.844 --> 00:10:56.244
control. They knew, no matter what
they said later in the Jim Crow era of

00:10:56.264 --> 00:11:00.224
how happy Black people were in the institution
of slavery, they knew that Black people,

00:11:00.284 --> 00:11:04.254
of course, did not want to be enslaved, and
they were afraid of what would happen if

00:11:04.284 --> 00:11:08.114
they weren't enslaved anymore. They had in
the backs of their minds Haiti. Is that

00:11:08.144 --> 00:11:11.664
what's gonna... If you live in one of the
coastal districts of South Carolina where

00:11:11.864 --> 00:11:15.744
90% of the residents are enslaved
and you're a white person, you

00:11:15.764 --> 00:11:19.174
probably don't sleep all that soundly
at night if you think there's any

00:11:19.284 --> 00:11:23.224
chance that the Black people are not
gonna remain under the control of the

00:11:23.264 --> 00:11:27.144
white people. So all white people in
the South had a stake in slavery,

00:11:27.344 --> 00:11:31.204
not just some. All of them did, and
there were all kinds of ways that

00:11:31.264 --> 00:11:35.004
it would go back and forth.
If you were a modest

00:11:35.084 --> 00:11:38.884
farmer and grew a little bit of cotton, you
didn't have your own cotton gin, you...

00:11:39.104 --> 00:11:43.044
but your more wealthy neighbor had a
gin, could gin your cotton there, and

00:11:43.084 --> 00:11:46.784
then you would take your turn on the slave
patrol, the slave patrols. So there's a

00:11:47.064 --> 00:11:49.624
reciprocity in that regard.

00:11:50.144 --> 00:11:53.984
- This is perhaps a bit outside the
scope of what we'll talk about today,

00:11:54.084 --> 00:11:57.984
but just to lay out the context
on slavery in general in human

00:11:58.044 --> 00:12:01.864
history, we should say that slavery was

00:12:01.924 --> 00:12:05.204
ubiquitous. It existed
in virtually every major

00:12:05.224 --> 00:12:08.044
civilization in the history of humans.

00:12:08.964 --> 00:12:12.604
Mesopotamia, Egypt, Greece,
Rome, China, the Islamic

00:12:12.684 --> 00:12:15.904
world, Africa, the Americas,
North and South America.

00:12:16.684 --> 00:12:19.874
We should also say something
that, as I was looking into this,

00:12:20.224 --> 00:12:25.440
another big topic, which is the Atlantic
slave trade. And it was surprising to me,

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:30.500
the stat I wasn't aware of, I would venture
to say most Americans probably don't know,

00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:37.480
that only 4% of African slaves were brought
to North America, to the United States.

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:39.439
- Brazil is the main destination.

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:43.600
- Brazil, the Caribbean was
over 90%, 4.9 million in

00:12:43.660 --> 00:12:45.760
Brazil, 4 million in the Caribbean,

00:12:46.640 --> 00:12:49.620
and it was also the last of the
Americas to abolish the slave trade. So

00:12:50.560 --> 00:12:55.880
only 4% of the Atlantic slave trade
went to the United States. So there's--

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:00.980
the story of human slavery
is one that reaches deep

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:05.740
into our history and
geographically is much

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:09.620
larger than we often study in the
context of the United States.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:13.880
- One thing the war, the Civil War, which
I'll just call the war for the rest of

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:17.300
our discussion, I mean, one
thing it did was end slavery

00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:20.500
in four years in the United States.

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:26.640
Whereas in the absence of that war, it
might have gone on for decades. I think the

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:30.400
United States would have had the
ignominious distinction of being the last

00:13:30.480 --> 00:13:34.440
place to get rid of slavery, would have been
after Brazil, would have been after Spanish

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:38.300
Cuba, because it was thriving in
the United States in 1860. It was

00:13:38.360 --> 00:13:42.300
making the transition from
cash crop agriculture-

00:13:42.520 --> 00:13:46.410
... to other, to industrial
applications and other applications

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:53.840
growing of non-- not growing, growing
just cotton and rice and sugar. It was--

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:58.420
There was a time in the literature
when people argued that the war

00:13:58.460 --> 00:14:02.320
was needless, the Civil War was needless because
slavery was on its way out. That's absolutely

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:06.320
wrong. It was not on its way out. It was
thriving. Lincoln said at one point he

00:14:06.340 --> 00:14:10.280
thought it might have lasted another
fifty years, and I don't know how many

00:14:10.320 --> 00:14:14.140
years it would have lasted, but I think it would
have lasted longer than it did in either Cuba or

00:14:14.220 --> 00:14:16.840
Brazil if it hadn't, if the
war hadn't taken place.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:20.579
- So slavery was flourishing
in the United States from-

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:21.160
- Flourishing.

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:23.840
- ... from an economics
perspective- ... especially.

00:14:24.240 --> 00:14:27.980
- The wealthiest part...
The 1860 census is our

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:31.900
friend, and it tells us that
the people who controlled the

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:35.940
most wealth in the United States were
slaveholders, and their property in

00:14:36.020 --> 00:14:39.690
slaves alone, not their other
holdings, not their land holdings,

00:14:39.820 --> 00:14:45.620
just property in slaves were valued at
about three billion dollars. The value

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:49.809
of all industries, all railroads,
all of that put together,

00:14:50.500 --> 00:14:54.440
fewer than two point five billion dollars.
So the people who controlled the most

00:14:54.500 --> 00:14:58.000
wealth, by far, were
the slaveholding class.

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:02.540
- So it was the most valuable
industry in the United States,

00:15:02.900 --> 00:15:04.490
and perhaps it is the reason,

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:09.520
Again, a bit out of scope, but
maybe it helps lay out the land.

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:13.160
Let's look at the nineteenth century. So

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:18.180
most slave-owning societies of the
era were able to abolish slavery

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:21.860
without people dying at the mass
scale that we'll discuss. So

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:26.000
Britain freed eight hundred
thousand slaves in 1833-

00:15:26.360 --> 00:15:33.020
... By buying their freedom. Russia freed
twenty-three million serfs in 1861 with

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:36.600
a decree, so legally. Brazil, which

00:15:36.700 --> 00:15:40.430
imported more than ten times as many
enslaved Africans as the United

00:15:40.540 --> 00:15:45.740
States, abolished slavery in 1888
through basic ordinary legislation.

00:15:46.700 --> 00:15:50.260
No violence. Why did
the US have a civil war

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:54.120
with an estimated seven hundred
and fifty thousand people dead in

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:55.280
order to abolish slavery?

00:15:55.620 --> 00:15:59.460
- Yes. That's-- Let's talk just for a second
about the seven hundred and fifty thousand

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:03.960
dead soldiers we're talking about.
The number for years was six

00:16:04.000 --> 00:16:08.500
hundred and eighteen thousand,
and that's been revised lately.

00:16:09.320 --> 00:16:13.240
I think we're probably certainly more than seven
hundred thousand. By far the bloodiest war in,

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:17.240
in our history as a percentage of
the population off the charts,

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:21.160
by far the bloodiest. I think there's
a big difference between Great

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:25.050
Britain doing it and the United States
doing it, is Great Britain did it in other

00:16:25.140 --> 00:16:30.760
places. There weren't millions of enslaved
people in Great Britain at the time.

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:35.280
It's-- I think, for them, it might
have been a little bit easier to free

00:16:35.340 --> 00:16:39.200
enslaved people who don't live among
you than it... In the United States,

00:16:39.260 --> 00:16:43.240
the ante is up for the Confederacy because they,
the enslaved people lived here, and I think they

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:46.059
were, a lot of them were afraid of them
and what would happen if they weren't

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:51.000
enslaved anymore. The war goes
from... It doesn't begin as a

00:16:51.040 --> 00:16:54.020
war to end slavery. There's
a major shift in the war,

00:16:54.660 --> 00:16:58.500
begins in the summer of
1862, where you add to the

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:02.180
war. The war aim for the United
States initially is to suppress the

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:06.110
rebellion, which is what they
called this. They didn't call

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:10.040
it the Civil War for the most part during
the war. It's the War of the Rebellion.

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:14.860
That's the goal initially. As the
war goes on and becomes costlier

00:17:14.900 --> 00:17:18.800
and more all-encompassing, they decide
that they're not gonna be able to suppress

00:17:18.820 --> 00:17:21.890
the rebellion unless they also
strike at slavery, because

00:17:21.940 --> 00:17:25.850
slavery underpins the
Confederate war-making capacity.

00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:30.460
You have enslaved labor that keeps the
economy going and allows the Confederacy

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:34.240
to mobilize between eighty-five and
ninety percent of all their white

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:37.580
military-aged males. It's an
astonishing mobilization. It's only

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:41.240
possible because enslaved labor keeps
the economy going. If you take that

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:47.080
enslaved labor away, it deals a significant
blow to the Confederate economy and,

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:50.800
and by extension, to its ability
to wage a nineteenth century war.

00:17:51.320 --> 00:17:56.380
- So the Emancipation Proclamation
was in part a military strategy.

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.640
- Abraham Lincoln based it entirely

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:04.300
on his war-making power as
commander-in-chief in a time of open

00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:08.240
rebellion. His inclination, there's
no-- He's often been criticized

00:18:08.260 --> 00:18:11.640
for not including the soaring
language he was capable of in the

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:15.300
Emancipation Proclamation. One
historian likened it to a bill of

00:18:15.360 --> 00:18:19.300
lading. It had all the soaring
language of a bill of lading. He

00:18:19.340 --> 00:18:23.040
did that on purpose. He did it because he,

00:18:23.580 --> 00:18:27.340
he doesn't have the power to strike at
slavery in the absence of a war. The

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:28.640
president doesn't. And

00:18:29.656 --> 00:18:33.056
he did it because he knows that
he can't win the war with only

00:18:33.096 --> 00:18:36.616
Republicans in the army. He needs to keep
Democrats on board with the war as well.

00:18:36.736 --> 00:18:40.596
They're not in favor of emancipation.
The one way that they might accept it

00:18:41.236 --> 00:18:45.176
is as a tool to help achieve union, and

00:18:45.236 --> 00:18:49.096
to do that we've got to strike at
the rebels this way, strike at

00:18:49.156 --> 00:18:52.996
slavery. That will hurt their war effort, and it
will also punish the people who brought on the war,

00:18:53.316 --> 00:18:56.776
and that would make... So
Lincoln hoped that by basing it

00:18:56.956 --> 00:19:00.596
strictly on his war power, it
might be more widely accepted

00:19:01.416 --> 00:19:05.336
across all parts of the
loyal states and not as

00:19:05.376 --> 00:19:09.336
controversial as it was. It was still
intensely controversial. It alienated

00:19:09.496 --> 00:19:13.356
many Democrats when he issued it,
who said, "You're changing what this

00:19:13.396 --> 00:19:17.246
war is about. We're willing to shed
our blood and maybe risk our lives to

00:19:17.276 --> 00:19:21.176
restore the union of the founders.
I'm not gonna risk my life to

00:19:21.256 --> 00:19:25.576
free a Black person." That's a very,
very common attitude among Democrats.

00:19:25.956 --> 00:19:29.856
- So what did the Emancipation
Proclamation actually do? It

00:19:30.016 --> 00:19:35.256
emphasized the freeing
of slaves in the South.

00:19:35.816 --> 00:19:39.696
- It's even more complicated than that.
The only place that Lincoln said he had

00:19:39.716 --> 00:19:45.276
the power as the war leader to
strike at slavery is where the

00:19:45.296 --> 00:19:48.996
rebels were actually still in
rebellion and in control. So

00:19:49.176 --> 00:19:53.076
any place where the US Army was in
control, any place-- He said that

00:19:53.176 --> 00:19:56.956
it is in essence United States
territory now. I can't free them there

00:19:57.016 --> 00:20:00.856
because I don't have the power. I can
only free them where we don't have the

00:20:00.956 --> 00:20:04.856
power. So he exempted parts of
states and areas in the South

00:20:04.916 --> 00:20:08.856
that it didn't apply to as of the
moment he signed it. Going forward,

00:20:10.536 --> 00:20:14.496
any place that the United States Army
went where it hadn't been on January 1st,

00:20:14.556 --> 00:20:20.436
1863, the Emancipation Proclamation
applied and enslaved people would be

00:20:20.516 --> 00:20:24.236
freed by it. At the, literally at the
time he signed it, he didn't free

00:20:24.296 --> 00:20:28.176
anybody because he's only saying, "I can free
them where we're not in control. We have to be

00:20:28.216 --> 00:20:31.356
in control for this to have effect
and for this to have power."

00:20:31.716 --> 00:20:39.196
- So in part, this is why the army was the
boundary which the freeing was happening.

00:20:39.256 --> 00:20:43.096
- It's, it's, it's absolutely the boundary,
and Congress had already passed what was

00:20:43.136 --> 00:20:47.696
called the Second Confiscation Act.
It passed this in July of 1862

00:20:48.516 --> 00:20:52.356
that announced that all
enslaved people belonging

00:20:52.366 --> 00:20:55.996
to rebels were free. It didn't apply

00:20:56.056 --> 00:20:59.646
to people who could prove they were
unionists living in the South and owned

00:20:59.736 --> 00:21:03.296
slaves. Lincoln didn't make that distinction
in the Emancipation Proclamation.

00:21:03.356 --> 00:21:07.136
His proclamation is broader than
the Second Confiscation Act was.

00:21:07.996 --> 00:21:11.776
- Okay. There's so many tangents we can
take, but let's zoom out once again.

00:21:12.576 --> 00:21:16.536
There's a difference between cause, as
you've talked about, cause and motivation.

00:21:16.636 --> 00:21:17.516
- Absolutely.

00:21:17.596 --> 00:21:21.096
- Quite a large number of
people volunteered to fight.

00:21:21.616 --> 00:21:24.936
- In fact, a majority of all the
soldiers who fought on both

00:21:24.996 --> 00:21:28.916
sides were volunteers during
the Civil War. About 3 million

00:21:29.036 --> 00:21:34.316
served, 2.2 million in the United
States, 800, 900,000 in the Confederacy.

00:21:36.096 --> 00:21:39.916
It's, again, I talk about things that are hard to
get students to understand. This is one of them.

00:21:39.976 --> 00:21:43.896
They have to separate... The
causation and motivation are

00:21:43.976 --> 00:21:47.956
often conflated, and not just by students
but also by lots of talking heads on

00:21:48.016 --> 00:21:51.896
newscasts in the United States who
don't know anything about United States

00:21:51.916 --> 00:21:55.856
history- ... but know what they
know. In terms of causation, if

00:21:55.896 --> 00:21:59.376
you take slavery out of the picture,
there is no Civil War, period.

00:21:59.696 --> 00:22:03.076
That's not a long conversation you need.
You ask the question. That's the answer.

00:22:03.136 --> 00:22:04.416
Let's go to the next question.

00:22:04.556 --> 00:22:07.945
The next question is, okay,
so were most white people

00:22:08.276 --> 00:22:11.996
motivated to put on blue uniforms
because they wanted to kill

00:22:12.096 --> 00:22:16.036
slavery? The answer to that is no,
they were not. They were motivated

00:22:16.076 --> 00:22:19.176
to put on blue uniforms
to salvage the Union,

00:22:19.916 --> 00:22:23.516
which they saw as a unique gift that the

00:22:23.556 --> 00:22:27.416
founding generation, based on the
bloodshed in the American Revolution,

00:22:27.456 --> 00:22:31.276
had bequeathed the successive generations.
And the two key parts of what the

00:22:31.376 --> 00:22:33.776
Union meant for them was, number one,

00:22:35.416 --> 00:22:39.236
you had a voice in your own government,
and someone will always say now to me,

00:22:39.246 --> 00:22:40.816
"Well, but women couldn't
vote." That's right.

00:22:42.536 --> 00:22:46.436
The people who could vote by our
standards were limited in the 19th

00:22:46.475 --> 00:22:49.946
century, but within the context
of the Western world in

00:22:49.996 --> 00:22:53.506
1860, the United States had by
far the broadest franchise, by

00:22:53.536 --> 00:22:57.486
far. And so they argued that's one
thing the Union gives us, and it

00:22:57.556 --> 00:23:01.536
also gives us the chance to rise
economically. Doesn't guarantee that you can

00:23:01.606 --> 00:23:05.656
succeed, but it means you're not
doomed to be what your father was.

00:23:06.076 --> 00:23:09.956
And those are worth fighting for. And
so I will put on a uniform so the

00:23:10.016 --> 00:23:13.536
slave-holding aristocrats and
oligarchs, which is what they called

00:23:13.576 --> 00:23:17.086
them, of the South, they said they're not...
They don't really believe in democracy.

00:23:17.156 --> 00:23:20.856
It's an oligarchy- ... in the South.
It's a slavery-based oligarchy,

00:23:21.756 --> 00:23:25.436
and we know which way Europe's going,
they said. The revolutions of the

00:23:25.456 --> 00:23:29.356
1840s failed in Europe. Europe's going
the wrong way. They're going toward

00:23:29.416 --> 00:23:31.936
more aristocracy, more oligarchy, more...

00:23:33.036 --> 00:23:36.976
And we are the last hope.
When Lincoln spoke of

00:23:37.016 --> 00:23:40.836
the last best hope of Earth,
which he did in one of his most

00:23:40.876 --> 00:23:43.096
memorable phrases, he's meaning

00:23:43.956 --> 00:23:47.816
that the United States is a
beacon of small D democracy in a

00:23:47.856 --> 00:23:51.636
Western world that seems to be turning
the other way. And those soldiers,

00:23:52.036 --> 00:23:56.166
it meant, it meant it was that important
to them. They are citizens in a republic

00:23:57.056 --> 00:24:00.966
that people shed blood to
establish, and we can't let it

00:24:00.996 --> 00:24:04.896
go. Part of the compact is
I'm a citizen. I derive these

00:24:05.036 --> 00:24:07.276
benefits from this
republic, from the Union.

00:24:08.476 --> 00:24:12.396
I can have a voice in government. I can
rise economically or have the chance to.

00:24:12.796 --> 00:24:16.756
I owe my service to that republic.
That's what most of them would've

00:24:16.796 --> 00:24:20.726
said if you'd pressed them on why they're doing it.
Doesn't mean everybody was on fire with ideology

00:24:20.816 --> 00:24:24.596
that way. Some guys would say, "Well, you
know what? I'm broke, and I'm gonna get

00:24:24.656 --> 00:24:30.786
fed and clothed in the army." But this
sense of exceptionalism, exceptionalism's

00:24:30.856 --> 00:24:34.764
unpopular now in the United
States, but Americans had a

00:24:34.824 --> 00:24:38.414
sense of themselves as a truly
exceptionalist society within the

00:24:38.444 --> 00:24:39.974
context of the Western world.

00:24:40.424 --> 00:24:43.034
- And as a small aside terminology-wise,

00:24:44.164 --> 00:24:47.274
blue is the Union or what we
think of as the United States,

00:24:48.044 --> 00:24:49.534
and red is the Confederacy.

00:24:49.884 --> 00:24:53.764
- Yes. On the maps, the Confederate
units are red blocks. And the United

00:24:53.864 --> 00:24:55.624
States units are blue
blocks. That's right.

00:24:56.804 --> 00:24:57.194
- And, uh

00:24:57.504 --> 00:24:58.624
- That is correct.

00:24:58.664 --> 00:25:03.204
- And the North and the South is another
way we'll refer to it just to clarify.

00:25:03.424 --> 00:25:06.684
- And let me have a very brief
rant on North and South.

00:25:06.864 --> 00:25:12.044
I prefer to see this as a war between
two mid-19th-century republics,

00:25:12.324 --> 00:25:16.284
One of which didn't last very long
and spent its whole life in the midst

00:25:16.324 --> 00:25:20.304
of a great war. There were 15
slaveholding states in 1860.

00:25:20.644 --> 00:25:23.544
Only 11 of them seceded
from the United States.

00:25:23.624 --> 00:25:25.584
- Even though there's 13 stars.
We'll talk about that.

00:25:25.804 --> 00:25:29.744
- We'll talk about that demented notion
of things in just a minute, but only 11

00:25:29.784 --> 00:25:33.714
leave. So there are four slaveholding
states in the United States: Kentucky, and

00:25:33.744 --> 00:25:37.583
Missouri, and Maryland, and Delaware
remain in the United States. So

00:25:37.624 --> 00:25:41.484
it's not just North versus South.
Those states are all part of the

00:25:41.524 --> 00:25:44.144
South. It's the United States
versus the Confederacy.

00:25:44.224 --> 00:25:47.324
- But see how you referred to it
as the United States. So the

00:25:47.424 --> 00:25:50.284
Union is also referred
to as the United States

00:25:50.464 --> 00:25:52.144
- That's correct. Yes, yes

00:25:52.224 --> 00:25:56.024
- But the Confederacy was, as
you write about, a set of

00:25:56.064 --> 00:25:59.244
people who also had a sense
of nationalism, pride

00:25:59.744 --> 00:26:03.734
- Well, and took their founding document.
I mean, it took the whole summer of

00:26:03.744 --> 00:26:07.714
1787 in Philadelphia, that
hot, steamy, humid summer of

00:26:07.724 --> 00:26:11.404
1787 to come up with the
Constitution. The Confederates did it

00:26:11.444 --> 00:26:15.324
quickly 'cause they basically had the
US Constitution here, a blank piece of

00:26:15.364 --> 00:26:16.234
paper over here

00:26:16.564 --> 00:26:20.504
... copied most of the Constitution over
here, just changed the parts that they needed

00:26:20.524 --> 00:26:24.284
to make sure slavery was protected,
and you didn't have the wrong

00:26:24.364 --> 00:26:27.824
kind of tariffs and so forth. That's one
reason they could do it so quickly. It's

00:26:27.924 --> 00:26:29.664
absolutely based on

00:26:30.684 --> 00:26:34.614
the government they've been thriving under,
which is under the United States Constitution.

00:26:34.764 --> 00:26:38.694
Both sides claimed they were the true
inheritors of the revolutionary generation.

00:26:38.744 --> 00:26:42.504
That was one of their... They
all... Jefferson Davis loved the

00:26:42.564 --> 00:26:46.144
Declaration of Independence. The
Declaration is Abraham Lincoln's favorite

00:26:46.244 --> 00:26:48.234
document. They just liked
different parts of it

00:26:48.744 --> 00:26:52.064
- So we should think of
this as two republics

00:26:52.524 --> 00:26:55.304
- I argue that. That makes
many people very angry

00:26:55.444 --> 00:26:57.544
- I will make many people angry today

00:26:57.804 --> 00:27:01.744
- They say that I am conferring
legitimacy on the Confederacy that they

00:27:01.804 --> 00:27:02.404
don't deserve,

00:27:03.284 --> 00:27:06.893
and my response to that is they have
a national government. They have a

00:27:06.924 --> 00:27:10.574
Senate. They have a Congress. They have
an army. They have a navy. They have

00:27:10.584 --> 00:27:14.344
departments. They... It's...
It walks like a duck. It

00:27:14.404 --> 00:27:18.374
quacks like a duck. In the end, it's a
slaughtered duck faster than it would've been

00:27:18.444 --> 00:27:20.964
because the United States
military forces crushed it

00:27:21.644 --> 00:27:29.204
- We should say that you are respected,
admired, loved by most people on both sides

00:27:29.844 --> 00:27:33.604
in, from a perspective sense. You're

00:27:33.644 --> 00:27:37.384
deeply respected by historians of
Civil War and just historians of

00:27:37.424 --> 00:27:40.904
American history. But of
course, when you do your job

00:27:40.964 --> 00:27:45.034
well on a controversial topic in a
controversial period of human history-

00:27:45.864 --> 00:27:51.804
... you're going to get, hopefully
an equal amount of critics on both

00:27:51.864 --> 00:27:56.364
sides. And that's when you do your
job well. So sometimes when we

00:27:56.464 --> 00:28:00.324
speak today, some of the
controversial, quote-unquote,

00:28:00.364 --> 00:28:04.124
controversial things that you express
are controversial from that small

00:28:04.204 --> 00:28:06.904
minority of folks on both
sides for different reasons

00:28:07.124 --> 00:28:10.984
- Yes. I won't be... One of my favorite
pieces of hate mail I got was an

00:28:11.084 --> 00:28:14.544
email where this guy, I had... He
was upset that I referred to the

00:28:14.584 --> 00:28:18.244
Confederacy as a slave-holding republic
that was... And he said that he

00:28:18.344 --> 00:28:24.944
hoped I would develop virulent pancreatic
cancer and die a horrible death. Not

00:28:25.004 --> 00:28:28.893
just regular pancreatic cancer. He
wanted virulent pancreatic cancer

00:28:28.904 --> 00:28:32.844
And then he signed his name and said he'd
be happy to talk to me anytime I wanted

00:28:32.864 --> 00:28:33.004
to

00:28:33.224 --> 00:28:36.664
- Yeah. I'm sure that would
be a wonderful conversation

00:28:36.684 --> 00:28:40.514
But there's also really
strong senses throughout,

00:28:40.704 --> 00:28:45.044
throughout the United States,
the United States, of the,

00:28:46.044 --> 00:28:51.824
this diversity of opinion of
what is the United States

00:28:51.864 --> 00:28:52.764
- Of course. Yes

00:28:52.773 --> 00:28:55.564
- And that's where the
passion is really rooted

00:28:56.024 --> 00:28:59.694
- Both sides claim... In
the election of 1860,

00:29:00.904 --> 00:29:04.804
Douglas talked about how much he loved the
Union. Lincoln talked about how much he

00:29:04.844 --> 00:29:08.784
loved the Union. Breckinridge talked
about how much he loved the Union.

00:29:08.824 --> 00:29:12.754
They're all... And John Bell, who's the
Unionist, the Constitutional Union-

00:29:12.804 --> 00:29:17.654
That's all he talked about. They just had
different versions of what the Union was

00:29:18.264 --> 00:29:21.854
- And that's why I love this country. I think
it's the greatest country on Earth, and it

00:29:21.924 --> 00:29:25.484
has a bunch of people that very
strongly disagree with each other.

00:29:26.204 --> 00:29:29.744
Of course, hopefully never again

00:29:29.804 --> 00:29:33.544
escalating to the degree that it
did during this period of time.

00:29:33.924 --> 00:29:37.464
So we talked about the blue people on
the map and the red people on the map.

00:29:38.704 --> 00:29:43.533
It may be a silly thing to ask,
but sticking with the motivation,

00:29:45.064 --> 00:29:53.144
why would, let's say, a farm boy in
Ohio- ... want to die for the Union?

00:29:53.264 --> 00:29:57.164
- That is the most important, and I didn't
even ask you to ask that question. That's

00:29:57.204 --> 00:29:58.444
the, that's the hardest thing-

00:29:58.524 --> 00:30:00.444
- Let's be honest. You paid me a lot
of money to ask that question, yeah.

00:30:00.624 --> 00:30:01.684
- I didn't think it was so much.

00:30:03.064 --> 00:30:04.514
- Okay. I'm sorry. Go
ahead. I won't distract.

00:30:04.514 --> 00:30:07.904
- No. The, I tell my students at the beginning
of my Civil War class that if they couldn't

00:30:07.944 --> 00:30:13.424
explain, I didn't use Ohio. I said, "If you
can't explain why a farmer from Vermont-

00:30:13.434 --> 00:30:16.984
... would put on a blue uniform and
pick up a musket to make South Carolina

00:30:17.704 --> 00:30:21.644
come back in, why didn't he just say, 'Great.
South Carolina's gone. I never liked them

00:30:21.684 --> 00:30:24.344
anyway. This is a better union.'
If you can't explain that,

00:30:25.464 --> 00:30:29.444
then you can't understand the Civil War." And
the key to that, I'll come back to it, is

00:30:29.564 --> 00:30:32.804
union because he would've said,
"If we allow South Carolina to go

00:30:33.664 --> 00:30:37.644
That means that other places
could go and you could dismantle.

00:30:37.664 --> 00:30:41.304
And the only reason they're going is because
they didn't like who was elected president.

00:30:41.684 --> 00:30:45.184
They didn't say that Lincoln was corrupt. They
didn't say Republicans bought the election.

00:30:45.224 --> 00:30:49.024
They just didn't like who won the
election. And so if the republic is

00:30:49.084 --> 00:30:52.864
so fragile that it can't have
an election without having the

00:30:52.904 --> 00:30:56.824
people who didn't like who won
leave, then the founders had wasted

00:30:56.834 --> 00:31:00.664
their time, the revolution was for
nothing, all that bloodshed meant

00:31:00.744 --> 00:31:08.024
nothing. You had to step up and save this
precious republic that had put in place

00:31:08.684 --> 00:31:12.564
by the revolutionary generation
and gave its citizens the benefits

00:31:12.604 --> 00:31:16.584
that I've already talked about. That's why
they would put on a uniform and risk their

00:31:16.624 --> 00:31:20.904
life, because they believed a
citizen... These are citizen soldiers.

00:31:22.584 --> 00:31:24.504
We don't know what that means so much now.

00:31:25.304 --> 00:31:29.064
because we have a professional
military establishment now. Not--

00:31:29.604 --> 00:31:34.724
I mean, even when I was younger, there was
still a draft, and but that's gone now.

00:31:35.664 --> 00:31:39.544
And so we don't understand what the
concept of citizen soldier was, that you

00:31:39.584 --> 00:31:43.564
owe something back to the republic
that has conveyed these things

00:31:43.624 --> 00:31:47.744
to you, and that's why the guy from
Vermont or New Hampshire or Ohio

00:31:48.944 --> 00:31:51.804
would put on a uniform and
go into military service.

00:31:52.144 --> 00:31:55.904
- Is it possible for us to understand
that level of love of nation

00:31:56.684 --> 00:32:02.484
and believe in nation? Is this, is this
very distant from what America is today?

00:32:02.744 --> 00:32:06.664
- I actually think many people probably still
feel that way, but they're not the ones

00:32:06.704 --> 00:32:07.814
who are on television-

00:32:07.974 --> 00:32:11.764
... most of the time. Republicans
in the midst of the war commented

00:32:11.864 --> 00:32:15.654
about the fact that even with
this gigantic conflict going

00:32:15.784 --> 00:32:19.684
on, immigration continued
at a high level. People

00:32:19.764 --> 00:32:23.504
coming into a nation that
was in the middle of a great

00:32:23.644 --> 00:32:27.144
war. And they said, "This is
a testament to the promise

00:32:27.604 --> 00:32:31.044
of the United States."
And I think that promise

00:32:31.164 --> 00:32:35.024
continues. I mean, we've argued about...
As with so many things, Americans have

00:32:35.084 --> 00:32:38.104
no sense of history. They believe we've
never argued about immigration before.

00:32:38.164 --> 00:32:42.104
We've always argued about immigration, with
some people always saying, "Shut the door.

00:32:42.544 --> 00:32:46.484
They're ruining us. The Italians are ruining
us. The Irish are Catholics. They're ruining

00:32:46.544 --> 00:32:51.704
us. God, the Southern Europeans are
even worse than, than the Irish were."

00:32:52.524 --> 00:32:56.464
It's-- The people change, the
arguments stay very much the

00:32:56.504 --> 00:33:00.494
same, and I think it's really telling
that in the midst of the war, people

00:33:00.524 --> 00:33:04.344
would say, "Look at our republic.
It is-- It offers so much to people

00:33:04.384 --> 00:33:07.614
that even in the midst of this
great war, they're still coming-

00:33:07.664 --> 00:33:08.593
... to the United States."

00:33:09.144 --> 00:33:12.884
- And like you said, both
sides, both these republics

00:33:13.424 --> 00:33:16.064
claimed, for example, George Washington.

00:33:16.144 --> 00:33:19.164
- Yes. They both claim... I'll leave it at
that. They both claim George Washington.

00:33:19.244 --> 00:33:20.313
They wanted to be right

00:33:21.504 --> 00:33:25.384
with the man, which is what George
Washington was. He's the indispensable

00:33:25.484 --> 00:33:29.344
man, no question. Both sides
wanted him on their side. They

00:33:29.384 --> 00:33:33.284
both quoted Thomas Jefferson,
although Confederates were a

00:33:33.344 --> 00:33:36.904
little more leery of Jefferson
'cause he's too complicated for them

00:33:36.984 --> 00:33:40.924
sometimes. But no, they wanted to be
right with the founders and claimed to

00:33:40.964 --> 00:33:44.843
be right with the founders. The Confederates
would say, "The Constitution allowed

00:33:44.884 --> 00:33:48.664
slavery. What are you talking about we're out
of step with the Constitution? We're in step

00:33:48.804 --> 00:33:51.744
with the Con- You're the ones who are
out of step with the Constitution."

00:33:51.784 --> 00:33:54.554
- What about the topic of states'
rights? At the very least-

00:33:54.574 --> 00:33:58.504
- I love state rights. State rights...
I mean, I love the way people use

00:33:58.544 --> 00:34:02.654
it with the Civil War. They associate
it solely with Southern states-

00:34:02.684 --> 00:34:06.624
... of course. State rights is always
available in United States history for

00:34:06.664 --> 00:34:09.434
somebody who's not happy with what
the federal government is doing.

00:34:10.004 --> 00:34:14.093
During the War of 1812, it was New
England that most often talked-

00:34:14.093 --> 00:34:17.804
... about resorting to state rights. They had
a convention where they sort of wandered over

00:34:17.844 --> 00:34:20.524
towards secession territory
in the Hartford Convention.

00:34:21.404 --> 00:34:25.304
We think of it as state rights
because as you lead up to the Civil

00:34:25.404 --> 00:34:29.184
War, you hear con- white Southerners
talking about it all the

00:34:29.244 --> 00:34:32.944
time. "We need our state
rights." I always love to ask

00:34:33.524 --> 00:34:37.444
people who say that, "What are all the state
rights? Give me-- List the state rights that

00:34:37.464 --> 00:34:38.884
the white South is concerned."

00:34:38.964 --> 00:34:42.444
You know, "Give me the top three. No, really,
just give me the top two." There's only one.

00:34:43.064 --> 00:34:46.824
The state right that they're worried
about is the right of their state to

00:34:46.884 --> 00:34:50.744
interpose its power between a federal
government that might try to interfere with

00:34:50.784 --> 00:34:54.774
slavery and a state government that wants
to maintain it. That's the state right that

00:34:54.904 --> 00:35:00.304
matters then. Northern states also
resorted to state rights during this, the

00:35:00.444 --> 00:35:04.324
ramp-up to the Civil War, with what
were called personal liberty laws. They

00:35:04.364 --> 00:35:08.174
tried to interpose their state power in
between a federal government that would

00:35:08.224 --> 00:35:13.374
use its power to to force escaped,
enslaved people back into slavery.

00:35:14.774 --> 00:35:18.734
It's whoever's unhappy with the federal
government, the concept of state rights is

00:35:18.764 --> 00:35:21.054
available, and both sides used it.

00:35:21.524 --> 00:35:23.884
- And of course, the beautiful, dark,

00:35:25.104 --> 00:35:31.304
ironic hypocrisy was illustrated by the
Confederacy when they were the first to

00:35:31.364 --> 00:35:34.094
pass a national draft in American history.

00:35:34.684 --> 00:35:41.564
- Like, irony is wonderful- ... in history.
Another thing that I love to teach

00:35:42.544 --> 00:35:46.324
is that the most intrusive
central government in American

00:35:46.404 --> 00:35:50.384
history, by far, until deep
into the 20th century, was the

00:35:50.404 --> 00:35:54.004
Confederate central government, because
it had to do things to keep the war

00:35:54.044 --> 00:35:57.944
going that it couldn't have done unless
the central government stepped in,

00:35:57.953 --> 00:36:01.944
such as passing the first national
conscription law in American history. The

00:36:02.024 --> 00:36:05.704
government, the US government never forced
people into uniform before the Civil War.

00:36:05.844 --> 00:36:09.784
The Confederates did it first. They did
it in the spring of 1862. They passed a

00:36:09.844 --> 00:36:13.764
national graduated income tax.
And when taxing didn't work,

00:36:13.824 --> 00:36:17.664
when their money became so devalued, they
then passed what they called the tax in

00:36:17.744 --> 00:36:21.724
kind. They would take a percentage of
your crop. They allowed the government to

00:36:21.784 --> 00:36:25.584
impress horses and mules
and enslaved labor to work

00:36:25.684 --> 00:36:29.204
on All of these were intrusions
from the central government that

00:36:29.344 --> 00:36:32.964
drove true state rights people
crazy in the Confederacy.

00:36:33.644 --> 00:36:37.534
Confederates didn't have political parties
because they said the founders didn't want them,

00:36:37.624 --> 00:36:41.374
so we won't have them. But what
they did have was a sort of

00:36:41.404 --> 00:36:45.244
nationalist, "We're gonna let the central government
do whatever it needs to do." That's Jefferson

00:36:45.264 --> 00:36:49.184
Davis' wing, and Robert E. Lee's over
there, too. And the people like the

00:36:49.244 --> 00:36:52.564
vice president, Alexander Stephens,
who said, "You can't do all this stuff

00:36:52.804 --> 00:36:54.664
because the states
should have more power."

00:36:55.624 --> 00:36:59.524
The only reason the war lasted as long as it did
is because the central government did things

00:36:59.624 --> 00:37:03.544
no one ever would have believed they
would do. He also got rid of habeas

00:37:03.624 --> 00:37:06.804
corpus just like Abraham Lincoln
did in selected situations.

00:37:07.234 --> 00:37:11.674
- And of course, we should really emphasize
that on both sides, those volunteers,

00:37:12.644 --> 00:37:16.344
the on the Confederacy, there's
a very large number of people

00:37:16.444 --> 00:37:20.304
percentage-wise that volunteered
to fight. And then I

00:37:20.344 --> 00:37:24.254
believe the volunteer
period was one year, and

00:37:24.284 --> 00:37:28.124
then there's all kind of funky stuff that
the that the government started doing to

00:37:28.204 --> 00:37:30.394
trap those people into then serving and-

00:37:30.464 --> 00:37:32.184
- Here's what the Confederacy did.

00:37:34.304 --> 00:37:36.804
They... Nobody thinks the
war's gonna last a long time-

00:37:36.814 --> 00:37:39.804
... in the spring of 1861.
So if you enlist for a year-

00:37:39.814 --> 00:37:43.704
... that's probably plenty of time to
finish this war. Well, a year goes by and a

00:37:43.724 --> 00:37:47.644
significant percentage of the Confederate
men under arms are due to get out. So what

00:37:47.704 --> 00:37:50.284
the Confederate Congress
did was pass conscription.

00:37:50.644 --> 00:37:53.604
We're gonna put every- make everybody
liable to three-year service.

00:37:54.504 --> 00:37:58.324
And in the midst of doing that,
all you guys who volunteered for

00:37:58.364 --> 00:38:00.604
one year are now in for two more years.

00:38:01.224 --> 00:38:04.554
Well, that outraged... I mean, "That's,
you're breaking faith with us," they said.

00:38:04.804 --> 00:38:05.794
They didn't stop there.

00:38:07.124 --> 00:38:10.934
Later they revised it when the war kept
going on and then the three years guys were

00:38:10.944 --> 00:38:14.844
gonna get out, then the Congress stepped in and
said, "You're in for the duration." The bottom

00:38:14.884 --> 00:38:18.664
line is, the Confederacy ever
gets you in uniform, you don't

00:38:18.784 --> 00:38:22.744
get out because they keep changing
the rules on you. And that

00:38:22.824 --> 00:38:26.684
was considered, national conscription
was considered a fundamental

00:38:26.804 --> 00:38:30.724
abridgment of individual rights by many people.
That's something the government should never

00:38:30.764 --> 00:38:34.564
force a citizen into uniform. A citizen
should volunteer their service.

00:38:35.244 --> 00:38:38.984
The US government waited a year, then
it did it in the spring of 1863,

00:38:39.104 --> 00:38:42.774
although it always respected
original terms of enlistment.

00:38:44.094 --> 00:38:47.264
Never made it the war for the
duration the way the Confederacy did.

00:38:48.424 --> 00:38:50.384
- Quick pause for a bathroom
break if it's okay.

00:38:51.624 --> 00:38:55.304
Quick 10-second shout-out to our sponsors.
Check them out in the description.

00:38:55.604 --> 00:38:58.464
It really is the best way to
support this podcast. Go to

00:38:58.504 --> 00:39:02.484
lexfridman.com/sponsors.
And now, dear friends,

00:39:03.124 --> 00:39:05.704
back to my conversation
with Gary Gallagher.

00:39:07.084 --> 00:39:10.064
All right, we're back. There's so many
different directions we can go, but

00:39:11.164 --> 00:39:14.844
I am somebody who believes that individuals
can steer the direction of history.

00:39:14.984 --> 00:39:18.554
So one angle to look at here is,

00:39:18.664 --> 00:39:22.664
some of the legendary historical
figures from this time.

00:39:24.564 --> 00:39:28.464
Let's talk about some of the key figures
of the Civil War. We have Abraham Lincoln,

00:39:28.524 --> 00:39:32.504
of course, the President of the United States
who held the Union together and turned the war

00:39:32.574 --> 00:39:36.204
into a fight for a new birth of
freedom. Jefferson Davis, who you

00:39:36.264 --> 00:39:40.024
mentioned, President of the
Confederacy. Ulysses S.

00:39:40.084 --> 00:39:43.804
Grant, somebody we'll talk
about quite a bit, General in

00:39:43.884 --> 00:39:47.854
Chief of the Union
armies. Robert E. Lee on

00:39:47.864 --> 00:39:51.844
the other side, the commander of
the Army of Northern Virginia,

00:39:52.004 --> 00:39:55.804
somebody who you have studied for
decades now. We'll talk a lot

00:39:55.864 --> 00:40:01.624
about him. There's Sherman, there's Stonewall
Jackson, there's Frederick Douglass,

00:40:02.524 --> 00:40:07.984
there is McClellan, there is John
Brown, a militant abolitionist.

00:40:08.864 --> 00:40:11.124
Many more figures, but let's look at three

00:40:12.604 --> 00:40:18.864
at first. Let's look at Lincoln,
Grant, and Lee. Lincoln first. So

00:40:20.504 --> 00:40:24.204
Abraham Lincoln, we have
this backwoods lawyer with a

00:40:24.284 --> 00:40:27.824
single year of schooling
who somehow outgrew every

00:40:27.904 --> 00:40:31.864
polished man around him and became one
of the most consequential leaders in

00:40:31.924 --> 00:40:35.844
human history. So what do we
need to understand? What can we

00:40:35.884 --> 00:40:38.514
possibly understand at
a 10,000-foot level,

00:40:39.524 --> 00:40:43.414
About Lincoln that made it
all possible? What was it

00:40:43.504 --> 00:40:47.064
about Lincoln's mind that
made this man do what he did?

00:40:48.053 --> 00:40:51.974
- Almost nobody continues to go up in
my estimation as I get older and

00:40:52.044 --> 00:40:55.544
older, but Lincoln is one of the people
who does. And Lincoln is quite frankly

00:40:57.424 --> 00:41:01.364
a mystery to me. I don't see how you
get Abraham Lincoln out of someone who

00:41:01.424 --> 00:41:05.344
came from his very poor background. You

00:41:05.424 --> 00:41:09.324
said he had a year of schooling. You're being very
generous to him there. He didn't even really have a

00:41:09.384 --> 00:41:12.824
year of formal schooling.
And you end up with a person

00:41:14.484 --> 00:41:18.164
who is the most eloquent president
we've ever had by a very wide margin

00:41:18.444 --> 00:41:20.984
and I think even more
eloquent than Jefferson,

00:41:21.804 --> 00:41:25.764
and who is able to distill
into the fewest possible

00:41:25.824 --> 00:41:32.484
words the most complex ideas and
topics imaginable and just have them

00:41:33.664 --> 00:41:37.194
ring in a way that almost no one
is able to do. He is someone...

00:41:37.864 --> 00:41:41.804
He had an ego, but I think one of
his real gifts was the ability to

00:41:41.844 --> 00:41:48.724
put his ego aside and keep his eye on
the goal. He was able to really focus,

00:41:48.904 --> 00:41:51.204
and he focused relentlessly
on, number one,

00:41:53.064 --> 00:41:55.664
suppressing the rebellion
and restoring the Union,

00:41:56.604 --> 00:42:00.104
but also getting... He hated the
institution of slavery. He's not an

00:42:00.164 --> 00:42:04.124
abolitionist, but he hated the institution
of slavery and he was very happy to

00:42:04.164 --> 00:42:07.824
see that slavery was added to
the roster of what the United

00:42:07.884 --> 00:42:11.604
States would insist on
at the end of the war.

00:42:12.524 --> 00:42:16.364
He's able to let all the other
things go. He can pay attention to

00:42:16.424 --> 00:42:19.984
many things, but he never loses his...
his principal focus was, "How do I

00:42:20.044 --> 00:42:24.014
maintain this great war?"
And it's an all en- it's

00:42:24.124 --> 00:42:27.144
really hard to get people
to understand the scope

00:42:28.152 --> 00:42:31.492
of the Civil War. There are 31
million people in the United States

00:42:31.552 --> 00:42:35.442
then. About a shade more than 3
million put on uniforms. It would

00:42:35.472 --> 00:42:39.192
be as if we had 35 to 40 million

00:42:39.272 --> 00:42:42.632
people under arms now. For the Confederates,
it would be like having more like

00:42:42.932 --> 00:42:46.991
55 million people- ... under arms
as a percentage of the population.

00:42:47.932 --> 00:42:51.762
The dead would be between 6
million and 7.5 million, depending

00:42:51.812 --> 00:42:55.772
on how you count it. The scope
is just... During the Gulf Wars,

00:42:55.952 --> 00:42:59.692
The New York Times put a picture of every
United States service person who was

00:42:59.752 --> 00:43:01.252
killed, put a picture in the paper

00:43:02.632 --> 00:43:06.592
over the scope of those wars,
and that is about as many people

00:43:06.792 --> 00:43:10.632
as were shot in a few hours at
Antietam. It's just very different to

00:43:10.672 --> 00:43:14.302
get students now to understand the
different scale, and that's what

00:43:14.332 --> 00:43:18.292
Lincoln's dealing with. How
do I keep the focus on a war

00:43:19.052 --> 00:43:22.732
that is becoming far more
costly than anyone imagined,

00:43:25.332 --> 00:43:29.312
and eating up our young manpower at a
rate that no one... There's never been

00:43:29.321 --> 00:43:32.972
anything in our history to prepare
the United States for this. The

00:43:32.992 --> 00:43:36.962
Winfield Scott captured Mexico City
with an army of fewer than 15,000

00:43:36.972 --> 00:43:38.872
during the war with Mexico.

00:43:40.642 --> 00:43:44.502
U.S. Grant has 120,000. I mean, the whole
scale is just so different that it's

00:43:44.572 --> 00:43:45.782
hard to come to terms with it.

00:43:45.812 --> 00:43:48.392
And that's what Lincoln was
dealing with. I think the fact

00:43:49.532 --> 00:43:52.232
that he was willing to surround
himself with really able

00:43:53.012 --> 00:43:56.792
counselors is important. Who does
he put in his cabinet? He puts his

00:43:56.852 --> 00:44:00.752
main rivals in the cabinet. He
put William Henry Seward in his

00:44:00.812 --> 00:44:04.752
cabinet, who's the most famous Republican
in the country. He puts Salmon P. Chase,

00:44:04.812 --> 00:44:10.372
who's a very smart and effective and
ambitious person from Ohio, in his

00:44:10.432 --> 00:44:17.592
cabinet. He surrounds himself with
people who are capable and energetic

00:44:17.692 --> 00:44:21.372
and have their own ambitions, he's not afraid
to do that, all of whom had far better

00:44:21.412 --> 00:44:25.382
educations than he had and didn't have
a rural backwoods accent like he did.

00:44:25.432 --> 00:44:29.392
It's also a measure of how confident he was. He
didn't pretend to talk a way he, uh, to talk

00:44:29.432 --> 00:44:31.622
in a fashion that he- ...
didn't actually talk.

00:44:31.672 --> 00:44:35.572
He would've pronounced words in ways that
anybody who went to Harvard would just

00:44:35.632 --> 00:44:38.852
sneer at. He didn't, and he knew
they sneered, and he didn't care.

00:44:39.652 --> 00:44:43.332
Because eventually, all of them,
except the ones with only a 20-watt

00:44:43.412 --> 00:44:47.072
bulb, figured out that
Lincoln was actually probably

00:44:47.152 --> 00:44:51.032
smarter than they were and had a better
grasp of things than they did and

00:44:51.072 --> 00:44:54.772
brought them around. He really brought
Seward around, which is very impressive.

00:44:56.132 --> 00:44:59.872
- Yeah, there's so many fascinating
things about Lincoln. So backwoods of

00:44:59.952 --> 00:45:04.372
Kentucky, and Kentucky is a very
important state in this story. And

00:45:06.192 --> 00:45:08.192
the right words, the right speeches,

00:45:09.112 --> 00:45:14.402
the right debates at this moment in
history. It really does make me wonder,

00:45:15.972 --> 00:45:23.252
you know, if Lincoln didn't exist, if
certain humans in history didn't exist,

00:45:24.092 --> 00:45:26.672
that everything could have
turned out very differently.

00:45:27.332 --> 00:45:31.092
- The great forces are all that matters and
individuals don't really matter notion of

00:45:31.132 --> 00:45:34.712
history I think is ridiculous.
Individuals really matter.

00:45:35.352 --> 00:45:40.042
For the United States, Lincoln and
Grant matter beyond calculation--

00:45:40.092 --> 00:45:44.012
... in this, in this war. And on the Confederate
side, if you take Lee out of the picture, the

00:45:44.052 --> 00:45:47.952
war doesn't last nearly as long as
it does. In fact, we'll talk about

00:45:47.992 --> 00:45:51.952
this later probably, I think it could
have ended early in the summer of 1862

00:45:52.012 --> 00:45:55.652
with slavery still intact.
I mean, in a perverse way,

00:45:56.372 --> 00:45:59.881
Lee furthers the cause of
emancipation because he--

00:46:00.292 --> 00:46:04.222
extends the war long enough that the
United States says, "We have to do this.

00:46:04.312 --> 00:46:07.161
We've gotta get rid of slavery
to defeat the rebels."

00:46:08.212 --> 00:46:12.132
- So, and also on the psychological
side, Lincoln is a,

00:46:12.332 --> 00:46:16.592
this is a fascinating study because he,
he carried deep depression all his life.

00:46:16.712 --> 00:46:20.072
- He had black, black moods
in his life, yes, he did.

00:46:20.352 --> 00:46:24.572
- And so for somebody that has to go
through an all-encompassing war,

00:46:26.232 --> 00:46:31.032
to be able to sort of survive
through that, to think through that,

00:46:31.852 --> 00:46:37.632
without an education,
without any acquired skill

00:46:37.732 --> 00:46:42.012
that enables him to be a
commander-in-chief during a time of war.

00:46:43.092 --> 00:46:46.592
He's got nothing in the
toolbox, and yet he steps up.

00:46:46.672 --> 00:46:51.082
- If you looked at his resume
and Jefferson Davis's--

00:46:51.082 --> 00:46:54.372
... in 1860 and said, "Okay, here are the
two guys who are gonna be your respective

00:46:54.472 --> 00:46:57.352
commanders-in-chief. Who
do you want on your team?"

00:46:57.432 --> 00:47:01.212
Lincoln had no administrative
experience beyond running a little

00:47:01.252 --> 00:47:05.132
law office. He had one
two-year term in the House of

00:47:05.192 --> 00:47:09.072
Representatives in terms of
national government. He had no

00:47:09.112 --> 00:47:12.872
military service to speak of, a
little while in the Black Hawk

00:47:12.952 --> 00:47:15.932
War as a junior officer
in the Black Hawk War.

00:47:17.112 --> 00:47:20.982
Didn't check any of the education boxes, as
you said. Jefferson Davis was a graduate of

00:47:21.012 --> 00:47:24.892
West Point. He'd commanded more
men in the war with Mexico than

00:47:24.932 --> 00:47:28.732
most of the men who commanded armies
during the Civil War. He'd chaired the

00:47:28.752 --> 00:47:32.692
Senate Committee on Military
Affairs. He'd been Secretary of

00:47:32.772 --> 00:47:36.372
War, a lot of administrative experience. He
checked every box. Lincoln checked none of the

00:47:36.432 --> 00:47:40.032
boxes, and yet you got Lincoln as a

00:47:40.072 --> 00:47:43.852
commander-in-chief. It's really quite
remarkable. He could listen to people

00:47:43.952 --> 00:47:47.892
that he thought knew more about
something than he did. That's a

00:47:47.932 --> 00:47:51.722
quality that all of our presidents,
to put it mildly, have not

00:47:51.752 --> 00:47:55.642
had. Lincoln did have that. He could
listen. He could admit when he was wrong

00:47:56.532 --> 00:48:00.282
and change his mind about
things, and he could put up

00:48:00.352 --> 00:48:04.272
with obnoxious behavior on
the part of people that

00:48:04.312 --> 00:48:08.232
he thought might deliver the goods.
And a perfect example of this

00:48:08.292 --> 00:48:11.892
is George Brinton McClellan, whom we'll
talk about later, his young... who was

00:48:12.032 --> 00:48:14.472
general-in-chief for a while,
commanded the Army of the Potomac.

00:48:15.472 --> 00:48:19.332
In 1861, Lincoln and his
Secretary of State, William Henry

00:48:19.432 --> 00:48:23.272
Seward, went to McClellan's house to
talk to McClellan. Went to McClellan's

00:48:23.332 --> 00:48:26.612
house. The commander-in-chief goes to
McClellan's house. McClellan wasn't there.

00:48:27.212 --> 00:48:30.984
The butler or somebody put them in a
side room. McClellan came home, was

00:48:31.024 --> 00:48:34.454
told Lincoln was there.
He went upstairs and then

00:48:34.484 --> 00:48:38.384
told the person who worked for him to go tell
the president that he'd be happy to talk to

00:48:38.404 --> 00:48:42.284
him some other time. And so
Lincoln just left. He never went

00:48:42.324 --> 00:48:46.184
back to see McClellan again,
didn't pitch a fit, filed

00:48:46.244 --> 00:48:49.984
that away. Okay, McClellan's an ass, but
I think he might win the war. I mean, he

00:48:50.044 --> 00:48:53.984
had that ability to do that,
to put aside that kind of

00:48:55.224 --> 00:48:58.524
behavior on the part of someone
if he thought they would deliver.

00:48:59.784 --> 00:49:03.604
He just has a number of qualities,
I think, that are very unusual to

00:49:03.644 --> 00:49:07.514
find in someone. And as you say, he
did have depressive moments, and

00:49:07.564 --> 00:49:11.504
he-- we talk about how, what
bad days presidents have. Now,

00:49:11.644 --> 00:49:15.334
Lincoln would have said, I'll take
that president's worst day every

00:49:15.444 --> 00:49:19.184
day rather than the days that I get,
for the most part. I mean, he--

00:49:19.224 --> 00:49:21.344
it's hard to imagine the
burdens that he carried.

00:49:22.184 --> 00:49:26.064
- And at the right time was
just a handful of words

00:49:27.124 --> 00:49:30.744
expressed something that no
military could possibly do.

00:49:30.804 --> 00:49:32.134
- Explain what the war was about,

00:49:33.404 --> 00:49:37.074
why it was worth fighting, what it
meant, his second inaugural address.

00:49:37.474 --> 00:49:41.104
I mean, people went-- when people went
to hear the president give his second

00:49:41.164 --> 00:49:44.644
inaugural address on that cold day in

00:49:44.664 --> 00:49:48.204
1865, what they wanted to hear,
they finally beat the rebel

00:49:48.244 --> 00:49:52.124
scum from their point of view. Let's have some
retribution here. What are we going to do to them?

00:49:52.204 --> 00:49:54.984
How are we going to make these
people who brought this great war

00:49:56.024 --> 00:49:59.384
pay for this? And what does
Lincoln tell them? He tells them

00:49:59.684 --> 00:50:03.454
slavery caused it. Everybody knows that
somehow that's the truth, and we're

00:50:03.564 --> 00:50:07.494
all implicated in it. I mean, he
didn't tell them anything that

00:50:07.524 --> 00:50:11.504
they wanted to hear that
day. And it was-- and it

00:50:11.544 --> 00:50:15.164
took great courage, I think, to do
that at that moment, to tell an

00:50:15.224 --> 00:50:18.784
audience what they didn't want to hear.
And he did it in the most eloquent

00:50:18.844 --> 00:50:22.724
language that any president's-- that's the
greatest speech in American history, the second

00:50:22.784 --> 00:50:24.104
inaugural, in my view.

00:50:25.204 --> 00:50:28.884
- With malice toward none, with
charity for all, with firmness

00:50:29.464 --> 00:50:33.164
in the right as God gives
us to see the right, let us

00:50:33.264 --> 00:50:37.024
strive on to finish the
work we are in, to bind

00:50:37.104 --> 00:50:40.513
up the nation's wounds,
to care for him who shall

00:50:40.624 --> 00:50:48.324
have borne the battle and for his widow and
his orphan, to do all which may achieve

00:50:48.384 --> 00:50:53.964
and cherish a just and lasting peace
among ourselves and with all nations.

00:50:54.484 --> 00:50:57.984
- And we're all complicit
in this because we all

00:50:58.064 --> 00:51:01.944
countenanced slavery. I mean, that's all
in that one speech. And it's a short

00:51:02.084 --> 00:51:07.304
speech. The whole speech probably only
lasted eight or nine minutes. And he

00:51:07.404 --> 00:51:11.364
also pays tribute to the citizen soldiers at
the beginning of it because he says that the

00:51:11.404 --> 00:51:15.703
war is going well and we all know that
everything depends on what our soldiers do.

00:51:16.744 --> 00:51:20.564
And by that he meant saving
the union, killing slavery, it

00:51:20.664 --> 00:51:24.584
all depends on them. And then he comes back
and finishes with his tribute to the soldiers

00:51:24.624 --> 00:51:25.864
at the end.

00:51:25.944 --> 00:51:27.724
- And I have to say the Gettysburg Address.

00:51:27.844 --> 00:51:29.084
- Oh, no, it's you can't.

00:51:30.724 --> 00:51:39.784
- If I may, it's only maybe 200 words. Four
score and seven years ago, our fathers

00:51:39.944 --> 00:51:42.744
brought forth on this
continent a new nation,

00:51:43.584 --> 00:51:47.504
conceived in liberty and dedicated
to the proposition that all men

00:51:47.624 --> 00:51:51.344
are created equal. Now we are engaged in a

00:51:51.384 --> 00:51:55.284
great civil war, testing
whether that nation or

00:51:55.364 --> 00:52:02.144
any nation so conceived and so dedicated
can long endure. We are met on a great

00:52:02.184 --> 00:52:09.034
battlefield of that war. We have come
to dedicate a portion of that field as

00:52:09.064 --> 00:52:12.664
a final resting place for
those who here gave their

00:52:12.724 --> 00:52:15.024
lives that that nation might live.

00:52:15.964 --> 00:52:19.304
It is altogether fitting and
proper that we should do this.

00:52:20.544 --> 00:52:24.524
But in a larger sense, we cannot
dedicate, we cannot consecrate, we

00:52:24.564 --> 00:52:28.264
cannot hallow this ground. The brave men

00:52:28.504 --> 00:52:32.044
living and dead who struggled
here have consecrated it

00:52:32.144 --> 00:52:35.464
far above our poor power
to add or detract.

00:52:36.034 --> 00:52:39.824
The world will little note
nor long remember what

00:52:39.884 --> 00:52:43.673
we say here, but it can
never forget what they did

00:52:43.724 --> 00:52:47.674
here. It is for us the living,
rather, to be dedicated

00:52:47.724 --> 00:52:51.523
here to the unfinished work
which they who fought here have

00:52:51.544 --> 00:52:55.344
thus far so nobly advanced.
It is rather for us to be

00:52:55.444 --> 00:52:59.004
here dedicated to the great
task remaining before us, that

00:52:59.084 --> 00:53:02.444
from these honored dead we take increased

00:53:02.484 --> 00:53:06.404
devotion to that cause for
which they here gave the

00:53:06.464 --> 00:53:10.264
last full measure of
devotion, that we here highly

00:53:10.304 --> 00:53:14.164
resolve that these dead
shall not have died in vain,

00:53:14.584 --> 00:53:19.064
that this nation, under God, shall
have a new birth of freedom,

00:53:19.904 --> 00:53:23.504
and that government of the people,
by the people, for the people shall

00:53:23.564 --> 00:53:25.984
not perish from the earth.

00:53:27.264 --> 00:53:31.204
That's a big statement about what this
country is about. It's about freedom.

00:53:31.264 --> 00:53:37.364
- And he comes back to, when most people
read that, the very last part of it, they

00:53:37.504 --> 00:53:46.004
emphasize the prepositions of the
people, by the people, for the people.

00:53:47.444 --> 00:53:51.364
Lincoln, it's not about the
prepositions. It's about the people. He

00:53:51.524 --> 00:53:54.724
earlier in the war referred to
this as a people's contest.

00:53:55.424 --> 00:53:58.984
It's the people that are the key, the
people who make up the republic, the

00:53:59.024 --> 00:54:02.544
people who will derive the freedoms and

00:54:02.584 --> 00:54:05.904
liberties that the-- I mean,
it's an amazing-- and it

00:54:06.384 --> 00:54:10.184
probably took you two minutes to read
that. He was finished before most people

00:54:10.264 --> 00:54:14.084
knew he had started at Gettysburg. Almost
nobody heard him say that. The one

00:54:14.104 --> 00:54:17.924
thing wrong about it is when he said no one
would remember very long what he said there.

00:54:17.964 --> 00:54:19.944
It's been remembered pretty well.

00:54:19.984 --> 00:54:23.864
- And let's talk about the two other figures
which we'll return to time and time again.

00:54:24.304 --> 00:54:28.924
Ulysses S. Grant and Robert
E. Lee. So who was Grant?

00:54:29.804 --> 00:54:33.828
So this is a man who failed at
farming. Failed at business,

00:54:34.868 --> 00:54:42.228
failed at his role in the peacetime army.
Then when the war came, the war revealed a

00:54:42.348 --> 00:54:42.868
genius.

00:54:43.388 --> 00:54:47.328
- So he'd known a lot of failure,
he'd known a lot of setbacks, and

00:54:47.368 --> 00:54:51.208
his wartime record shows that he'd learned
to deal with those things. He had a great,

00:54:51.808 --> 00:54:55.508
tremendous relationship with his wife,
a very strong family life, a family he

00:54:55.568 --> 00:54:59.428
loved. I think that was the rock for him.
But during the war, what he exhibited

00:54:59.528 --> 00:55:03.308
was an absolute willingness
to break rules,

00:55:04.328 --> 00:55:08.118
to take risks, to take responsibility
for what he was doing,

00:55:08.188 --> 00:55:11.928
whether it was a success or a
failure, and not to be cast

00:55:11.948 --> 00:55:15.428
down by things that didn't work. If something
didn't work, he'd say, "Okay, that didn't work.

00:55:16.128 --> 00:55:18.988
Now let's do this." He very
famously, when he got to

00:55:19.028 --> 00:55:22.918
Virginia in 1864 and took
over the Army of the Potomac,

00:55:22.968 --> 00:55:26.028
he had all these subordinates who were
saying, "Well, what if General Lee does this?

00:55:26.108 --> 00:55:30.098
What if General Lee does that?" He finally
said, "Why don't we start worrying-- let's--

00:55:30.248 --> 00:55:33.968
not worrying about what General Lee is gonna
do and let General Lee worry about what

00:55:34.068 --> 00:55:37.958
we're going to do?" I mean, he
just had... He was unflappable

00:55:39.248 --> 00:55:43.148
under stress. He was the
perfect person to end up

00:55:43.368 --> 00:55:47.008
heading the Union armies, and he and
Lincoln-- And Lincoln realized that,

00:55:47.988 --> 00:55:51.868
and he proved indispensable. But he has

00:55:51.908 --> 00:55:55.868
this reputation as a butcher, Grant the Butcher.
That's something that former Confederates

00:55:55.908 --> 00:55:59.418
and some Democrats in the
North fastened on him. His

00:55:59.428 --> 00:56:03.018
preferred way of fighting wasn't just
kind of slug it out. It's really

00:56:03.068 --> 00:56:06.888
interesting to compare
his record in the war

00:56:06.948 --> 00:56:10.848
and Lee's record in the war before
they wound up opposing each other

00:56:10.888 --> 00:56:14.288
in Virginia in a really bloody
overland campaign. Grant's total

00:56:14.328 --> 00:56:18.138
casualties in all his great battles
before they met were 37,000.

00:56:18.348 --> 00:56:20.488
Lee's were over 98,000--

00:56:20.548 --> 00:56:24.448
... in the same period. The bloodiest
general in US history by far in terms of

00:56:24.488 --> 00:56:27.908
how many of his men get
shot is Robert E. Lee.

00:56:27.968 --> 00:56:31.868
The most careful demographic
study of any Civil War army is

00:56:31.988 --> 00:56:35.588
one done of Lee's army by a scholar
named Joe Glatthaar, and Joe figured out

00:56:36.208 --> 00:56:40.128
that if you were a soldier in
Robert E. Lee's army, you had a 73%

00:56:40.188 --> 00:56:46.368
chance of becoming a casualty. 73%.
Grant's casualties get high when he

00:56:46.408 --> 00:56:50.278
runs into Robert E. Lee, just like
everybody else. So the butcher tag is

00:56:50.288 --> 00:56:54.028
completely unfair about Grant.
He's also known as a drunk,

00:56:54.268 --> 00:56:57.948
and people have gone back and forth
on that, whether he just had a

00:56:57.988 --> 00:57:02.908
weakness, couldn't hold
his alcohol very well or

00:57:03.828 --> 00:57:07.808
was okay as long as his wife was around and
he had a key staff officer to help him.

00:57:07.848 --> 00:57:11.808
The point is, alcohol never got in the
way of anything Grant did during the

00:57:11.848 --> 00:57:13.998
war, and he had

00:57:15.008 --> 00:57:18.888
a superlative record of delivering victories
when the United States needed them,

00:57:18.948 --> 00:57:22.688
and Lincoln figured that
out. And the absolute

00:57:22.868 --> 00:57:26.788
proof of his stature because
of this is that in the March

00:57:26.848 --> 00:57:30.588
of 1864, Congress decided to dust

00:57:30.668 --> 00:57:34.648
off the rank of three-star general,
lieutenant general, and give it to

00:57:34.728 --> 00:57:38.008
Grant. The only person who'd ever
had that rank was George Washington,

00:57:38.908 --> 00:57:42.808
the man, Jorge. I mean, he
is the man. And so they

00:57:42.848 --> 00:57:46.788
are saying, "Okay, Grant, you get
the rank the man had," and that

00:57:46.848 --> 00:57:50.808
shows there's never been anyone else who
got it. You're the first. And then he would

00:57:50.888 --> 00:57:54.728
also be the first to get a four-star
rank in the United States Army. That...

00:57:54.868 --> 00:57:58.798
There couldn't be a
better indication of his

00:57:58.848 --> 00:58:03.288
stature than that he got Washington's
rank. Winfield Scott had had it by brevet.

00:58:04.788 --> 00:58:06.048
Grant got the actual rank.

00:58:06.538 --> 00:58:08.408
- So in part, the Grant the Butcher

00:58:09.308 --> 00:58:11.548
label was from the Overland
Campaign that we'll talk about-

00:58:11.588 --> 00:58:12.788
- It is from the Overland Campaign

00:58:12.808 --> 00:58:14.848
- ... as the military decisions made there.

00:58:14.888 --> 00:58:14.948
- Yep.

00:58:15.088 --> 00:58:18.888
- Uh, many would argue that those are
critically important for the success

00:58:19.868 --> 00:58:20.328
of the war.

00:58:20.628 --> 00:58:22.888
- Yes. He and Lee are often compared,

00:58:24.548 --> 00:58:28.408
but for the last year of the war,
they're operating on it's not

00:58:28.488 --> 00:58:32.368
apples to apples because Grant is
general in chief and is managing

00:58:32.408 --> 00:58:36.348
the entire war effort. He's managing what
Sherman's doing and what Sheridan's doing

00:58:36.388 --> 00:58:39.288
and what Nathaniel Bank...
Lee is an army commander.

00:58:39.768 --> 00:58:43.488
He can recommend things to Jefferson Davis,
but he never commands more than an army,

00:58:43.708 --> 00:58:47.628
and so he never operates at the level
that Grant did. Very late in the war, he

00:58:47.648 --> 00:58:50.688
was made general in chief when it
was so late that it didn't matter.

00:58:51.448 --> 00:58:58.178
- So who was Robert E. Lee? In April 1861,
Lee was offered command of the Union Army-

00:58:58.548 --> 00:58:58.928
- He was

00:58:59.128 --> 00:59:01.488
- ... and chose Virginia instead.

00:59:01.628 --> 00:59:01.968
- He did.

00:59:02.288 --> 00:59:06.268
- What was he actually choosing in that
decision? Was he choosing home? Was he

00:59:06.328 --> 00:59:09.888
choosing honor? Was he choosing
slavery? And maybe just

00:59:10.008 --> 00:59:13.948
zooming out, what do we need to understand
in the early steps about Robert

00:59:14.008 --> 00:59:15.628
E. Lee? Who was the man?

00:59:16.057 --> 00:59:19.768
- Well, Lee is sort of the antithesis
of Grant in some ways. Lee is from an

00:59:19.808 --> 00:59:23.738
aristocratic family. Grant was from
a solidly middle-class family. Lee's

00:59:23.748 --> 00:59:25.938
from an aristocratic
family at the very top

00:59:26.908 --> 00:59:30.848
of the Virginia aristocracy. The
Lees are all... He has uncles who

00:59:30.868 --> 00:59:34.658
were prominent, two signed the
Declaration of Independence. His

00:59:34.688 --> 00:59:38.648
father was a famous commander under George
Washington. His father's the one who

00:59:38.828 --> 00:59:42.768
said that Washington was first in war, first in
peace, first in the hearts of his countrymen.

00:59:42.808 --> 00:59:46.358
That's Lee's father, who was later
a prominent Virginia politician.

00:59:46.868 --> 00:59:50.388
Lee's idol is George
Washington. He patterns

00:59:50.428 --> 00:59:54.348
himself on Washington. He differs
from him in some ways, but

00:59:54.428 --> 00:59:58.208
he patterns himself on Washington.
He considered Washington the

00:59:58.248 --> 01:00:01.968
most admirable person in United States
history. And then, of course, he almost

01:00:02.108 --> 01:00:05.848
undoes the work that George Washington
put in place during the American

01:00:05.908 --> 01:00:07.468
Revolution. He chose

01:00:08.588 --> 01:00:12.488
Virginia. He didn't leave... He didn't resign
his commission in the United States Army

01:00:12.528 --> 01:00:16.068
to go with the Confederacy. He resigned his
commission in the United States Army to,

01:00:16.628 --> 01:00:20.548
to go to Virginia, which wasn't in the
Confederacy yet, become the head of state

01:00:20.668 --> 01:00:24.288
forces in Virginia. But then
when Virginia went into the

01:00:24.308 --> 01:00:28.768
Confederacy, he took his Confederate
commission and ended up as the

01:00:30.148 --> 01:00:35.288
third-ranking officer in Confederate
service, and he makes an easy transition to

01:00:36.244 --> 01:00:40.024
A Confederate loyalty. I think
it's his loyalty to Virginia

01:00:40.184 --> 01:00:43.664
and what that means. That means his
family, and of course that means slavery.

01:00:43.704 --> 01:00:47.484
That's part of what that loyalty is.
He's devoted to the slave-holding

01:00:47.564 --> 01:00:51.464
South as well as to Virginia.
Virginia's a subset of that. All of

01:00:51.524 --> 01:00:55.344
that is in one bundle
with Lee. It's all... But

01:00:55.624 --> 01:00:59.244
th- he also had been an
ardent US nationalist

01:00:59.304 --> 01:01:02.124
earlier in his career, and a very
successful military officer.

01:01:02.944 --> 01:01:06.834
Winfield Scott thought he was the greatest soldier
in the army, and Scott, he probably really

01:01:06.884 --> 01:01:10.764
thought, "Except for me." But Scott
is a great soldier that people don't

01:01:10.804 --> 01:01:14.714
remember that way because he became a kinda
Jabba the Hutt figure by the time of the

01:01:14.784 --> 01:01:18.604
Civil War. He's enormous, weighs 350
pounds, has to be winched onto his horse.

01:01:19.784 --> 01:01:23.744
But he was a very smart guy
and he thought very highly

01:01:23.784 --> 01:01:27.984
of Lee. Lee was a superb soldier,
as he would show during the war,

01:01:28.944 --> 01:01:30.024
but he's on the wrong side.

01:01:30.784 --> 01:01:33.524
- Maybe just to throw in a few
more characters, I do think

01:01:34.644 --> 01:01:36.524
the folks on both sides that are the,

01:01:37.364 --> 01:01:41.134
of the more radical persuasion are quite
interesting. Somebody you spoke about,

01:01:41.324 --> 01:01:44.844
I'm not sure where, think it might've been
in your lecture series about Thaddeus

01:01:44.904 --> 01:01:45.704
Stevens.

01:01:47.034 --> 01:01:48.084
- Yes, the Thaddeus Stevens.

01:01:48.664 --> 01:01:51.764
Thaddeus Stevens was one of the most
radical of the radical Republicans.

01:01:51.804 --> 01:01:54.014
He's a congressman from Pennsylvania

01:01:54.864 --> 01:01:58.724
who... People think people say harsh
things now in Congress. Thaddeus Stevens

01:01:58.784 --> 01:02:04.144
just eviscerated people. And he was
way out... I mean, he was an ardent

01:02:04.224 --> 01:02:08.044
abolitionist and for equal rights
for women in many ways and,

01:02:08.184 --> 01:02:10.784
and he picked up a number of
causes and championed them.

01:02:11.084 --> 01:02:17.044
- I think in your lecture you mentioned this
thing which I just love, the slime line.

01:02:17.504 --> 01:02:21.074
- He had a-- This is his kind of
line. He's sitting in the House of

01:02:21.164 --> 01:02:24.184
Representatives and one of his opponents-

01:02:24.194 --> 01:02:28.044
... and he's speaking, and one of his opponents
starts to come down to the floor and he

01:02:28.104 --> 01:02:31.964
says, and this is a paraphrase, "I'm
gonna pause while my colleague,"

01:02:31.984 --> 01:02:35.844
from wherever he was from, "slinks
across the floor of this House and

01:02:35.884 --> 01:02:38.324
adheres to his seat by his own slime."

01:02:38.364 --> 01:02:39.044
- Yeah. Beautiful.

01:02:39.084 --> 01:02:41.524
- That's a great line. Impromptu.

01:02:41.764 --> 01:02:45.434
He had a... Women used to ask
for locks of hair as, from

01:02:45.544 --> 01:02:49.484
famous people, and he was completely
bald and wore a full wig. He had

01:02:49.524 --> 01:02:53.444
a woman stop him on the steps to the
Capitol and ask for a lock of his

01:02:53.484 --> 01:02:57.224
hair, took his whole wig off and
said, "Madam, have them all." And c-

01:02:57.484 --> 01:02:57.904
- Which is brilliant

01:02:57.924 --> 01:02:59.464
- no, it's very good.

01:02:59.764 --> 01:03:01.584
- But of the radical persuasion.

01:03:01.764 --> 01:03:05.844
- Oh, absolutely. Yes, he defined it. He
is the character in Birth of a Nation

01:03:06.744 --> 01:03:10.624
Stoneman, the character of Stoneman,
the evil radical Republican

01:03:10.664 --> 01:03:13.584
named Stoneman, is patterned
on Thaddeus Stevens.

01:03:14.044 --> 01:03:18.444
- All right. If we can, let's talk
about the buildup to the war.

01:03:18.984 --> 01:03:19.304
- All right.

01:03:19.564 --> 01:03:23.504
- What were the key states on
both sides that were debating?

01:03:23.744 --> 01:03:27.344
What are the ones that
were certainly going to be

01:03:27.424 --> 01:03:31.414
firmly on one side or the other?
And maybe what are the ones that

01:03:31.444 --> 01:03:33.424
were critical at the, at the borderline?

01:03:33.904 --> 01:03:37.844
- The states that were in the forefront of the
secession movement were the Deep South states, and

01:03:37.904 --> 01:03:41.744
South Carolina was kind of ground
central for what was going

01:03:41.804 --> 01:03:45.624
on there. It just so happened that
the Democrats had their national

01:03:45.704 --> 01:03:49.564
convention in Charleston in 1860,
which is the worst place in the

01:03:49.604 --> 01:03:56.324
world they could've had it if they wanted
a quiet, rational discussion of anything.

01:03:56.524 --> 01:04:00.264
South Carolina is the first state to secede,
and then is quickly followed. You can

01:04:00.324 --> 01:04:04.124
just go the tier across Alabama,
Mississippi, Florida, Georgia,

01:04:04.424 --> 01:04:07.934
Texas. That's the... It's seven
states. The Deep South states go

01:04:08.104 --> 01:04:11.994
out in that initial... But
there's a long period then,

01:04:12.004 --> 01:04:15.394
and they gather in Montgomery, Alabama,
to establish a new government with

01:04:15.404 --> 01:04:17.954
Montgomery as the capital
of the Confederacy.

01:04:18.664 --> 01:04:22.564
Delegates from those states do. And
they set up a provisional government

01:04:22.624 --> 01:04:25.924
that's gonna last a year and call
for the first national elections in

01:04:25.944 --> 01:04:29.924
1861. There are only seven states
there, and really only six for a

01:04:29.984 --> 01:04:33.584
lot of it 'cause Texas got there late.
It was the last of the first seven to

01:04:33.624 --> 01:04:37.573
secede. But the states that haven't
seceded yet are the ones with

01:04:37.604 --> 01:04:40.884
the most people. Virginia, which
is the most populous, North

01:04:40.924 --> 01:04:44.764
Carolina, Tennessee,
Kentucky, Missouri. Eight

01:04:44.904 --> 01:04:48.824
states have not gone out. Eight slaveholding
states have not gone out. The most

01:04:48.864 --> 01:04:51.073
important one is Virginia
among those that-

01:04:51.073 --> 01:04:54.034
... that have not gone
out. The question is,

01:04:55.364 --> 01:04:59.154
can they find some resolution to
all of this, or is it gonna get

01:04:59.184 --> 01:05:02.993
more serious, and are those states gonna
go? And it came to focus, once again, on

01:05:03.004 --> 01:05:04.364
Charleston, South Carolina,

01:05:05.234 --> 01:05:08.204
and a federal installation in
Charleston Harbor, Fort Sumter.

01:05:09.444 --> 01:05:13.004
Much United States property and illic- and

01:05:13.164 --> 01:05:16.784
installations had been just
given over to the Confederates.

01:05:17.684 --> 01:05:21.424
And the question is, can we do it
with this one as well? This is a very

01:05:21.544 --> 01:05:25.444
difficult decision to make, and
it comes down to what's gonna

01:05:25.484 --> 01:05:29.424
happen in Charleston Harbor. And in the
end, I mean, Jefferson Davis believed

01:05:29.434 --> 01:05:33.284
that he couldn't allow it to remain a
federal installation 'cause it's actually

01:05:33.364 --> 01:05:37.324
in the Confederacy from their point
of view. And Lincoln, who's not

01:05:37.364 --> 01:05:41.304
conceding the right of states to secede.
They're, they're temporarily out

01:05:41.344 --> 01:05:45.304
of our power, but we have to maintain...
He decided in the end to maintain

01:05:45.344 --> 01:05:50.724
power there, not to reinforce it,
but to resupply it, needed supplies.

01:05:51.804 --> 01:05:55.524
And that's what triggers it. Then, then
the ball's in Jefferson Davis' court.

01:05:55.534 --> 01:05:59.484
"Okay, they're gonna resupply it. What
do we do?" And they decided we need to

01:05:59.544 --> 01:06:03.454
insist that it surrender, and if it won't
surrender, we need to fire on it. And

01:06:03.484 --> 01:06:07.384
the Confederates fired the first shots. They
would've said, "We were forced to fire the

01:06:07.464 --> 01:06:11.264
first shots by what Lincoln did." Nobody
wanted to be the ones who fired the first

01:06:11.304 --> 01:06:14.984
shots. But in the end, it's the
Confederates who fired the first shots.

01:06:15.164 --> 01:06:19.124
And once that is fired,
the key thing, the key

01:06:19.204 --> 01:06:21.744
thing is not the firing
on Sumter in the end,

01:06:22.764 --> 01:06:25.444
but that sets up the key
thing, which is Lincoln's call

01:06:26.384 --> 01:06:30.004
for volunteers to suppress the
rebellion, and he is gonna ask each

01:06:30.124 --> 01:06:31.544
state to supply people.

01:06:32.364 --> 01:06:36.244
And the Upper South states, most of them
have said, "We're not gonna secede, but

01:06:36.404 --> 01:06:40.204
you can't... But, but the states that
are gone had the right to secede-

01:06:40.224 --> 01:06:44.184
... and you can't do anything to make
them come back." Lincoln is seeming to

01:06:44.244 --> 01:06:49.148
make them do things to come back.
And so four more states went out.

01:06:49.548 --> 01:06:53.288
Virginia went out. Arkansas
went out. Tennessee went out.

01:06:53.728 --> 01:06:57.708
Then North Carolina went out. And
now you have an 11-state Confederacy

01:06:57.768 --> 01:07:01.108
rather than seven. But more
than that, you have Virginia.

01:07:02.078 --> 01:07:05.828
The city of Richmond has 40% of all
the industrial capacity in the entire

01:07:05.908 --> 01:07:09.828
Confederacy, what became the Confederacy.
Virginia is the most populous state, has

01:07:09.848 --> 01:07:13.148
the largest slave population,
the largest white population.

01:07:13.748 --> 01:07:17.508
It supplies the most important
military leaders. And the

01:07:17.568 --> 01:07:20.908
capital ends up in Richmond. They
move from Montgomery to Richmond

01:07:22.068 --> 01:07:25.808
in acknowledgment of how important
Richmond is, that this is the key

01:07:25.888 --> 01:07:29.368
state. The key states that didn't
go out were Maryland, Kentucky,

01:07:30.028 --> 01:07:34.008
and Missouri. Delaware didn't either, but who
cares? The other three didn't go out, and that

01:07:34.048 --> 01:07:37.788
really mattered because they were
more populous. But 11 are gone.

01:07:38.688 --> 01:07:39.888
Four remain in the Union.

01:07:40.628 --> 01:07:43.568
- And to lay out some more context,
going to Perplexity here,

01:07:44.668 --> 01:07:48.608
by total enslaved population
on the eve of the Civil War,

01:07:49.188 --> 01:07:52.668
we have looking at the top
five states, Virginia,

01:07:53.488 --> 01:07:57.408
as you said, with the most
number of slaves, 490,000.

01:07:57.568 --> 01:08:03.768
Then Georgia, Mississippi, Alabama, South
Carolina, all with over 400,000 slaves.

01:08:04.168 --> 01:08:07.928
- Yes. And Mississippi and South Carolina
have the highest proportion of

01:08:07.948 --> 01:08:11.648
enslaved people of any of
the states. And this is an

01:08:11.688 --> 01:08:15.488
interesting little fact from the 1860 census.
When you're talking about white people in the

01:08:15.548 --> 01:08:19.228
United States, those are the two wealthiest
states per capita in the United States,

01:08:19.448 --> 01:08:23.528
are South Carolina and
Mississippi in 1860.

01:08:23.568 --> 01:08:26.748
- Boy, the complicated history
of this great nation of ours.

01:08:27.208 --> 01:08:30.888
- It's hard to overstate the resources.
The states that sent the most men

01:08:31.068 --> 01:08:33.328
into the Confederate Army are Virginia,

01:08:34.228 --> 01:08:37.918
Tennessee, North Carolina,
and Georgia. Three of those

01:08:38.128 --> 01:08:41.978
come in this second wave of secessions.
Three of them, three of the four. Only

01:08:41.988 --> 01:08:45.948
Georgia among the original ones was in those
top four states. And so you would have had a

01:08:46.108 --> 01:08:50.028
much, much smaller pool
of manpower for that

01:08:50.108 --> 01:08:54.107
earlier version of the Confederacy. It's
inconceivable to me that you would have

01:08:54.168 --> 01:08:56.068
had the war we had with just that version.

01:08:56.708 --> 01:08:58.948
- So it does feel, just to linger on it,

01:08:59.968 --> 01:09:03.868
like there was a lot on the
balance here. Like there was a

01:09:03.988 --> 01:09:07.018
lot of different decisions
that could have been made,

01:09:08.308 --> 01:09:12.158
even on the political side, that
could have turned the tides.

01:09:12.488 --> 01:09:16.068
- For example, there's a secession
convention in Virginia.

01:09:16.708 --> 01:09:20.108
It goes in... It votes
twice against secession.

01:09:20.688 --> 01:09:24.568
Twice. It's only after Sumter that
they come back for another vote.

01:09:24.768 --> 01:09:28.288
I mean, it's a Unionist vote in
that convention in Virginia.

01:09:29.268 --> 01:09:31.768
And it's only after Fort Sumter.

01:09:32.048 --> 01:09:32.058
- Wow.

01:09:32.058 --> 01:09:35.608
- And then it's still so divided
that a big chunk of Virginia

01:09:36.188 --> 01:09:39.688
decide that's how you get West
Virginia. West Virginia essentially

01:09:39.728 --> 01:09:42.848
secedes from Virginia- ... in
the course of the Civil War.

01:09:42.888 --> 01:09:46.668
- And given the industrial capacity of Virginia, that
was a critical state. So it could have been...

01:09:47.388 --> 01:09:50.988
Virginia could have changed. We could
have avoided the Civil War with

01:09:51.068 --> 01:09:51.868
Virginia alone.

01:09:52.008 --> 01:09:56.068
- It would have really changed. I mean,
Virginia is the key. Virginia is the key

01:09:56.908 --> 01:10:00.308
because of the manpower, the industrial
capacity, the food production,

01:10:00.548 --> 01:10:04.168
and Virginia gives you Lee, Jackson, Jeb

01:10:04.228 --> 01:10:07.668
Stuart. I mean, it, and the most
soldiers of any state in the

01:10:07.688 --> 01:10:11.528
Confederacy. It's just, it's a real game

01:10:11.628 --> 01:10:15.448
changer to get Virginia.
Plus, it also gives you these

01:10:15.548 --> 01:10:19.508
unbelievable ties to the revolutionary
generation. Hey, it's George's

01:10:19.568 --> 01:10:23.528
home state. It is now in the
Confederacy with his big statue

01:10:23.588 --> 01:10:27.328
on the Capitol grounds of George Washington.
I mean, gives you, you get Washington, the

01:10:27.408 --> 01:10:31.408
ties to Jefferson, the ties to Madison.
All of this comes in with Virginia.

01:10:32.108 --> 01:10:36.368
- So I think a lot of historians have
said that the Civil War was inevitable.

01:10:37.348 --> 01:10:41.328
It... You're really painting a picture
here where it perhaps was not inevitable.

01:10:41.968 --> 01:10:45.897
- I don't think it's inevitable. Things
happen in a sequence. I was at a

01:10:45.928 --> 01:10:49.888
conf- academic conference once when they were
trying to decide what important dates were,

01:10:49.908 --> 01:10:53.588
and one... And they concluded
that no dates are, dates are an

01:10:53.648 --> 01:10:57.288
artificial construction. No date
is more important than any other.

01:10:57.368 --> 01:11:00.968
That is so obviously
preposterous. I mean, things

01:11:01.028 --> 01:11:04.608
happen in a sequence. They don't just
sort of happen. Lincoln's elected,

01:11:04.828 --> 01:11:08.478
then South Carolina secedes, then other
states secede, then... I mean, things

01:11:08.568 --> 01:11:12.168
happen as a consequence of what happened
before, and you have these various

01:11:12.228 --> 01:11:16.098
things that have to happen
before the next thing does. And

01:11:16.128 --> 01:11:18.878
then we end up with a war
that seems to be inevitable,

01:11:19.688 --> 01:11:23.528
but it's not... It's just the way things
played out. All of these factors are in

01:11:23.608 --> 01:11:27.538
motion, and you end up with the sequence that gets
you to where people start shooting each other.

01:11:28.128 --> 01:11:31.888
- Yeah. When you read history and look back,
it seems inevitable. But if we were to

01:11:31.928 --> 01:11:35.817
place ourselves in that moment of time, do
you think there's decisions that could have

01:11:35.848 --> 01:11:36.317
been made?

01:11:36.948 --> 01:11:40.858
- The decision to not let Fort
Sumter, I mean, that's a

01:11:40.888 --> 01:11:44.808
big decision to make. If they had just, if Lincoln
had just said, "Go ahead and take Fort Sumter,"

01:11:45.868 --> 01:11:49.568
okay, then maybe... Then what
would it have taken to trigger

01:11:49.848 --> 01:11:53.768
the departure of those upper South states?
That would have changed that whole equation.

01:11:54.188 --> 01:11:57.068
But Lincoln decided you
just can't keep giving them

01:11:57.728 --> 01:12:01.548
everything that they want if you don't concede
that they have the right to do what they did.

01:12:01.808 --> 01:12:05.508
- Yeah, there would be a
continued heated political

01:12:06.428 --> 01:12:10.308
debates, intense political debates
about the expansion of slavery, but it

01:12:10.348 --> 01:12:15.668
would not cause rebellion to then
escalate into a full-on hot war.

01:12:16.288 --> 01:12:20.188
- I think that's conceivable. I don't
think the war was inevitable. I think it

01:12:20.328 --> 01:12:24.288
But I think the conditions were
present for it to happen, obviously.

01:12:24.408 --> 01:12:28.158
I mean, that's stating... 'Cause
it did happen. But I think it's

01:12:28.208 --> 01:12:32.128
very tricky to just say that
it was inevitable. That's kind

01:12:32.148 --> 01:12:35.888
of starting at the end of the story and just saying,
"Let's look at the factors that got us here.

01:12:36.028 --> 01:12:39.068
We know it was gonna happen, and these are
the things that pointed this direction."

01:12:39.748 --> 01:12:44.948
- Can we just actually linger on this, on the
nature of war? Over 700,000 people dead.

01:12:45.948 --> 01:12:51.608
How do we make sense of our capacity
as, in that context, Americans?

01:12:52.876 --> 01:12:55.536
To kill sometimes literally our brothers

01:12:55.856 --> 01:12:57.436
- Yes, sometimes literally our brothers

01:12:57.496 --> 01:12:58.596
- How is that even possible?

01:12:58.796 --> 01:13:02.736
- Well, one thing, it tells me things are pretty
important to people on both sides if you're

01:13:02.756 --> 01:13:06.656
gonna continue for a war that long.
I mean, there was a period in the

01:13:06.696 --> 01:13:08.416
literature, in the scholarly literature

01:13:09.276 --> 01:13:12.996
in the 1980s and into the 1990s where
they argued that the Confederacy

01:13:13.036 --> 01:13:16.916
wasn't a nation, and the proof was that they didn't
try very hard, that they... If they'd really

01:13:16.956 --> 01:13:19.976
thought of themselves as a nation, they
would have tried harder and fought longer.

01:13:20.696 --> 01:13:24.416
Well, they lost between four and a half
and five and a half percent of their

01:13:24.456 --> 01:13:30.456
entire population, which is more
than Great Britain, France, Germany,

01:13:31.276 --> 01:13:35.216
or Russia during World War I. They
had a higher percentage loss than any

01:13:35.256 --> 01:13:39.906
of the powers in World War I. The US lost
about 1.8% of its population from the war.

01:13:40.956 --> 01:13:44.816
US, in World War II, three-tenths of
1% of its population... That seems...

01:13:44.976 --> 01:13:48.256
They lost more than 30% of
their entire white military-age

01:13:48.356 --> 01:13:52.056
males in the war. It'd be the equivalent
of about 18 million Americans being killed

01:13:52.116 --> 01:13:58.276
now in a war. That seems to me that they're
pretty serious about something there.

01:13:58.336 --> 01:14:02.216
- It means that's one of the
largest percentages of loss.

01:14:02.846 --> 01:14:04.786
That's... If that's a
measure of dedication-

01:14:05.056 --> 01:14:08.956
- If that's your measure of dedication,
yes, and it seems to me that it should be

01:14:09.036 --> 01:14:12.956
one of the metrics that you bring to
play that, that it's... They just keep

01:14:12.976 --> 01:14:15.136
at it. They didn't give up easily.

01:14:15.596 --> 01:14:20.256
- What can we say about the motivation?
On the psychological side, did the

01:14:21.356 --> 01:14:25.216
members of the Confederacy
hate the members of the Union?

01:14:25.236 --> 01:14:29.056
- Well, that escalated on both sides during
the war. As more and more loss came,

01:14:29.156 --> 01:14:32.916
you... They did. There was more hatred
directed toward the enemy more and more.

01:14:33.176 --> 01:14:36.496
This notion that it's they're all brothers
and not really mad at each other and...

01:14:37.456 --> 01:14:39.056
That just doesn't hold up. There are

01:14:39.936 --> 01:14:43.676
places where the soldiers fraternized
with one another and traded tobacco for

01:14:43.756 --> 01:14:46.016
coffee and that kind of
stuff, but no, the bitterness

01:14:46.836 --> 01:14:50.656
as the war went on, and there was greater
loss on the home front, and as the

01:14:50.696 --> 01:14:54.676
war became more all-encompassing
and really went after the entire,

01:14:55.276 --> 01:14:59.236
The civilian side of the Confederacy
was not shielded from it, the

01:14:59.316 --> 01:15:03.135
bitterness increased and increased
and increased. And there's a

01:15:03.176 --> 01:15:06.586
lot of unhappiness with the war
behind the lines in the Confederacy,

01:15:06.756 --> 01:15:11.186
absolutely, unhappiness with the
government. There's desertion.

01:15:12.616 --> 01:15:15.145
About 15% of Confederate
soldiers deserted.

01:15:16.156 --> 01:15:19.786
About 12% of United States
soldiers deserted. So there's

01:15:19.796 --> 01:15:22.396
desertion on both sides.
There's war-weariness,

01:15:23.216 --> 01:15:27.196
but the war goes on because I think if
you had been able to poll most white

01:15:27.256 --> 01:15:30.276
Southerners even late in the war and
say, "Okay, here are your choices.

01:15:32.776 --> 01:15:36.556
You either have Jefferson Davis in the government
and the war goes on, or you're going back

01:15:36.616 --> 01:15:40.366
into the United States and the Lincoln
government will be in charge and you won't have

01:15:40.396 --> 01:15:44.386
slavery. Which one do you pick?" They're
gonna pick the first one, and that's why

01:15:44.416 --> 01:15:45.816
the war went on as long as it did.

01:15:46.276 --> 01:15:49.516
- But at the same time, to fast-forward,

01:15:50.736 --> 01:15:55.516
and that maybe teaches you something about
the nature of war, but after the war,

01:15:56.896 --> 01:16:00.516
maybe some of the decisions that
Grant made, people were able to

01:16:00.536 --> 01:16:01.705
rediscover the brotherhood.

01:16:02.216 --> 01:16:03.806
- You mean at Appomattox
you're talking about-

01:16:03.856 --> 01:16:04.016
- Appomattox

01:16:04.056 --> 01:16:05.076
- ... the terms at Appomattox.

01:16:05.176 --> 01:16:05.296
- Yes.

01:16:05.316 --> 01:16:07.746
- Yes, the... And those
were... And he didn't just

01:16:08.816 --> 01:16:12.696
reach up in the air and say, "I've got some
terms." The... Lincoln very specifically...

01:16:12.776 --> 01:16:16.316
He met with Lincoln shortly before
Appomattox. Sherman was there.

01:16:16.856 --> 01:16:20.786
One top naval officer was there, Grant
and Lincoln, and Lincoln said, "This is

01:16:20.816 --> 01:16:22.485
what I want. This is the
kind of peace I want."

01:16:22.485 --> 01:16:26.425
"The whole point of this war is to put
the nation back together, and a good

01:16:26.556 --> 01:16:27.616
start on that is..."

01:16:28.496 --> 01:16:32.476
I mean, if it's just a rebellion, what
do you do with traitors and rebels?

01:16:32.536 --> 01:16:33.516
You hang them.

01:16:33.656 --> 01:16:37.376
You don't bring them back. You don't treat them
as prisoners of war, which the United States

01:16:37.516 --> 01:16:41.476
did during this. You, he... But the goal is
to put the nation back together, and Lincoln

01:16:41.516 --> 01:16:46.036
said, "This is a good start. Make
them sign a parole. Make them accept

01:16:47.056 --> 01:16:51.026
the end of slavery and the fact that they're back
in the Union. If they do that, they get to go

01:16:51.036 --> 01:16:54.856
home and resume their lives." Hadn't
been decided yet whether they'll get to

01:16:54.896 --> 01:16:58.876
vote, but let them go home. Don't
imprison them. Don't execute

01:16:58.916 --> 01:17:01.976
them. Don't execute their
leaders. That's the decision.

01:17:02.016 --> 01:17:05.056
- From the perspective of the Union, what do
you think about that kind of forgiveness?

01:17:05.796 --> 01:17:09.446
- Well, I think that it was sort
of a nod toward the direction of

01:17:09.476 --> 01:17:13.276
forgiveness, but I don't think the real...
I don't think real forgiveness was there.

01:17:13.286 --> 01:17:16.966
It was still extremely hard feelings
on both sides, and Lincoln's

01:17:17.016 --> 01:17:20.846
assassination upped the ante on
that, especially for a while,

01:17:21.636 --> 01:17:24.706
in the United States.
The, the forgiveness...

01:17:27.696 --> 01:17:31.586
Two things in terms of the aftermath of
the war, two words that often come into

01:17:31.656 --> 01:17:35.296
play are reunion and reconciliation.

01:17:35.836 --> 01:17:39.716
Those are two other things that are
often conflated. Reunion is literally

01:17:39.736 --> 01:17:43.296
putting the nation back together.
That happened. Reconcile, true

01:17:43.376 --> 01:17:47.336
reconciliation, that's much, much,
much tougher, and that didn't

01:17:47.396 --> 01:17:51.436
come anytime soon on the part of millions
of people on both sides, I think.

01:17:51.936 --> 01:17:57.996
- And we'll probably maybe talk about
in multiple ways how maybe that is

01:17:58.036 --> 01:17:59.296
something that's still going on.

01:17:59.456 --> 01:18:03.446
- Oh, there are echoes of the Civil War all
over the place. It's not still being fought,

01:18:03.996 --> 01:18:07.466
but you can see resonances of the
Civil War because it's such a

01:18:07.496 --> 01:18:11.376
profoundly disruptive
event that gets right

01:18:11.416 --> 01:18:15.316
at the heart of what the republic means.
I mean, and so it's... There's nothing

01:18:15.336 --> 01:18:19.556
else like it in US history, and
it's a great moment of truth,

01:18:20.536 --> 01:18:24.096
the residual aftermath and
effects of which lingered for

01:18:24.116 --> 01:18:26.516
decades and are still present
in some ways, I think.

01:18:27.496 --> 01:18:33.536
- So in 1859, most Americans
were just living their life.

01:18:34.896 --> 01:18:38.376
Did they have a sense
what was on the horizon?

01:18:39.136 --> 01:18:43.116
- It's what I like to call the Appomattox
Syndrome, which starts at the end of the story

01:18:44.156 --> 01:18:48.036
And says, "Of course there was gonna be a war
because the war was inevitable, and of course the

01:18:48.056 --> 01:18:51.775
United States was gonna win and slavery was
gonna be destroyed," because they were.

01:18:52.116 --> 01:18:53.826
So let's find out how we got right here.

01:18:54.096 --> 01:18:58.056
But no, most people didn't
wake up in Iowa in 1857

01:18:58.096 --> 01:19:00.386
and their first thought was,
"Boy, I hate the South."

01:19:00.446 --> 01:19:03.856
"Those slaveholders, they're really..."
No. People got on with their

01:19:03.896 --> 01:19:07.856
quotidian lives and they got to the
farm or they went to their business and

01:19:07.896 --> 01:19:11.696
they did... They weren't... Nobody
knew that they were living in the

01:19:11.796 --> 01:19:17.076
antebellum era, and that those great
antebellum years were about to come to an end.

01:19:17.356 --> 01:19:21.096
They... That is something that
it's hard to get people not...

01:19:21.556 --> 01:19:25.456
To just put it out of your mind that that's all
anybody is thinking about. They're thinking,

01:19:25.545 --> 01:19:27.556
"There's a lot of other stuff going on."

01:19:28.676 --> 01:19:32.156
There's a big gold rush in Colorado.
More people go to Colorado than went to

01:19:32.196 --> 01:19:35.986
California for a while.
There is... And you have

01:19:36.076 --> 01:19:40.005
these tremendous changes coming about,

01:19:40.856 --> 01:19:44.836
technological changes of the 1850s
where you have the real expansion of

01:19:44.896 --> 01:19:48.696
railroads, the real expansion of the
telegraph. There's a shrinking of time and

01:19:48.856 --> 01:19:52.736
space that's remarkable for people
at the time. It seemed dizzying

01:19:52.776 --> 01:19:56.696
to them at the time. There are a lot of things
to think about other than how much I hate

01:19:56.736 --> 01:20:00.746
Yankees if I'm in South Carolina, or I hate
slaveholders if I live in New Hampshire.

01:20:01.996 --> 01:20:05.516
But periodically, there are
these events that pop up that

01:20:05.736 --> 01:20:09.316
refocus on the differences
between the sections.

01:20:09.576 --> 01:20:13.436
And you have the Kansas-Nebraska
Act. You have Dred

01:20:13.496 --> 01:20:17.856
Scott. You have the caning of Charles
Sumner on the floor of the Senate.

01:20:18.756 --> 01:20:22.466
You have Uncle Tom's Cabin earlier
in the decade in 18-- and what...

01:20:22.566 --> 01:20:26.316
And the impact that that had on attitudes
towards slavery. Doesn't create a bunch of

01:20:26.396 --> 01:20:30.336
abolitionists, but it creates a
bunch of people who rethink their

01:20:30.356 --> 01:20:31.506
thoughts about slavery.

01:20:32.416 --> 01:20:36.856
- Sorry to ask the big
question about history. But,

01:20:38.356 --> 01:20:40.656
you know, we're living today, 2026,

01:20:43.136 --> 01:20:46.066
and there's the sense that
every day you wake up,

01:20:47.836 --> 01:20:50.276
the possibilities before us are endless,

01:20:51.196 --> 01:20:55.156
for the good, for the bad, for
the truly destructive. There's a

01:20:55.316 --> 01:20:59.556
an eerie sense that the possibility of
a world war in different kinds of ways,

01:21:00.416 --> 01:21:07.976
whether it's sparked in the Middle East or
sparked in China and Taiwan, or sparked

01:21:08.036 --> 01:21:09.096
in Ukraine-Russia,

01:21:10.056 --> 01:21:13.816
or elsewhere totally surprising in
the world, may be related to nuclear

01:21:13.876 --> 01:21:17.256
weapons or not. There's a sense
that everything is possible.

01:21:17.876 --> 01:21:20.886
And of course, when history is
written about the 21st century,

01:21:21.756 --> 01:21:23.076
it will all be obvious.

01:21:23.316 --> 01:21:25.336
- It's always obvious in
retrospect. That's right.

01:21:25.596 --> 01:21:32.996
- What can we learn about... from the
Civil War about how history unfolds

01:21:33.036 --> 01:21:35.416
itself, I suppose, for the modern day?

01:21:35.716 --> 01:21:37.836
- Well, I think that the
potential threats now

01:21:38.676 --> 01:21:42.596
are different from the potential threats in
the mid-19th century. The nuclear dimension,

01:21:42.756 --> 01:21:46.586
some kind of really brutal use of

01:21:46.986 --> 01:21:50.806
pathogens. I mean, there are lots of
things that could happen now that couldn't

01:21:50.856 --> 01:21:57.356
happen then, at least not because humans
were making them happen. But I mean, to,

01:21:58.176 --> 01:22:02.896
to me, one of the things to take away from
the Civil War is really the resiliency

01:22:03.976 --> 01:22:07.936
of this republic, which I
still think it's a resilient

01:22:07.996 --> 01:22:10.596
republic. We're living
in a sort of wacky time.

01:22:10.756 --> 01:22:14.616
Uh, but I believe that
the... I think that...

01:22:14.716 --> 01:22:18.596
I have a great faith in the
resiliency of the nation, I really

01:22:18.656 --> 01:22:22.276
do, of the United States. I think it's been
through much tougher times than we're in now,

01:22:23.056 --> 01:22:26.976
and I think that we will weather
this, although everybody

01:22:27.016 --> 01:22:30.956
will have some scar tissue.
There might even come a

01:22:30.996 --> 01:22:34.576
time when our leadership class
actually leads, and which would be

01:22:34.656 --> 01:22:38.496
interesting. And I'm saying, you
know, I'm talking about both

01:22:38.696 --> 01:22:42.576
sides of the fence here.
But it is... That, to me,

01:22:43.456 --> 01:22:47.356
this is, this is the supreme test
of a republic that had been put in

01:22:47.396 --> 01:22:50.696
place in the late 18th century. Can
that late 18th century republic

01:22:50.776 --> 01:22:54.416
adjust, including adjusting
to deal with the profoundly

01:22:54.436 --> 01:22:58.396
disruptive issue of slavery,
in a way that allows it

01:22:58.416 --> 01:23:02.196
to go forward? And the answer
is yes. In a war that brings

01:23:02.276 --> 01:23:06.236
slaughter on an unimaginable scale and
brings social disruption in the former

01:23:06.376 --> 01:23:10.356
slave-holding states on an unimaginable...
It just couldn't be more profoundly

01:23:10.416 --> 01:23:14.256
disruptive if you're a
Confederate. There's not slavery

01:23:14.276 --> 01:23:18.036
anymore, and we lost the war, and a third
of our military-age white men are dead.

01:23:18.136 --> 01:23:22.016
Wow, that's a bad four years
for our team. What's gonna

01:23:22.076 --> 01:23:25.766
happen here? The Republic emerged from
that and worked it out, and I think it

01:23:25.956 --> 01:23:28.856
is... It goes back to, to me,

01:23:29.756 --> 01:23:33.316
the intelligence of the founding generation
that put in place documents that

01:23:33.396 --> 01:23:36.546
actually allowed the Republic to
deal with something like this.

01:23:36.696 --> 01:23:39.396
And it's sort of a miracle to me

01:23:40.336 --> 01:23:43.836
that from a nation that was
as prejudiced, racially

01:23:43.856 --> 01:23:47.756
prejudiced as the United
States was, you get the 13th,

01:23:47.816 --> 01:23:51.716
14th, and 15th Amendments out
of this. It's just kind of

01:23:53.876 --> 01:23:55.696
mind-boggling that that happens.

01:23:56.856 --> 01:24:00.656
- Yeah, I think resilience and

01:24:01.576 --> 01:24:05.236
adaptive innovation often looks
like chaos in the moment.

01:24:05.316 --> 01:24:09.286
- It absolutely does. It absolutely
does. But then when you really get

01:24:09.316 --> 01:24:13.096
to the end of that story, you look, and
there you are, and oh, it's okay, the

01:24:13.136 --> 01:24:19.876
nation is still intact. And the 13th
Amendment is in place. Wow, we didn't think-

01:24:20.006 --> 01:24:22.676
... that was gonna happen in 1861-

01:24:22.876 --> 01:24:26.756
... when secession started. I mean, it
really... One, another lesson is that you

01:24:26.836 --> 01:24:32.226
cannot control what a great
military event can set loose.

01:24:32.226 --> 01:24:37.146
I mean, no, things that nobody imagined
would happen, including expansion of

01:24:37.176 --> 01:24:37.876
central power

01:24:38.916 --> 01:24:42.776
in ways that no one would have imagined. You don't
get to ratchet that back and say, "Well, that'll

01:24:42.816 --> 01:24:46.676
never happen again." No, you know what? It happened,
and maybe it will happen again because we've

01:24:46.736 --> 01:24:50.424
seen it happen. Nobody imagined
that. The federal budget in

01:24:50.464 --> 01:24:54.343
1860 was $63 million. That was
the entire federal budget.

01:24:55.224 --> 01:24:59.214
The federal budget in 1865
was $1.2 billion, and

01:24:59.284 --> 01:25:03.084
if you throw in the Confederate side of that,
it gets you over $2 billion, which means

01:25:03.124 --> 01:25:10.504
federal expenditures in 1860 would've
lasted about 11 days in 1865. Nobody

01:25:10.544 --> 01:25:14.204
foresaw that. Absolutely
nobody foresaw that. And

01:25:16.144 --> 01:25:19.524
education legislation that would create
the great land grant colleges and

01:25:19.624 --> 01:25:25.324
universities and no- it's...
The war brought a lot

01:25:26.224 --> 01:25:31.404
- And I think most of the things that have
happened through the hard periods of our

01:25:31.444 --> 01:25:33.914
country's history, nobody
could have really f-

01:25:34.384 --> 01:25:38.964
foreseen, and then we somehow figure it
out, and that gives me a lot of hope.

01:25:39.124 --> 01:25:39.164
- We-

01:25:39.194 --> 01:25:45.384
- That this kinda like extreme
division, the arguments, the people

01:25:45.544 --> 01:25:49.044
basically speaking in the harshest
of ways against each other,

01:25:50.824 --> 01:25:53.724
that is like the engine of,

01:25:54.544 --> 01:26:02.084
brainstorming and figuring problems out
that in the long arc of history create

01:26:02.124 --> 01:26:06.064
progress. But in the short term,
it feels like a dumpster fire.

01:26:06.604 --> 01:26:11.944
- It does. And to me, the arc of US history

01:26:13.484 --> 01:26:17.324
has been, and Lincoln talked about this
too, it's an expansion of freedoms and

01:26:17.364 --> 01:26:19.564
liberties. That's the,
that's the long arc,

01:26:20.384 --> 01:26:23.884
with very problematical
places along the way. But

01:26:25.004 --> 01:26:28.984
again, the documents allow for
that, and the process allows

01:26:29.004 --> 01:26:32.264
for that, and I think that's one
of the remarkable things about

01:26:33.704 --> 01:26:40.534
Philadelphia in the summer of 1787. I mean,
those guys, a lot of whom own slaves,

01:26:41.784 --> 01:26:45.584
put together something that's proved to be
resilient and adaptable and changeable,

01:26:45.684 --> 01:26:49.514
and it has evolved in a way that is in
the right direction, although there are

01:26:49.584 --> 01:26:51.324
always problems.

01:26:52.204 --> 01:26:56.024
- I love this country. I
will never stop saying how

01:26:56.063 --> 01:26:58.864
much I love this country. It's
an incredible experiment.

01:26:59.744 --> 01:27:06.844
I'm... we're this year celebrating its
250th birthday. I can't wait for the next

01:27:07.004 --> 01:27:12.784
250 years. This is such a fascinating
study of human civilization.

01:27:12.884 --> 01:27:20.204
- And it is complicated. What we want now
is a sort of cartoon version of what we

01:27:20.244 --> 01:27:23.814
Americans, as you watch
popular culture, we want a

01:27:23.824 --> 01:27:27.764
30-second view of whatever. Well, who should I
like? Who are the good guys? Who are the bad

01:27:27.844 --> 01:27:31.084
guys? I want a 30-second version
of the Civil War. What do I...

01:27:31.724 --> 01:27:34.844
History is not that way.
I've spent my whole life

01:27:35.664 --> 01:27:39.604
studying history, and the maybe the main
thing I've learned is however complicated

01:27:39.804 --> 01:27:43.664
I thought something was when I really started
to explore it, it's more complicated than

01:27:43.744 --> 01:27:48.254
I thought it was. People aren't simple.
They're complicated. And you have to

01:27:50.364 --> 01:27:51.044
at least

01:27:52.264 --> 01:27:56.244
appreciate, you don't have to embrace the
complexity. I guess you can't make people do that,

01:27:56.264 --> 01:28:00.034
but you have to at least concede that
things are... they're just... There's not a

01:28:00.184 --> 01:28:04.014
20-second version of anything in
the past. The past is complicated,

01:28:04.244 --> 01:28:06.804
because humanoids are involved
in it. They're complicated.

01:28:07.024 --> 01:28:10.924
- Yeah, the... when you look at the system
as a whole. But as individual humans, we

01:28:10.984 --> 01:28:13.454
like to do the black
and white thing where-

01:28:13.864 --> 01:28:14.234
- Of course.

01:28:14.234 --> 01:28:16.193
- ... we just see the good, everything
as good and evil, and then we bicker.

01:28:16.193 --> 01:28:16.944
- 'Cause it's easier.

01:28:17.284 --> 01:28:21.184
- And then we get together at Thanksgiving dinner,
and we yell at each other, and then people lose

01:28:21.204 --> 01:28:25.064
their shit, and they stop talking to
each other for a year, and then they,

01:28:25.164 --> 01:28:28.184
they make up and all this 'cause this is
just how humans interact with each other.

01:28:28.804 --> 01:28:32.444
And, but as a system, especially
with the increasing levels of

01:28:32.524 --> 01:28:36.124
freedom, with the freedoms of
speech and be able to speak out

01:28:36.704 --> 01:28:40.444
about your feelings, even when they're
intense feelings, that somehow works as a

01:28:40.544 --> 01:28:40.864
system.

01:28:40.964 --> 01:28:42.004
- And it's very messy.

01:28:42.524 --> 01:28:42.764
- Yeah, yeah.

01:28:42.784 --> 01:28:46.264
- The more freedom, the messier it
is. Really, the messier it is.

01:28:46.284 --> 01:28:47.854
- And your job as a historian

01:28:49.054 --> 01:28:52.924
is a difficult job, and as you
said, because the more you learn,

01:28:53.304 --> 01:28:56.624
the more you come to understand, the
more complicated the picture appears.

01:28:56.844 --> 01:29:00.704
- Yep, and the more you learn, the more you
learn that you don't know as much as you

01:29:00.744 --> 01:29:01.344
thought you did.

01:29:02.024 --> 01:29:05.064
- So some humility will take you a long way.

01:29:06.383 --> 01:29:07.773
- Yes, you want more of Grant,

01:29:08.724 --> 01:29:12.604
than McClellan in that, on that
humility score. Grant would

01:29:12.644 --> 01:29:14.184
score high on humility. McClellan,

01:29:15.204 --> 01:29:18.403
every day, every day the first
thought was, "What about me?"

01:29:19.894 --> 01:29:23.764
"Who is gonna not appreciate how wonderful
I am today?" thought George B. McClellan.

01:29:23.884 --> 01:29:27.864
That's the thought that nagged at him.
"Somebody might not know how great I am."

01:29:28.294 --> 01:29:29.064
That's an aside.

01:29:29.204 --> 01:29:31.664
- Reminds me of somebody. But anyway.

01:29:32.904 --> 01:29:36.564
Quick ten-second shout-out to our sponsors.
Check them out in the description.

01:29:36.904 --> 01:29:39.744
It really is the best way to
support this podcast. Go to

01:29:39.784 --> 01:29:43.404
lexfridman.com/sponsors. And now, dear

01:29:43.424 --> 01:29:46.944
friends, back to my conversation
with Gary Gallagher.

01:29:48.384 --> 01:29:54.324
Okay, let's at the start, first shots
fired at Fort Sumter, as you mentioned,

01:29:55.504 --> 01:30:00.374
and this thing starts brewing and
escalating towards a full-on war.

01:30:01.924 --> 01:30:06.644
What are the different advantages of
each side from a military perspective?

01:30:07.044 --> 01:30:11.004
- Here is one of the great misconceptions about
the Civil War. Among the people who know

01:30:11.044 --> 01:30:13.824
anything about it, which in the
US is a very small percentage,

01:30:14.684 --> 01:30:19.084
but if they know anything, they know
United States' victory was inevitable.

01:30:19.134 --> 01:30:22.754
Because the United States
had 22 million people.

01:30:22.784 --> 01:30:25.594
The Confederacy had five and
a half million white people-

01:30:25.594 --> 01:30:28.464
... and three and a half million
enslaved people. The United States'

01:30:28.704 --> 01:30:32.464
economy was infinitely
more powerful than the

01:30:32.504 --> 01:30:36.324
Confederate economy. The United States
had the United States Army. The United

01:30:36.404 --> 01:30:38.524
States had the United States Navy.

01:30:39.144 --> 01:30:42.944
Really, who's gonna win this war?
The US, it's inevitable, next

01:30:42.984 --> 01:30:46.804
question. It's not true at all. The
US did have those advantages, but the

01:30:46.844 --> 01:30:50.796
Confederacy had compensating
advantages. And

01:30:50.856 --> 01:30:54.056
one of those advantages was
it had a much lower bar to

01:30:54.116 --> 01:30:57.846
cross for success. It didn't have
to invade the United States,

01:30:58.076 --> 01:31:02.016
didn't have to capture Washington. It
didn't have to project its military

01:31:02.096 --> 01:31:06.086
power into New England. All it
had to do was convince a majority

01:31:06.696 --> 01:31:10.156
of the civilian population
of the loyal states that it

01:31:10.176 --> 01:31:14.116
wasn't worth the cost to make
these rebel states come back into

01:31:14.156 --> 01:31:18.056
the Union. These are two democratic
republics at war, and in

01:31:18.096 --> 01:31:21.876
the end, the civilian sector
decides. Somebody my age

01:31:22.396 --> 01:31:26.266
can... We have the perfect example,
Vietnam. The United States was

01:31:26.296 --> 01:31:29.476
not defeated militarily in
Vietnam, and it didn't matter.

01:31:29.516 --> 01:31:33.416
'Cause when the people said, "We're not gonna
support this war anymore," the war's gonna come to

01:31:33.456 --> 01:31:37.236
an end. The smart people in the
Confederacy understood this. They

01:31:37.296 --> 01:31:40.796
understood it at the time. Lee understood
it. Jefferson Davis understood it.

01:31:41.516 --> 01:31:45.136
You had key people at the time
saying, "There's no way that a

01:31:45.216 --> 01:31:49.116
nation as large as ours and a population

01:31:49.136 --> 01:31:52.136
as large as ours has ever been
subjected in a war like this.

01:31:53.216 --> 01:31:57.036
A tie is as good as a win for the
Confederacy." And they came very close to

01:31:57.096 --> 01:32:01.016
that in the summer of 1864. The
other thing is the sheer size

01:32:01.076 --> 01:32:04.996
of the Confederacy is a huge
advantage to them. It's huge, with

01:32:05.076 --> 01:32:08.346
a very poor transportation and

01:32:08.416 --> 01:32:12.376
communication infrastructure in
many ways. And to move a large army

01:32:12.496 --> 01:32:16.256
deep into the Confederacy is
a huge logistical problem.

01:32:16.296 --> 01:32:19.886
The same thing had happened to the British both
during the American Revolution and the War of

01:32:19.956 --> 01:32:25.535
1812. They confront this... The Confederacy's
as big as all of Western Europe.

01:32:27.296 --> 01:32:30.696
That's a problem. It's also a problem
that the Confederates are fighting,

01:32:30.916 --> 01:32:34.036
literally fighting to defend their
homes. That's always, almost

01:32:34.156 --> 01:32:37.996
always a stronger factor
than trying to impose your

01:32:38.076 --> 01:32:41.726
will on somebody else. All of these
factors help the Confederacy.

01:32:42.586 --> 01:32:46.416
The US did have an army. The
army had 15,000 men in it in

01:32:46.436 --> 01:32:51.016
1860, 15,000. The Navy had
fewer than 50 vessels,

01:32:52.176 --> 01:32:55.596
most of them on overseas
stations. There were exactly

01:32:55.636 --> 01:32:59.616
three vessels available to blockade
the Confederacy at the beginning

01:32:59.656 --> 01:33:03.516
of the war. The Confederate coastline's
3,600 miles long. Probably not gonna be

01:33:03.536 --> 01:33:08.396
able to shut down a lot there.
So the point is that there are

01:33:09.596 --> 01:33:13.436
factors that help both sides. To me, the

01:33:13.736 --> 01:33:17.136
conditions for victory are the most important
factor that favors the Confederacy.

01:33:17.196 --> 01:33:21.736
We just have to make it painful
enough to the United States

01:33:22.836 --> 01:33:26.796
that they're gonna say, "Let them go. We
never liked South Carolina anyway. Just

01:33:26.856 --> 01:33:30.776
let them go. We're not... It's not worth it. It's
killing too many of our boys. It's costing too

01:33:30.816 --> 01:33:34.236
much money, and we don't have to worry about
slavery in the territories if they're gone,

01:33:34.706 --> 01:33:38.256
'cause they'll be gone. And our
territories will be free of slavery."

01:33:38.776 --> 01:33:41.776
- So again, the Union is the invading force.

01:33:41.836 --> 01:33:42.056
- Yes.

01:33:42.176 --> 01:33:44.256
- Geography comes into
play, as you mentioned.

01:33:44.316 --> 01:33:45.316
- Immediately, yes.

01:33:45.336 --> 01:33:48.676
- Vast land. And so in some
sense, it's a miracle

01:33:48.756 --> 01:33:54.286
that the war lasted as long as it did,
and then there was as much motivation-

01:33:54.476 --> 01:33:55.596
- Yes, that they stuck to it

01:33:55.756 --> 01:33:59.196
- ... because again, victory for
the Confederacy was stop the war.

01:33:59.436 --> 01:34:02.816
- Stop the war. Neither side has
an advantage in terms of its

01:34:02.856 --> 01:34:06.516
leadership. The generals
all come... You often

01:34:06.696 --> 01:34:10.646
used to read frequently Confederates had
better generals than the... And, but

01:34:11.436 --> 01:34:15.046
this is the first war where West-- where
West Pointers are in charge. They were

01:34:15.076 --> 01:34:20.896
subalterns in Mexico. The West Point is the
United States Military Academy where the

01:34:20.916 --> 01:34:24.736
professional officer corps gets its training.
It was established under Jefferson.

01:34:24.796 --> 01:34:28.456
It's the premier engineering school in
the United States. And I mean, West

01:34:28.536 --> 01:34:32.266
Point-trained engineers are very, very
prominent when they get out of the

01:34:32.356 --> 01:34:36.036
army and even when they're in the
army. But by the time of the Civil

01:34:36.156 --> 01:34:39.696
War, it is West Pointers
who were lieutenants and

01:34:39.856 --> 01:34:43.616
captains in the war with Mexico
are now gonna be the people in

01:34:43.656 --> 01:34:47.576
charge. Almost every army commander,
almost every corps commander, which

01:34:47.636 --> 01:34:48.926
is the next level,

01:34:49.816 --> 01:34:53.596
are West Point graduates. They took
the same classes from the same

01:34:53.636 --> 01:34:57.456
professors, read the same textbooks,
served under either Winfield

01:34:57.516 --> 01:35:01.456
Scott or Zachary Taylor in Mexico.
They bring the same background

01:35:01.496 --> 01:35:04.016
and body of doctrine and
training to the table.

01:35:05.236 --> 01:35:09.016
It would've been weird if one
side or the other had an inherent

01:35:09.116 --> 01:35:12.956
advantage in leadership. The one
place where the US ends up with an

01:35:13.016 --> 01:35:16.896
advantage is that over the course
of the war, it found, in my

01:35:16.976 --> 01:35:20.816
view, four men who could command
armies effectively: Grant,

01:35:20.876 --> 01:35:24.696
Sherman, Philip Henry Sheridan, and
George H. Thomas. The Confederacy

01:35:24.736 --> 01:35:28.696
only ever found one, and that's
Lee. And they had more than one

01:35:28.796 --> 01:35:32.436
army, so that's a problem. If you have more
than one army but only one really good army

01:35:32.516 --> 01:35:34.836
commander, that puts
you at a disadvantage.

01:35:35.396 --> 01:35:38.825
- In what ways were the two
sides balanced militarily,

01:35:40.016 --> 01:35:43.966
Equal militarily? So the, the
passion, like we mentioned,

01:35:44.056 --> 01:35:49.116
of the fighting force, the soldiers,
the dedication to the fighting.

01:35:49.696 --> 01:35:53.516
- Both brought the same notions of how to
fight a war to the table because the

01:35:53.596 --> 01:35:57.536
officer corps had been, on both
sides, had been trained in the

01:35:57.596 --> 01:36:01.016
same way. Neither side has
a real advantage in terms

01:36:01.236 --> 01:36:04.936
of technology. Both sides
use the railroad. Both

01:36:04.996 --> 01:36:08.816
sides use the telegraph. The United States
could have used repeating weapons but

01:36:08.856 --> 01:36:12.636
decided not to. The Confederacy didn't
have the industrial capacity to

01:36:12.716 --> 01:36:16.496
produce. So the war is fought by
infantrymen, for the most part,

01:36:16.716 --> 01:36:20.346
carrying muzzle-loading single-shot
muskets, rifle muskets.

01:36:20.516 --> 01:36:25.146
That's the weapon that inflicts
85 to 90% of the casualties-

01:36:25.146 --> 01:36:28.896
... in the Civil War, and they both
have exactly the same thing. And they

01:36:28.976 --> 01:36:32.546
have the same kinds of artillery
pieces. The United States

01:36:32.636 --> 01:36:36.476
Navy's much bigger, but it's...
And the Navy is important,

01:36:37.016 --> 01:36:40.456
but armies really decide the war,
and the armies are... They're

01:36:40.736 --> 01:36:42.486
organized exactly the same way.

01:36:43.420 --> 01:36:47.620
They are-- They get officers the
same way. They're very similar.

01:36:47.680 --> 01:36:51.610
- So what was the actual fighting like?
Like you mentioned, most of it was with

01:36:51.680 --> 01:36:54.080
muskets. What was the
experience of it like?

01:36:54.420 --> 01:36:58.260
- It's linear formations,
shoulder-to-shoulder kinds of formations

01:36:58.300 --> 01:37:01.980
that would allow you to
maintain unit integrity on a

01:37:02.020 --> 01:37:05.740
battlefield, which is one reason
casualties were high. They'd line up and

01:37:06.040 --> 01:37:07.000
fight each other.

01:37:07.190 --> 01:37:11.060
As the war went on, they resorted more
and more. One of the developments

01:37:11.140 --> 01:37:14.440
was the field entrenchments
became very common by

01:37:14.460 --> 01:37:18.320
1864. And both armies, if they would
stop for the night, they would entrench.

01:37:18.360 --> 01:37:19.550
They'd throw up field works--

01:37:20.180 --> 01:37:24.110
... which gave the defenders a
much greater power vis-a-vis

01:37:24.160 --> 01:37:27.700
the attacking force. They came to
decide that you needed about a

01:37:27.740 --> 01:37:31.260
three-to-one advantage in numbers to
have a successful attack once field

01:37:31.320 --> 01:37:36.900
entrenchments were in place. But
it is... It's an infantryman's war

01:37:37.620 --> 01:37:43.100
overwhelmingly, and the infantryman's
experience on both sides. The United States

01:37:43.140 --> 01:37:46.980
side was a little better supplied.
Union soldiers ate a little

01:37:47.020 --> 01:37:50.900
better for the most part.
They got regular issues

01:37:50.940 --> 01:37:54.920
of clothing a little more regularly than the
Confederates did. Their uniforms all kind of looked

01:37:54.940 --> 01:37:57.720
the same. The Confederates would
have had a more motley look.

01:37:58.760 --> 01:38:02.480
But the Confederates didn't lose any
battles because they didn't have enough

01:38:02.540 --> 01:38:06.520
shoes, and they didn't lose any battles 'cause
they didn't have enough powder. They never had

01:38:06.560 --> 01:38:10.360
a problem with that. So it's
a war where each side looks

01:38:10.400 --> 01:38:14.160
like the other side in terms of what the
armies actually... If we had wandered

01:38:14.170 --> 01:38:17.760
through the armies and, or watched
them in battle, they would have looked

01:38:18.360 --> 01:38:19.140
the same to us.

01:38:19.380 --> 01:38:23.060
- But it wasn't just about bullets.
There was also dysentery and disease.

01:38:23.120 --> 01:38:26.760
- Two out of every three soldiers
who died, died of disease.

01:38:27.860 --> 01:38:31.840
It depends on what unit you're in. If you're
in the Irish Brigade, ah, God love them.

01:38:32.600 --> 01:38:36.000
You know, just point us towards
something and let us indulge in

01:38:36.060 --> 01:38:39.800
self-immolation, for God's
sake. The Irish Brigade had the

01:38:39.840 --> 01:38:43.580
reverse. They had two men who died
from battle wounds for every man who

01:38:43.660 --> 01:38:49.050
died from disease. But overall, it's
one man died from battle wounds

01:38:49.720 --> 01:38:54.290
and two died from disease. Overall
casualties, killed, wounded,

01:38:55.160 --> 01:38:59.260
prisoners of war, about a million and a
half-- ... casualties in the Civil War.

01:38:59.700 --> 01:39:03.640
- There-- You know, there's this idea that
a big part of the American spirit is the

01:39:03.660 --> 01:39:08.400
Scots-Irish spirit, the fighting spirit.
You think there's some truth to that?

01:39:08.440 --> 01:39:12.029
- Well, the songs are Irish songs that
both sides sing with different words,

01:39:12.100 --> 01:39:16.060
and I mean, there are a lot of
Irish soldiers in the Union Army.

01:39:17.020 --> 01:39:20.960
But in the entire military-age
white population of

01:39:21.020 --> 01:39:24.520
the loyal states in
1860, just about a third

01:39:25.120 --> 01:39:27.280
were born somewhere other
than the United States.

01:39:27.880 --> 01:39:31.820
And they made up about a quarter of
all the soldiers. So they're actually

01:39:31.860 --> 01:39:35.840
underrepresented in the United States
Army. Far lower percentage of foreign-born

01:39:35.900 --> 01:39:39.600
soldiers in the Confederate Army, but there
they're a little bit overrepresented.

01:39:40.320 --> 01:39:43.960
So there are lots of foreign-born
soldiers in the army. The

01:39:44.000 --> 01:39:47.680
largest group are not Irish. They're the second
largest group. The largest group are Germans,

01:39:48.200 --> 01:39:52.060
On the US side. The Irish are second,
and then you'd go way down the

01:39:52.120 --> 01:39:53.060
line for yet others.

01:39:53.260 --> 01:39:57.100
- So we talked pretty thoroughly about
slavery. Can we talk about immigration?

01:39:57.720 --> 01:40:02.780
What was the view at
the time of immigrants?

01:40:03.740 --> 01:40:03.960
- Well,

01:40:04.780 --> 01:40:08.680
I think you know the answer to that. We
need to have all that stuff done, but we

01:40:08.700 --> 01:40:13.390
don't especially like the people doing
it. That's basically the... Well,

01:40:14.300 --> 01:40:14.570
I mean, the--

01:40:14.600 --> 01:40:15.249
- Oh, America

01:40:15.460 --> 01:40:19.340
- ...the anti-Irish, and a lot of
it is bound up with anti-Catholic

01:40:19.500 --> 01:40:23.370
sentiment. The-- 'Cause the Irish are
changing the nature of the country.

01:40:23.440 --> 01:40:24.640
The country's a Protestant,

01:40:25.749 --> 01:40:29.650
British country, and here come all
these Irishmen. And especially in

01:40:29.680 --> 01:40:33.050
the '40s and '50s, the-- My
Gallaghers came in the late '50s.

01:40:33.820 --> 01:40:37.760
They are changing the nature, and they
take over. Look at them. They don't know

01:40:37.800 --> 01:40:38.660
their place. They're--

01:40:38.870 --> 01:40:42.860
... pretty soon they're taking over cities, and
they all cheat when they vote. We know that.

01:40:42.900 --> 01:40:44.840
You can buy an Irish vote for one drink.

01:40:44.960 --> 01:40:46.280
- This all sounds familiar, right?

01:40:47.040 --> 01:40:50.960
- The cartoons were brutal on depicting
African American characters, but they also

01:40:51.000 --> 01:40:54.600
depicted the Irish as sort of simian
characters, often with a jug of

01:40:54.660 --> 01:40:58.500
whiskey in one hand. I mean, it's
very... There's profound prejudice. And

01:40:58.560 --> 01:41:02.460
prejudice against the Germans, whom
they called the Dutch. That was what

01:41:02.700 --> 01:41:06.660
the soldiers would call the German
regiments, Dutch regiments. And

01:41:07.560 --> 01:41:09.580
yeah, there's a lot of ethnic prejudice.

01:41:09.880 --> 01:41:13.660
- So to mention, you mentioned
Protestants, so both sides were

01:41:13.700 --> 01:41:15.940
primarily Protestant Christians.

01:41:16.120 --> 01:41:16.920
- Overwhelmingly.

01:41:17.200 --> 01:41:19.880
- At that time, it was a
very religious country.

01:41:20.520 --> 01:41:20.530
- Yes, yes.

01:41:20.560 --> 01:41:22.500
- So the religious sentiment
was pretty strong.

01:41:23.020 --> 01:41:26.730
- Units had chaplains, and there
were Catholic priests with

01:41:26.820 --> 01:41:28.900
Catholic units. Yes, a very big presence.

01:41:29.240 --> 01:41:31.460
- Was Christianity used to justify--

01:41:31.480 --> 01:41:33.480
- Of course it was. Both sides used it, yes.

01:41:34.320 --> 01:41:36.440
I don't mean to jump on your
question, but yes, they did.

01:41:36.760 --> 01:41:41.420
- I mean, how was it used to
justify fighting for a nation?

01:41:42.080 --> 01:41:46.040
- Yeah, there's a lot of fighting in the Bible,
a lot of people killing other people in

01:41:46.060 --> 01:41:47.930
the Bible-- As long as you're
doing it for the right reasons--

01:41:48.340 --> 01:41:48.420
- Right.

01:41:48.600 --> 01:41:52.360
- ...you can... And you could use it
different ways. I mean, you can deal with

01:41:52.500 --> 01:41:56.440
setbacks by saying, "Well, God tests
us. We're being tested." What do you

01:41:56.480 --> 01:42:00.419
do when God tests you? You don't give up when God
tests you. You try harder when God tests you.

01:42:01.220 --> 01:42:04.910
The very top leaders... This is
interesting. The top leaders

01:42:05.140 --> 01:42:11.180
on the Confederate side, the most famous
ones, Lee, Stonewall Jackson, Jeb Stuart,

01:42:12.320 --> 01:42:16.220
they were much more overtly
religious than people like

01:42:16.280 --> 01:42:21.140
Grant, Sherman, Philip Sheridan.
I mean, much, much more. Lee,

01:42:22.320 --> 01:42:27.920
was an Episcopalian. Jackson was a
Presbyterian. But they talked-- I mean, their

01:42:28.240 --> 01:42:32.160
official reports, their correspondence,
it's filled with allusions to

01:42:32.220 --> 01:42:35.120
religion much more than
Grant's or Sherman's.

01:42:35.540 --> 01:42:38.260
- What about African Americans in the army?

01:42:38.940 --> 01:42:42.419
- African Americans made up... There
were about 200,000 Black men

01:42:42.760 --> 01:42:46.510
served in what were called United
States Colored Troops units, USCT.

01:42:46.780 --> 01:42:47.860
- It was on the Union side.

01:42:48.100 --> 01:42:51.820
- On the U- Yes. Okay, let's
just dispense with the

01:42:51.900 --> 01:42:55.780
hallucination that there were a lot
of Black men in the Confederate Army.

01:42:55.840 --> 01:43:00.812
That is such total- Ridiculous nonsense-

01:43:00.822 --> 01:43:05.492
... utterly unsupported by any evidence
that anyone would consider credible.

01:43:06.652 --> 01:43:10.272
This whole notion of... I mean,
it's just, it's preposterous.

01:43:10.572 --> 01:43:16.252
It's ridic- and I'm really
managing my language right here.

01:43:16.292 --> 01:43:20.192
I'm not saying what I really
think it is. So, so just... I've

01:43:20.252 --> 01:43:24.402
seen estimates of as high as 100,000
Black men in the Confederate Army.

01:43:24.472 --> 01:43:24.812
- Not true.

01:43:25.012 --> 01:43:26.702
- Really, really, no.

01:43:26.712 --> 01:43:30.512
No. Were there Black men with the
Confederate armies? Yes. There are enslaved

01:43:30.532 --> 01:43:34.212
Black men who are doing all kinds
of non-military things in the army

01:43:34.832 --> 01:43:38.392
to free more white men to shoulder
muskets and shoot at Yankees.

01:43:38.452 --> 01:43:42.252
That's what's going on in the Confederate
Army. There are a lot of Black men around

01:43:42.372 --> 01:43:47.192
Union armies before they became soldiers
as well. But there are these...

01:43:48.712 --> 01:43:52.462
200,000 Black men join the military service
during the war. They're in the Navy and in the

01:43:52.572 --> 01:43:56.502
Army. They'd always been in the
Navy. The US Navy... And they're

01:43:56.502 --> 01:44:00.412
not segregated in the Navy in the, in the way
that they can be in the Army. They're just in

01:44:00.472 --> 01:44:02.362
Bl- you have integrated crews-

01:44:02.362 --> 01:44:06.102
... on naval vessels, although they're separated
in some ways, but there've always been

01:44:06.152 --> 01:44:10.082
Black sailors in the Navy.
The Army, no. That was

01:44:10.132 --> 01:44:14.032
a big, it's a big thing to
put Black men in uniform. And

01:44:14.072 --> 01:44:17.982
for a Confederate, it's bad enough if
you announce emancipation, but you, then

01:44:17.992 --> 01:44:21.272
you start arming Black men.
That, to Confederates,

01:44:21.532 --> 01:44:27.332
is urging a widespread
slave rebellion, basically.

01:44:27.752 --> 01:44:32.372
- Black regiments on the Union side
was enraging the Confederate side.

01:44:32.592 --> 01:44:36.502
- Yes. And they have white officers
overwhelmingly. There are a few

01:44:36.532 --> 01:44:43.032
Black officers, but overwhelmingly the USCT
units have white officers leading them.

01:44:43.412 --> 01:44:45.382
- But symbolically, that is a
really powerful thing, right?

01:44:45.382 --> 01:44:49.612
- Oh, it's hard for modern
Americans to understand just how

01:44:50.692 --> 01:44:54.532
jarring that would be to a Confederate,
that not only would you have enslaved

01:44:54.592 --> 01:45:01.032
people no longer enslaved, but you would
have the men in uniforms carrying muskets.

01:45:01.652 --> 01:45:06.542
- So you as a historian, you've looked at
every layer of this, including diaries and-

01:45:06.792 --> 01:45:11.452
- What, what was motivating?
What was going through the

01:45:11.512 --> 01:45:15.152
minds of African Americans who
were fighting for the Union side?

01:45:15.672 --> 01:45:19.432
I think what you would expect is going... I mean, they
would talk about Union occasionally, but what that

01:45:19.472 --> 01:45:23.452
means to them, it's a union without
slavery. They have the most... Of

01:45:23.532 --> 01:45:26.112
all the people who can have a direct

01:45:27.692 --> 01:45:31.532
impact on what's going on versus slavery,
there's nothing more direct than picking up a

01:45:31.592 --> 01:45:35.112
musket and firing at the people
who are defending a slavery-based

01:45:35.152 --> 01:45:38.981
society. It doesn't get... And at the
same time, you are not only doing that,

01:45:39.452 --> 01:45:43.292
you are also establishing an
unimpeachable claim to citizenship. The

01:45:43.332 --> 01:45:47.252
whole point of a citizen soldier
is that you do your bit for the

01:45:47.292 --> 01:45:51.212
nation that's helping you, and the
nation does these things for you.

01:45:51.292 --> 01:45:55.272
Well, okay, if I'm picking up and risking my life
with a musket in my hand, are you gonna tell

01:45:55.312 --> 01:45:58.992
me I can't vote after I do? I thought
that's the whole point of this, when I'm

01:45:59.052 --> 01:46:02.972
protecting the right to vote by doing
my citizen's role as a soldier.

01:46:03.392 --> 01:46:05.312
And that was a big debate in Congress.

01:46:05.372 --> 01:46:09.012
That really changed the equation of that
debate as well. How are you gonna treat

01:46:09.112 --> 01:46:12.642
people after they've served
in United States units?

01:46:13.092 --> 01:46:15.412
Can you tell them they can't vote?
You're not gonna give the vote to them?

01:46:16.112 --> 01:46:20.092
- How many people in the Black regiments,
but also, you know, folks like Frederick

01:46:20.152 --> 01:46:25.232
Douglass, saw that Black Americans and
white Americans should have equal rights?

01:46:25.452 --> 01:46:29.392
- Well, Douglass saw that, of course. But
even within their context, their notion

01:46:29.432 --> 01:46:33.232
of equal rights is that that doesn't mean that
Black women are gonna vote, just like for white

01:46:33.272 --> 01:46:37.002
people, it didn't mean white women would
vote. But in terms of the context of the

01:46:37.092 --> 01:46:37.732
time, yes,

01:46:38.652 --> 01:46:42.212
equal rights should come out of this.
Abolishing slavery is the first step of this.

01:46:42.252 --> 01:46:46.012
It's not the end game. The end game
is equal rights for everybody.

01:46:46.112 --> 01:46:47.132
- Did many people see that?

01:46:47.332 --> 01:46:51.232
- No. A tiny percentage of people
saw that. There's a notion

01:46:51.292 --> 01:46:55.072
that's very common that somehow
the white North turned its back

01:46:55.132 --> 01:46:59.032
on Black people after the war, and it
should've tried harder during Reconstruction

01:46:59.072 --> 01:47:02.892
to force the white South to convey
full equal rights to Black people.

01:47:03.872 --> 01:47:07.612
The assumption there is simply
flawed. Most white people in the

01:47:07.632 --> 01:47:11.172
North didn't believe that... They believed

01:47:11.652 --> 01:47:15.592
first save the Union, and yes, we'll need
to kill slavery as well. Those are the two

01:47:15.671 --> 01:47:19.592
things on the list. Check, check, we've done
those. Full equal rights for Black people is

01:47:19.671 --> 01:47:23.512
not on their list. That... And they were not
willing to do the things that it would've taken to

01:47:23.552 --> 01:47:27.292
do that, including the white
soldiers in uniforms who

01:47:27.332 --> 01:47:31.152
wanted out of the army after the Confederates
surrendered and said, "Well, now we

01:47:31.192 --> 01:47:35.052
need the army to make the white South do
the right thing vis-à-vis Black people."

01:47:35.172 --> 01:47:36.872
They would've said, "What
are you talking about?"

01:47:37.332 --> 01:47:41.292
"I signed on to do this. We did
it. Send us home right now."

01:47:41.932 --> 01:47:45.612
- I think that's one of the lessons you learn
from human history across the different

01:47:45.912 --> 01:47:47.652
places where slavery was abolished,

01:47:48.552 --> 01:47:52.751
is abolishing slavery is not the end of a
journey. It's the beginning of a journey.

01:47:52.762 --> 01:47:53.202
- It's the beginning.

01:47:53.592 --> 01:47:57.252
- And it sometimes takes, quite
often takes at least 100 years

01:47:57.812 --> 01:48:01.592
to go from that to something you
would identify as equal rights.

01:48:02.292 --> 01:48:05.732
And then maybe even beyond that,
there's a complicated story about

01:48:06.732 --> 01:48:09.462
even outside of rights, just how
society sees the different people.

01:48:09.472 --> 01:48:13.452
- There's a difference between passing...
I mean, the Great Society passed most of

01:48:13.492 --> 01:48:16.752
the... I mean, you got the Civil Rights Act,
the Voting Rights Act, the Fair Housing Act.

01:48:16.792 --> 01:48:20.732
You got all of this legislation that does
a lot of the legal stuff, but it's what's

01:48:20.792 --> 01:48:27.512
in people's heads- ... that takes longer
and is a, is a more frustrating process.

01:48:27.972 --> 01:48:33.892
- Remains, as has appeared in
human history, potential fuel

01:48:34.032 --> 01:48:36.232
for the division to rise up again.

01:48:36.332 --> 01:48:40.152
- Of course. And then people can exploit
that. It's, it's waiting to be

01:48:40.212 --> 01:48:42.452
exploited, and people exploit it.

01:48:43.052 --> 01:48:46.612
- This is something we do as humans.
We like to otherize certain groups.

01:48:46.672 --> 01:48:47.472
- Good to have an enemy.

01:48:47.772 --> 01:48:51.392
- And the Civil War for our country
is a story of this. It's...

01:48:51.732 --> 01:48:55.602
So understanding the Civil War
is in part extremely important

01:48:55.652 --> 01:49:03.152
for understanding the, the division,
maybe the dormant division that's there.

01:49:03.372 --> 01:49:05.862
- Yes. And there, and even
before the war, there were-

01:49:07.684 --> 01:49:11.364
There were notions about the
other side that sort-- for,

01:49:11.504 --> 01:49:15.364
for a lot of people in the free states
looking South, they said white Southerners

01:49:15.444 --> 01:49:19.024
are because of they're lazy, they're

01:49:19.064 --> 01:49:23.044
violent, they, they don't wanna
talk out a problem. They'll do

01:49:23.084 --> 01:49:26.784
what Preston Brooks did to Charles Sumner.
They'll beat him over the head with a

01:49:26.844 --> 01:49:30.444
cane, and they're used to this because
they have slavery, and they're used to

01:49:30.504 --> 01:49:34.264
brutalizing Black people. That's just
how they approach things. And the white

01:49:34.324 --> 01:49:37.994
South would look North and say they don't care
anything about family. They're all just lusting

01:49:38.064 --> 01:49:42.034
after money. All they care about is
material things. They don't have any

01:49:42.084 --> 01:49:45.504
sense of kinship or family.
They're... That's what Yankees

01:49:45.604 --> 01:49:49.404
are. It doesn't even matter whether
any of that is true, of course.

01:49:49.564 --> 01:49:53.284
There was a sense perceptions are more
important than reality with human

01:49:53.364 --> 01:49:57.024
beings, and the perceptions on the eve
of the Civil War were that the other

01:49:57.064 --> 01:50:00.944
side was different in a lot of
ways. They also had profoundly

01:50:00.984 --> 01:50:04.464
profound commonalities. They
had a common language, a common

01:50:04.484 --> 01:50:08.164
religion, a common history,
these founding documents. All of

01:50:08.264 --> 01:50:11.504
that would pull things,
pull people together.

01:50:11.843 --> 01:50:15.764
But they found ways to see
differences and divided

01:50:15.824 --> 01:50:18.884
some of the churches. I mean, Protestant
churches divided over slavery.

01:50:19.544 --> 01:50:23.484
- And many of these themes
continue to today. Let's

01:50:23.504 --> 01:50:27.324
talk about the different theaters of the war,
the Eastern Theater and the Western Theater.

01:50:27.404 --> 01:50:31.164
You mentioned the navies, the geography. How
does geography come into play? What are the

01:50:31.224 --> 01:50:32.244
different parts here?

01:50:32.584 --> 01:50:36.544
- There's three main theaters of the war.
The Eastern Theater is the theater

01:50:36.554 --> 01:50:39.694
that features Washington and Richmond.

01:50:39.884 --> 01:50:43.314
Plays out in Virginia, a little bit of
Maryland, and a little bit of Pennsylvania.

01:50:43.464 --> 01:50:47.254
That's-- it's where the Army of Northern Virginia
and the Army of the Potomac fight for the most

01:50:47.264 --> 01:50:49.244
part. That's the Eastern Theater.

01:50:49.254 --> 01:50:54.344
- And that's the theater that maybe
is most glorified and mythologized.

01:50:54.404 --> 01:50:56.484
- It's by far the most
famous of the theaters.

01:50:56.544 --> 01:51:00.414
There are famous battles in the other theaters,
but that's the one, and it's mainly because of the

01:51:00.504 --> 01:51:02.244
presence of Lee and then of Grant.

01:51:03.044 --> 01:51:06.844
The Western Theater is a much larger one.
It goes from the Appalachian Mountains

01:51:06.984 --> 01:51:10.464
to the Mississippi River,
east to west, and would go

01:51:10.624 --> 01:51:18.484
from the Gulf to the Ohio River, so it's
huge and involves Tennessee and, and then

01:51:18.544 --> 01:51:22.284
beyond the Mississippi River
is the descriptively named

01:51:22.324 --> 01:51:29.304
Trans-Mississippi River, part of Louisiana,
Arkansas, Texas, and so forth. In terms

01:51:29.484 --> 01:51:33.164
of commitment of resources and
effect on the outcome of the war,

01:51:33.324 --> 01:51:37.044
it's by far the least important. Some
people get very upset when I say that,

01:51:37.224 --> 01:51:40.904
say that, that the Trans-- you know, it's basically
who cares about what's going on out there.

01:51:41.864 --> 01:51:45.584
But that's the third theater, the
Trans-Mississippi, the Western Theater,

01:51:46.084 --> 01:51:46.984
and the Eastern Theater.

01:51:47.504 --> 01:51:51.104
- But I think you've argued that the Western
Theater is really important for the war.

01:51:51.344 --> 01:51:55.224
- Very important, and it's
where we get Grant. The

01:51:55.284 --> 01:51:57.844
soldiers who won the war
for the United States

01:51:58.584 --> 01:52:02.024
all come out of the Western
Theater. Grant, he brings

01:52:02.244 --> 01:52:06.804
Sherman, Philip Sheridan, George Thomas.
Those are all in the Western Theater.

01:52:07.644 --> 01:52:14.444
- So at the very beginning, the first real
battle, at the First Manassas in July

01:52:14.484 --> 01:52:17.444
1861, I think one
interesting aspect of that,

01:52:18.344 --> 01:52:20.864
as you've talked about, is that
people gathered for a picnic.

01:52:21.394 --> 01:52:24.934
- They came out to watch from Washington.
They brought the carriages out,

01:52:25.424 --> 01:52:28.804
set up on a little ridge that let
them look down over the battle.

01:52:29.124 --> 01:52:30.774
- So that kind of gives you a sense

01:52:31.604 --> 01:52:35.544
that people of the time, first of all,
didn't think this would-- this is going

01:52:35.584 --> 01:52:39.224
to scale to the size that it
did or last as long as it did,

01:52:39.624 --> 01:52:43.584
but also not understand the
seriousness of the situation.

01:52:44.004 --> 01:52:47.594
- They're clueless about what's actually
going on. To even think that you should go

01:52:47.744 --> 01:52:51.724
observe a battle like that
is ridiculous, but they did.

01:52:51.754 --> 01:52:55.674
And there was a strong belief,
and Lincoln was part of

01:52:55.724 --> 01:52:58.344
this at the beginning, that there's probably
gonna be one battle. It's not gonna last.

01:52:58.384 --> 01:53:05.584
He asked for 90-day volunteers to suppress
the rebellion right after Fort Sumter.

01:53:05.924 --> 01:53:09.784
William Tecumseh Sherman reacted to that
by saying that you might as well try to

01:53:09.824 --> 01:53:13.604
put out a fire with a squirt gun. I mean,
it's ridiculous. They're not thinking

01:53:13.684 --> 01:53:19.144
about what this war could easily
be. Take years, take hundreds of

01:53:19.184 --> 01:53:21.864
thousands of men maybe, not one battle.

01:53:22.184 --> 01:53:27.363
- When did it, the dark cloud, the
reality of the war, come in?

01:53:28.244 --> 01:53:31.864
- Well, First Bull Run and
Star or Manassas, th- each

01:53:31.904 --> 01:53:35.824
side, many battles have two names, one
that the United States soldiers used

01:53:35.864 --> 01:53:40.084
and one... And usually the Confederates
would pick the nearest town,

01:53:40.904 --> 01:53:44.804
and the United States often picked
terrain features, so you get Bull

01:53:44.884 --> 01:53:48.244
Run, which is a creek or stream. That's
what the Union soldiers called it.

01:53:48.304 --> 01:53:51.904
Manassas is what the Confederates
called it. Antietam,

01:53:52.864 --> 01:53:56.664
a creek in Maryland, is what the Union.
Sharpsburg is what Confederates called

01:53:56.704 --> 01:53:58.204
it. Anyway, there are several of those.

01:53:59.224 --> 01:54:03.024
That brought a little sense
of reality to people in the

01:54:03.044 --> 01:54:06.964
United States especially. They'd expected it to
be over, and it didn't go the way they thought

01:54:07.004 --> 01:54:10.504
it would go, and so it's gonna
take more men. The summer of

01:54:10.514 --> 01:54:14.084
'61 was spent, hundreds of thousands of
men went into uniform in the summer of

01:54:14.124 --> 01:54:19.484
'61. The whole supply situation
became more evidently imperative,

01:54:20.884 --> 01:54:22.144
but not much happened

01:54:23.244 --> 01:54:27.204
in the, for the rest of the war. There's a pretty
big battle called the Battle of Wilson's Creek

01:54:27.244 --> 01:54:31.104
that takes place in Missouri, but
the rest of 1861 doesn't have... The

01:54:31.184 --> 01:54:33.294
real fighting begins in the spring of '62,

01:54:34.324 --> 01:54:37.884
and what really brought home
in a way that no one had

01:54:37.924 --> 01:54:41.884
imagined what could be in store was one
battle in the East and one battle in the

01:54:41.924 --> 01:54:44.933
West. The one in the West is Shiloh,
which happened in early April.

01:54:45.884 --> 01:54:49.664
U.S. Grant on the, on the United
States side and a General Albert

01:54:49.744 --> 01:54:53.534
Sidney Johnston on the Confederate
side. But at Shiloh in two days,

01:54:54.264 --> 01:54:58.004
there were upwards of 25,000
casualties, and that's more,

01:54:58.464 --> 01:55:02.104
more battle casualties than U.S.
forces had suffered in every

01:55:02.164 --> 01:55:05.274
war combined down to that
point happened in two days.

01:55:07.364 --> 01:55:10.648
Two and a half months after
that—George B. McClellan's army

01:55:11.228 --> 01:55:15.048
engaged with Lee's army in what came to be
called the Seven Days Battles at Richmond,

01:55:15.328 --> 01:55:19.128
36,000 casualties. So
those two together just

01:55:19.168 --> 01:55:22.788
showed that this was gonna be
on a totally different scale,

01:55:23.908 --> 01:55:27.328
and it was gonna involve
sacrifices and allocation of

01:55:27.388 --> 01:55:30.308
resources that no one had imagined before.

01:55:30.368 --> 01:55:33.968
- Since you mentioned Missouri, you've talked
about how that was the most brutal guerrilla

01:55:34.048 --> 01:55:35.588
warfare of the conflict.

01:55:35.988 --> 01:55:39.968
And maybe can you speak to what do you mean
by guerrilla warfare? What kind of war

01:55:40.028 --> 01:55:43.488
was that? And then contrast
with the structure,

01:55:44.168 --> 01:55:48.288
the dynamics of battles
elsewhere in the war.

01:55:48.408 --> 01:55:52.068
- Yes. Guerrilla war is more of what
I think most Americans can sort of

01:55:52.108 --> 01:55:55.398
imagine what guerrilla war is.
It involves much, much smaller

01:55:55.468 --> 01:55:59.238
numbers of actors, obviously, on

01:55:59.268 --> 01:56:03.228
both sides. Guerrilla bands might have
a few dozen members. They might have

01:56:03.288 --> 01:56:07.268
a few hundred at different times.
There are no real battles,

01:56:07.808 --> 01:56:11.608
battles between guerrillas.
There is a lot of

01:56:11.648 --> 01:56:17.048
bushwhacking, which is what they called it,
ambushing people, terrorizing civilians,

01:56:18.028 --> 01:56:23.678
killing livestock, killing some
civilians, killing prisoners after you've

01:56:23.708 --> 01:56:27.538
captured them, hanging some
people. There are a few semi-large

01:56:27.628 --> 01:56:31.608
scale ones. There's a Confederate
guerrilla named Quantrill led a raid

01:56:31.618 --> 01:56:35.278
against Lawrence, Kansas, where
they burned parts of the town,

01:56:35.488 --> 01:56:38.868
executed more than 150 men in the
town, and terrorized the place.

01:56:39.868 --> 01:56:43.628
That is going on in the... There's a

01:56:44.108 --> 01:56:48.008
relationship in many places between
elevation and guerrilla activity. The

01:56:48.068 --> 01:56:51.988
more mountainous or hilly, the more likely
there'll be guerrillas operating. They operate

01:56:52.028 --> 01:56:57.208
in the mountains, East Tennessee, western
North Carolina, southwestern Virginia,

01:56:58.288 --> 01:57:02.188
Central... There's even some activity
in parts of Texas, but Missouri and

01:57:02.208 --> 01:57:05.728
Kentucky have the worst guerrilla warfare.
It's present through the whole war,

01:57:06.488 --> 01:57:09.748
probably involves maybe
50,000 men out of the

01:57:10.508 --> 01:57:12.668
3 million who were in
action during the war,

01:57:13.468 --> 01:57:20.817
and was very nasty, no rules. One
reason that rules of war were codified

01:57:21.308 --> 01:57:25.288
was because Henry Halleck, who was a
Union general in Missouri, was dealing

01:57:25.328 --> 01:57:29.258
with this guerrilla activity and he said, "We need
to get some rules about who's a civilian, who's

01:57:29.348 --> 01:57:33.188
not a civilian. How do you deal with
this kind of situation and that

01:57:33.288 --> 01:57:34.268
kind of situation?"

01:57:34.668 --> 01:57:37.888
- What should we take away from the
fact that most of the fighting

01:57:38.728 --> 01:57:42.118
had the rules of war behind it? So
it was more structured, it was-

01:57:42.208 --> 01:57:43.748
- I think what we take away from that

01:57:44.828 --> 01:57:48.148
supports my argument that
this is a war between two

01:57:48.228 --> 01:57:52.088
mid-19th century nation states that
have a sense of how you fight a

01:57:52.168 --> 01:57:56.068
war. You fight a war with
national armies who are led by

01:57:56.108 --> 01:57:59.868
professional soldiers and who understand
that there are limits to what you

01:57:59.908 --> 01:58:03.748
do in a war, and all the war, the
war's limits expand as it goes on,

01:58:04.448 --> 01:58:08.318
but it doesn't reach the point where you
have indiscriminate killing of civilians and

01:58:08.408 --> 01:58:11.948
so forth. You just don't... I mean, there's an
argument about whether it's a total war or not.

01:58:12.008 --> 01:58:13.948
It all depends on how
you define total war.

01:58:14.168 --> 01:58:18.048
- As you've mentioned, in terms of
how encompassing it was, as total a

01:58:18.068 --> 01:58:23.538
war as it gets because the, the entirety
of the society was involved in it.

01:58:23.588 --> 01:58:27.568
- When there's wide-scale targeting
of the economic infrastructure

01:58:27.628 --> 01:58:31.368
of your opponent that can be used to support
the war effort, which is what you get

01:58:31.408 --> 01:58:34.908
increasingly as the war goes on and
officially when Grant is in charge as

01:58:34.948 --> 01:58:41.028
general-in-chief, then certainly that's
a total... If you're the farmer who

01:58:41.688 --> 01:58:45.508
experiences an army coming
by and they, they take

01:58:45.588 --> 01:58:49.578
all, take or kill all your livestock
and destroy your crops, and that's,

01:58:49.608 --> 01:58:52.928
that's pretty much like a total
war, even if they don't kill you.

01:58:53.228 --> 01:58:55.148
- So the war escalates.

01:58:55.688 --> 01:58:59.608
- Yes, the war does escalate.
'62, it takes a quantum leap.

01:58:59.748 --> 01:59:02.258
In the spring and early summer of 1862,

01:59:03.228 --> 01:59:06.628
the signals are clear this is something
that we had not counted on before.

01:59:07.248 --> 01:59:10.628
And the armies are much, they're gonna be much
bigger. The Army of the Potomac's more than

01:59:10.668 --> 01:59:14.568
100,000 men. There's never been an army
like that in US history, nothing like that.

01:59:15.448 --> 01:59:20.828
- What do we understand from the
diaries of soldiers coming,

01:59:21.748 --> 01:59:25.598
For the first time face to face with
this scale of war? We're talking

01:59:25.627 --> 01:59:28.308
about a year. They've been volunteering
for a year, like you mentioned,

01:59:29.148 --> 01:59:31.568
you know, in the summer, in the
spring and the summer of '62.

01:59:32.488 --> 01:59:36.468
- There's so many themes in soldier
letters, and it's an incredible gift

01:59:37.328 --> 01:59:41.288
to people such as myself who
study this, is that almost, that

01:59:41.308 --> 01:59:44.648
a very large majority of the men
on both sides were literate.

01:59:45.288 --> 01:59:49.408
And so we have this mass of testimony.
And they, and they, they sent

01:59:50.608 --> 01:59:54.468
millions of letters from the armies
home and, and back to the armies, the,

01:59:54.708 --> 01:59:58.218
the on both sides. So we have this,

01:59:58.588 --> 02:00:02.428
this trove of first-hand
evidence and diaries too, many,

02:00:02.468 --> 02:00:06.398
many, many, many, many diaries.
But so you can see in real

02:00:06.548 --> 02:00:10.168
time how attitudes are changing,
hardening. You can see the

02:00:10.208 --> 02:00:14.148
hardening process in soldiers who they don't
have any idea what being a soldier is,

02:00:14.228 --> 02:00:18.148
then they do, and they don't like lots of things
about it. They don't... They talk a lot about food,

02:00:18.648 --> 02:00:21.508
talk a lot about clothing, talk
a lot about officers they don't

02:00:21.548 --> 02:00:25.308
like. They don't talk, what
one thing, it's really

02:00:25.348 --> 02:00:29.068
fascinating, the thing they
don't try to do is explain to

02:00:29.108 --> 02:00:33.008
people behind the lines exactly what
it's like to be in a battle because they

02:00:33.048 --> 02:00:36.948
understand the people back home won't
understand what they're talking about. And so

02:00:37.008 --> 02:00:40.948
they'll say things like, "It was awful,"
and, "Words just can't convey what

02:00:40.968 --> 02:00:44.408
it was," and then maybe tell
more about their individual

02:00:44.468 --> 02:00:46.868
experience. But there's a real sense

02:00:47.908 --> 02:00:51.678
that what a veteran knows about
being in the military and what the

02:00:51.748 --> 02:00:55.548
people back home will understand are two different
things, and you just can't explain it to them.

02:00:56.218 --> 02:01:00.408
- And so that takes us into the
fall of the Battle of Antietam.

02:01:01.228 --> 02:01:05.348
Can you walk me through what happened
at the battle and why it mattered?

02:01:05.848 --> 02:01:09.698
- George B. McClellan, whom I've already
referred to dismissively a couple of times-

02:01:11.252 --> 02:01:12.152
He was-

02:01:12.192 --> 02:01:16.132
... he was in charge of the Army of the
Potomac by this point. He'd been general

02:01:16.192 --> 02:01:19.452
in chief for a brief time and
commanding the Army of the Potomac.

02:01:20.212 --> 02:01:24.012
And he finally approached Richmond.
He got within, literally within

02:01:24.072 --> 02:01:27.572
five miles of Richmond. He was opposing
an officer named Joseph E. Johnston,

02:01:28.792 --> 02:01:32.392
who just, his first thought every day was,
"What a great day to retreat. Where could I go?

02:01:32.492 --> 02:01:35.972
I could go that way, or I could go that way."
He and McClellan were a match made in heaven.

02:01:36.032 --> 02:01:39.852
McClellan didn't wanna go anywhere,
and Johnston only wanted to

02:01:39.872 --> 02:01:42.432
retreat. Anyway, they finally
ended up at Richmond.

02:01:44.592 --> 02:01:48.492
Johnston got wounded, got hit
by a shell fragment outside

02:01:48.532 --> 02:01:51.532
Richmond in the Battle of Seven Pines,
and he's replaced by Robert E. Lee.

02:01:52.462 --> 02:01:56.372
And McClellan, in his usual perceptive
way, observed that this was great because

02:01:56.412 --> 02:02:00.112
he knew that Lee was too timid to ever be
an effective presence on the battlefield.

02:02:00.172 --> 02:02:02.222
So he read him just exactly the way-

02:02:02.292 --> 02:02:06.952
... that McClellan would. He
ends up fighting Lee, and he,

02:02:07.792 --> 02:02:11.582
in the Seven Days' Battles, McClellan is
pushed away from Richmond. He shouldn't have

02:02:11.612 --> 02:02:15.591
been, but he was. That was
considered a great victory.

02:02:15.712 --> 02:02:19.692
Lee's reputation goes
up after that. Then Lee

02:02:19.732 --> 02:02:23.092
follows that up with the campaign
of Second Bull Run, which

02:02:23.152 --> 02:02:27.042
reorients the war from
Richmond to Manassas,

02:02:27.092 --> 02:02:31.032
the old First Manassas battlefield in
northern Virginia. Stonewall Jackson plays

02:02:31.072 --> 02:02:34.932
a key role in it. He wins the Battle
of Second Bull Run in late August

02:02:35.012 --> 02:02:38.772
1862, and then invades the loyal

02:02:38.872 --> 02:02:42.272
states for the first time. He crosses
into Maryland in early September

02:02:43.072 --> 02:02:46.732
with an army that is broken
down in terms of supply.

02:02:47.512 --> 02:02:51.492
He has tremendous straggling problems
and desertion problems, Lee did,

02:02:51.732 --> 02:02:55.352
in ... He moved across the army
with about 50,000 men. When they

02:02:55.432 --> 02:02:59.272
finally came to battle at
Antietam, which is in western

02:02:59.312 --> 02:03:03.152
Maryland, he only had about 35,000
soldiers on the field. McClellan had

02:03:03.192 --> 02:03:05.072
about 80,000 on the other side.

02:03:05.872 --> 02:03:09.802
And McClellan had captured a copy
of Lee's entire orders for the

02:03:09.872 --> 02:03:13.812
whole campaign, Special Orders
Number 191. They had gone

02:03:13.832 --> 02:03:18.702
through an old Confederate camp- ...
and found a copy of Lee's orders.

02:03:18.712 --> 02:03:22.612
And still McClellan didn't move quickly.
I won't bash McClellan. They fight.

02:03:22.992 --> 02:03:26.312
The bloodiest single day in US
history is Antietam, the battle of

02:03:26.592 --> 02:03:30.432
23,000 casualties in one
day there. Very close

02:03:30.492 --> 02:03:33.582
thing. Lee hangs on by
just the narrowest margin,

02:03:34.832 --> 02:03:38.752
sits on the battlefield for another whole day,
which he shouldn't have done. He was being way too

02:03:38.772 --> 02:03:41.592
bold there, and then retreated,
got back across the Potomac River.

02:03:42.232 --> 02:03:43.972
Confederates looked at this whole thing

02:03:44.832 --> 02:03:48.692
as a drama in three acts:
save Richmond, reorient the

02:03:48.752 --> 02:03:52.642
war to northern Virginia, cross into
the United States, and they saw it as

02:03:52.772 --> 02:03:54.852
a success. Richmond's
no longer threatened.

02:03:55.332 --> 02:04:01.472
Lee's reputation has gone way up. McClellan
has frustrated Lincoln immeasurably,

02:04:02.292 --> 02:04:06.252
and as a result, Lincoln removes
McClellan from command. He waits

02:04:06.332 --> 02:04:09.832
till the day after the elections in
November 'cause McClellan's popular among

02:04:09.872 --> 02:04:13.512
Democrats, and Lincoln is an adept
politician. He fires him the day

02:04:13.612 --> 02:04:17.752
after the elections and replaces him
with a guy named Ambrose Burnside.

02:04:18.552 --> 02:04:22.412
- So what can you say about what makes a great
general and what makes a poor general in

02:04:22.492 --> 02:04:22.932
all of this?

02:04:23.532 --> 02:04:27.032
- Well, McClellan is a very smart guy,

02:04:27.132 --> 02:04:30.952
and he was on Winfield
Scott's staff in Mexico, as

02:04:30.992 --> 02:04:34.952
was Lee, which is a sign that
you're a pretty bright officer.

02:04:35.032 --> 02:04:38.752
He built the Army of the Potomac. He did
a brilliant job building the Army of the

02:04:38.772 --> 02:04:42.702
Potomac. The troops loved him. There
was never a bond with any other

02:04:42.732 --> 02:04:45.822
commander the way they had with
McClellan. But what he couldn't

02:04:45.852 --> 02:04:49.832
do when the moment came.
When the moment came, he

02:04:49.892 --> 02:04:54.912
couldn't risk things. He was risk-averse.
He had a chance to capture Richmond

02:04:55.912 --> 02:04:59.772
against Lee. He should have, and
he didn't. He withdrew. He had a

02:04:59.832 --> 02:05:03.532
chance ... You almost never had a chance
to destroy another army in the Civil War.

02:05:03.632 --> 02:05:07.452
He had a chance to destroy Lee's
army at Antietam, and he didn't. He

02:05:07.512 --> 02:05:11.452
pulled back. He didn't even
use 20,000 of his men. They

02:05:11.492 --> 02:05:15.252
never fired their muskets at Antietam.
If you're in Lee's army, you're gonna

02:05:15.292 --> 02:05:19.192
fire your musket. If you're in McClellan's
army, maybe, maybe not. He didn't

02:05:19.212 --> 02:05:23.181
at Antietam. He let Lee get away.
It frustrated Lincoln immensely,

02:05:23.352 --> 02:05:27.212
and Lincoln finally fired him
because of that. It was a flaw. He

02:05:27.312 --> 02:05:30.732
lacked what they would've called in
the mid-19th century the moral courage

02:05:31.212 --> 02:05:38.132
to risk everything. Grant had it. Lee
had it. McClellan didn't have it.

02:05:38.332 --> 02:05:41.722
- One of the components of a great general
is the willingness to risk it all,

02:05:42.472 --> 02:05:46.742
Obviously with, with good
advice, with intelligence, but--

02:05:47.412 --> 02:05:47.982
- Which he had.

02:05:47.992 --> 02:05:48.722
- ... risk failure.

02:05:49.352 --> 02:05:53.332
- The whole war changed in this arc
between the Seven Days and Antietam,

02:05:53.372 --> 02:05:57.292
and it changed in this
way. McClellan's defeat at

02:05:57.312 --> 02:06:01.202
the Seven Days. This is
in late June and the, and

02:06:01.202 --> 02:06:05.092
early July '62, convinced both Congress

02:06:05.652 --> 02:06:09.412
and Lincoln that slavery was gonna
have to be put on the table. The

02:06:09.452 --> 02:06:14.172
war's gonna be moral and con-- McClellan
could've won the war with slavery intact.

02:06:14.252 --> 02:06:18.112
He didn't, so Congress passed the
Second Confiscation Act in late

02:06:18.232 --> 02:06:20.992
July, which I mentioned earlier,
which strikes at slavery.

02:06:22.052 --> 02:06:26.032
And then Lincoln decided, he
told his cabinet toward the end

02:06:26.112 --> 02:06:29.142
of July that he was gonna issue
a proclamation of emancipation,

02:06:29.992 --> 02:06:33.712
and he was persuaded that he couldn't just do
it then in the wake of a defeat. He should

02:06:33.832 --> 02:06:37.812
wait until he had a victory. He got
another defeat at Second Bull Run.

02:06:37.892 --> 02:06:41.812
He finally got a victory at Antietam
because Lee retreated, and then that's

02:06:41.832 --> 02:06:45.262
when he announced the preliminary
proclamation. The key battle here isn't

02:06:45.332 --> 02:06:49.092
Antietam. The key battle is the Seven
Days and McClellan's defeat that

02:06:49.132 --> 02:06:53.032
persuades Lincoln to do this.
Antietam gives him the occasion to do

02:06:53.072 --> 02:06:55.132
it, and he issued his
preliminary proclamation

02:06:56.212 --> 02:06:59.632
five days after the battle
on September 22nd, 1862.

02:06:59.932 --> 02:07:03.892
- Can you elaborate why it became
obvious that the slavery has to be

02:07:03.912 --> 02:07:07.692
on the table as, as the motivation,
as the driving motivation-

02:07:07.742 --> 02:07:08.462
... for the war at that time?

02:07:08.532 --> 02:07:12.162
- Because people believed the...
And they were right. The war was

02:07:12.232 --> 02:07:16.032
almost over at the end of
May 1862. It had been a

02:07:16.072 --> 02:07:19.872
catastrophe for the Confederacy
in the Western Theater.

02:07:20.604 --> 02:07:24.104
They had lost New Orleans. New Orleans
was surrender- the biggest city in the

02:07:24.144 --> 02:07:28.024
Confederacy by far, 160,000
people, four times bigger than the

02:07:28.104 --> 02:07:31.884
next biggest con- the most
important port. The key to the

02:07:31.904 --> 02:07:35.604
Mississippi River Valley as a, as
an economic artery. If you don't

02:07:35.664 --> 02:07:39.524
control New Orleans, the
Mississippi isn't an economic river

02:07:39.604 --> 02:07:41.324
for you. New Orleans was gone in April.

02:07:42.264 --> 02:07:45.824
Nashville was gone, the key
city and capital of Tennessee.

02:07:45.984 --> 02:07:49.884
Memphis was gone in June. The
whole state of Tennessee, except

02:07:49.924 --> 02:07:53.804
the mountainous East Tennessee, was
basically not a Confederate state anymore by

02:07:53.864 --> 02:07:55.514
May 1862.

02:07:56.424 --> 02:08:02.444
There was a Union army of 100,000 in
northern Mississippi by the end of May.

02:08:03.564 --> 02:08:09.104
Catastrophic. And George B.
McClellan's army was in five miles of

02:08:09.164 --> 02:08:10.904
the national capital in Richmond.

02:08:11.744 --> 02:08:15.684
I don't think the Confederacy could
have absorbed the loss of Richmond at

02:08:15.724 --> 02:08:19.444
that point. I think it would've
been over, and emancipation

02:08:20.444 --> 02:08:24.784
was not on the table yet in an official
way. The fact that Richmond didn't fall

02:08:25.704 --> 02:08:29.524
convinced people in the North that
the war was gonna have to last a lot

02:08:29.564 --> 02:08:33.264
longer. The Confederates were gonna be
harder to beat, convinced Europe of the same

02:08:33.304 --> 02:08:36.544
thing. By Europe, I mean France
and England. That's what

02:08:36.604 --> 02:08:40.444
mattered. And so they decided that
they were gonna have to take more

02:08:40.454 --> 02:08:44.364
extreme measures, and then the key,
more extreme measure is strike at

02:08:44.424 --> 02:08:47.164
slavery. Strike at it as
a war measure directly.

02:08:47.844 --> 02:08:51.664
- And as a war measure, it
what? Attacks at the economic

02:08:51.724 --> 02:08:53.804
infrastructure of the Confederacy.

02:08:54.124 --> 02:08:58.064
- It attacks the, the economic infrastructure
that enables the Confederacy

02:08:58.104 --> 02:09:01.144
to maintain the largest
possible army in the war.

02:09:01.234 --> 02:09:06.054
- So this is not about the, the ethics,
the morality of slavery. It's the-

02:09:06.054 --> 02:09:09.794
- It is for the abolitionists who see it, and
Frederick Douglass realized that this is

02:09:09.804 --> 02:09:13.284
good. The abolitionists
realized early on, even in

02:09:13.304 --> 02:09:17.204
1861, some of them, Charles
Sumner, for example, saw

02:09:17.484 --> 02:09:21.404
the Union defeat at First Bull
Run. "Yes, it's bad that we

02:09:21.444 --> 02:09:25.394
lost, but the longer the war
goes, the more likely that

02:09:25.424 --> 02:09:29.223
we can get slavery on the table, get
emancipation on the table." Frederick Douglass

02:09:29.264 --> 02:09:30.283
believed the same thing.

02:09:31.223 --> 02:09:34.974
A longer war made that more
likely, and McClellan handed them

02:09:35.024 --> 02:09:40.564
that gift by not being as aggressive as
he should've been at Richmond in 1862.

02:09:40.584 --> 02:09:45.774
- But we should say, and correct me if
I'm wrong, that only a tiny minority

02:09:46.384 --> 02:09:49.484
of Americans were
abolitionists at that time.

02:09:49.504 --> 02:09:52.484
- We'll never know exactly. I would
say if you... I think if you guessed

02:09:52.664 --> 02:09:56.644
3% were- I mean, really
abolitionists, destroy

02:09:56.684 --> 02:09:59.764
it now, destroy it completely,
no compensation for rebel owners

02:10:00.544 --> 02:10:03.704
or any owners, kill it in Kentucky,
kill it in Missouri, kill it in

02:10:03.744 --> 02:10:08.564
Maryland. Yes, I think 3%
would be about the ballpark.

02:10:08.604 --> 02:10:12.464
- So of course, now we
venerate those folks, and

02:10:12.524 --> 02:10:16.424
we mythologize them, and so
they become bigger in our

02:10:16.464 --> 02:10:21.084
minds. But we should remember
that they're a tiny percentage.

02:10:21.224 --> 02:10:25.194
- They're a tiny percentage, and there are
other factors that are pushing forward the

02:10:25.204 --> 02:10:27.624
process that leads to the 13th Amendment.

02:10:27.704 --> 02:10:31.384
It's not just people who hate slavery so
much that they'll do anything to kill it.

02:10:31.694 --> 02:10:34.004
The... in fact, most of them
aren't. That's not what they are.

02:10:35.204 --> 02:10:38.764
We wish they were, but they're not.
And Lincoln is in a tough position.

02:10:39.284 --> 02:10:42.944
Lincoln hates slavery personally, but
he's president trying to win a war,

02:10:43.424 --> 02:10:47.304
and he can't win the war with just Republicans.
He has to try to keep Democrats on

02:10:47.344 --> 02:10:52.364
board, so he has to... I mean, and
the Constitution protects slavery.

02:10:52.464 --> 02:10:56.224
In Kentucky and Missouri and Maryland
and Delaware. They're still in the

02:10:56.264 --> 02:11:00.184
nation. The Constitution still
applies. Congress, by the time, by

02:11:00.224 --> 02:11:03.734
the end of June, had already gotten rid of
slavery in the only two places where it

02:11:03.784 --> 02:11:07.834
could. And that is in the federal
territories and the District of Columbia.

02:11:08.684 --> 02:11:12.344
No compensation in the territories, but
there were hardly any enslaved people there,

02:11:12.904 --> 02:11:16.084
and there was some compensation
in the District of Columbia.

02:11:17.104 --> 02:11:22.934
- You said that Lincoln hates slavery.
Can we just take that tangent and

02:11:24.164 --> 02:11:27.804
from a perspective of some,
there's a hypocrisy there

02:11:28.724 --> 02:11:32.324
in pushing for a policy of colonization.

02:11:32.404 --> 02:11:35.644
- Colonization. Yes,
colonization. In the summer of

02:11:35.704 --> 02:11:39.464
1862, Lincoln met with a group of
Black leaders and tried to convince

02:11:39.603 --> 02:11:43.284
them to support colonization,
which colonization

02:11:43.344 --> 02:11:47.274
meant if you freed enslaved people, then
they would go somewhere else. They'd

02:11:47.304 --> 02:11:52.394
go out of the United States. It's
why Liberia exists. And Lincoln,

02:11:52.744 --> 02:11:56.174
that is probably the least
attractive element of his total

02:11:56.344 --> 02:12:00.084
thinking on... He genuinely didn't
believe that white people would

02:12:00.124 --> 02:12:04.024
ever accept Black people as equals. He told
the leaders that when he met with them, and he

02:12:04.084 --> 02:12:08.064
said, "You wouldn't want it that way. I
wouldn't either. It's the way it is. It's

02:12:08.104 --> 02:12:08.744
a reality."

02:12:08.944 --> 02:12:12.444
- Do we know he believed that, or
is this a pragmatic politician

02:12:13.024 --> 02:12:14.404
understanding the lay of the land?

02:12:14.644 --> 02:12:18.304
- I think for someone who was
born in Kentucky and reared in

02:12:18.384 --> 02:12:21.824
Southern Indiana and Southern
Illinois, he was never around

02:12:21.844 --> 02:12:25.784
anything but a level of intensity
on race that he probably

02:12:26.264 --> 02:12:30.044
didn't ever see, wasn't ever around
white people who he could see

02:12:30.084 --> 02:12:34.224
accepting Black people as fully
equal. That's- ... I do believe that.

02:12:35.084 --> 02:12:39.004
- And still, it is a country
that where the words

02:12:39.024 --> 02:12:40.964
"All men are created
equal" is on the page.

02:12:40.974 --> 02:12:44.964
- They're right there on the page,
waiting for that to be made good

02:12:45.044 --> 02:12:49.664
on. Yes, that's correct. Well, it's true.

02:12:49.704 --> 02:12:53.744
And Lincoln-- I mean, Lincoln
also was capable of growth.

02:12:54.204 --> 02:13:00.724
- Well, he seemed to have evolved
quite a lot in the years of the war.

02:13:01.034 --> 02:13:02.884
- He did. No, he did. He did.

02:13:03.024 --> 02:13:06.124
- And it's very, very possible were he not

02:13:06.604 --> 02:13:09.784
assassinated, he would've arrived
at a very different worldview.

02:13:10.144 --> 02:13:13.864
- We'll never know, but we
do know that he was a lot

02:13:13.964 --> 02:13:17.304
smarter and a lot more adaptable
and a lot more politically

02:13:17.344 --> 02:13:20.654
adept than Andrew Johnson, and
so it certainly would've looked

02:13:20.924 --> 02:13:22.328
different. Well, it's true.

02:13:22.688 --> 02:13:23.698
- No, I just-

02:13:23.698 --> 02:13:23.838
- Yes, it is true.

02:13:23.838 --> 02:13:26.908
- ... I think I vaguely remember
you saying somewhere that Andrew

02:13:26.948 --> 02:13:32.488
Johnson is the second worst
president in America's history.

02:13:32.548 --> 02:13:36.438
- Yeah, he's right. He has... the
glory of being above James Buchanan.

02:13:36.608 --> 02:13:37.898
I said that a few years ago, yes.

02:13:37.907 --> 02:13:40.168
- I think you said something about Buchanan.

02:13:41.208 --> 02:13:44.518
is... There's like a basement, there's
like a basement below basement. I think-

02:13:44.558 --> 02:13:45.648
- Sub-basement. He's in the sub-basement.

02:13:45.908 --> 02:13:50.628
- ... He's in the sub-basement. And this I
put that in the category of the slime, uh-

02:13:50.638 --> 02:13:51.318
... poetic commentary. Uh-

02:13:51.348 --> 02:13:55.028
- He's in a little corner of the sub-basement
all by himself with one candle.

02:13:55.628 --> 02:13:59.528
- So can we take that tiny tangent of
why they were terrible pr- uh, tangent

02:13:59.568 --> 02:14:03.378
on a tangent, why was Andrew Johnson such a
terrible president, and Buchanan as well?

02:14:03.428 --> 02:14:06.568
- I think he was a terrible
president because he was,

02:14:07.968 --> 02:14:11.688
he was extremely prejudiced.
He had all kinds of grievances

02:14:11.868 --> 02:14:15.688
stacked up. He had the poor white
man's grievance from being in a

02:14:15.748 --> 02:14:19.568
slavehold. He hates... His slaveholders were
part of his enemies when he was growing up,

02:14:20.048 --> 02:14:21.928
then he embraced them after the war.

02:14:22.008 --> 02:14:29.708
He was... He couldn't get beyond his most
profound lifelong prejudices. He was not

02:14:30.068 --> 02:14:33.988
malleable in any way. He thought what he
thought, and that's what he was always

02:14:34.028 --> 02:14:37.628
gonna think. And he just was not the
right be- person to be in place.

02:14:38.008 --> 02:14:41.218
- Well, why is Buchanan the worst
president- ... in America's history?

02:14:41.788 --> 02:14:45.748
- If you had a dictionary that had the word
hack in it, it should have a picture of

02:14:45.808 --> 02:14:49.838
James Buchanan by it. He was
an absolute political hack-

02:14:50.148 --> 02:14:54.108
... who made his way up by staying alive
and doing everything that you should

02:14:54.128 --> 02:14:57.308
do as a party hack as you went
along. And then, oh, I ended up as

02:14:57.328 --> 02:15:01.298
president in 1856 because there's
a... The person I'm running

02:15:01.348 --> 02:15:05.218
against is from a party, this is the first
time they're running a candidate. His

02:15:05.248 --> 02:15:09.178
way of dealing with the secession crisis
was basically hiding under his desk and

02:15:09.208 --> 02:15:13.178
hoping the roof didn't fall in before he
got out of Washington. He's just, he's just

02:15:13.228 --> 02:15:13.628
horrible.

02:15:14.688 --> 02:15:18.628
- I think from that I get a sense that what
you admire in a leader and a president

02:15:18.688 --> 02:15:21.088
is a, is a kind of backbone, uh-

02:15:21.388 --> 02:15:25.208
... to be able to, again, take
risks and to stand behind

02:15:25.228 --> 02:15:29.028
political savvy, yes, but also
a backbone behind that savvy.

02:15:29.188 --> 02:15:32.958
- Yes, and to actually change a
position if you're persuaded-

02:15:33.368 --> 02:15:37.168
... by both events and good counsel that
you had the wrong position before. Go

02:15:37.228 --> 02:15:38.028
ahead and do that.

02:15:38.088 --> 02:15:43.608
- So some kind of balance of, of a
backbone behind principles and

02:15:43.788 --> 02:15:48.558
malleability based on the information,
knowledge, and wisdom that you gain-

02:15:48.848 --> 02:15:51.888
... versus for convenience or out of fear.

02:15:51.968 --> 02:15:55.808
- There's an apocryphal story about Lincoln
that I think gets at his willingness to

02:15:55.848 --> 02:15:59.768
row against the tide if he needed to. He's... They're
discussing something in a cabinet meeting, and he

02:15:59.808 --> 02:16:03.228
says, "Well, let's, let's vote. Let me get
your sense." And they went around the table,

02:16:03.688 --> 02:16:06.848
and every cabinet member
voted no. And, and

02:16:07.768 --> 02:16:10.918
Lincoln said, "Well, that's
six nos and one yes. Mine,

02:16:11.708 --> 02:16:15.568
the ayes have it." I mean, he's...
So he listened to them, and in that

02:16:15.668 --> 02:16:19.608
case, he's willing to do something
that's unpopular and knows that he's

02:16:19.648 --> 02:16:23.588
gonna get... But he'll do it because... And
that's, that's a quality not everybody has

02:16:23.668 --> 02:16:23.888
either.

02:16:24.428 --> 02:16:26.158
- Who's the greatest president
in the history of the United-

02:16:26.208 --> 02:16:27.168
- In my view?

02:16:27.908 --> 02:16:31.848
In my view, it's neck and neck with Washington
and Lincoln. Without Washington, there's no

02:16:31.928 --> 02:16:35.568
United States, so it's sort
of hard to go against him.

02:16:36.048 --> 02:16:38.928
He set all kinds of precedents
that were really important.

02:16:39.328 --> 02:16:40.648
- Just giving away power.

02:16:40.728 --> 02:16:44.608
- Twice. He did it twice. The Europeans
couldn't wrap their minds around that. He

02:16:44.628 --> 02:16:48.438
gave it away after the revolution.
In other words, his message

02:16:48.528 --> 02:16:52.188
is, "I'm not Caesar. I'm
not Cromwell. I'm not

02:16:52.268 --> 02:16:55.468
Napoleon." There hadn't been a Napoleon
yet, but anyway, he wasn't one.

02:16:56.168 --> 02:16:59.938
"I had power, and I gave it back." And then he
stepped down. He would've been president for

02:16:59.988 --> 02:17:04.968
life if he wanted to be. He did two terms.
That's enough. Let somebody else do it.

02:17:07.128 --> 02:17:14.228
Who does that? But he embodies the notion
of what... This is what a citizen soldier

02:17:14.688 --> 02:17:18.588
does. He comes forward at
a time of crisis, he does

02:17:18.628 --> 02:17:22.608
his duty, and when the crisis
is over, he is Cincinnatus.

02:17:22.988 --> 02:17:26.728
He goes back to his farm when
this is over with, because the

02:17:26.768 --> 02:17:30.428
point is, he'd done what
republic... small R republican

02:17:30.548 --> 02:17:33.418
virtue dictated, and then he went back.

02:17:34.628 --> 02:17:38.588
And, and that image was
invoked over and over and

02:17:38.708 --> 02:17:42.368
over again. The small R repub- This has
nothing to do with the Republican Party.

02:17:42.408 --> 02:17:46.348
Small R republican virtue
of disinterested service

02:17:46.408 --> 02:17:49.747
to your republic. It's... And
Washington embodied that.

02:17:50.528 --> 02:17:54.478
He's crucial. What's always been
fascinating to me, Lex, is you have

02:17:54.528 --> 02:17:57.808
this guy, he's surrounded
by this freakishly

02:17:57.868 --> 02:18:01.238
talented group of people in
this little nation. You have

02:18:01.268 --> 02:18:05.168
Madison and Hamilton and Jefferson
and Franklin, all these people

02:18:05.208 --> 02:18:08.518
who are probably smarter than Washington.
They've certainly read more Greek and

02:18:08.588 --> 02:18:14.318
Latin than Washington, and yet nobody's
concerned about who the man is.

02:18:14.428 --> 02:18:17.988
It's Washington. He's
the guy. We're smarter.

02:18:18.748 --> 02:18:22.068
It's like what Sherman
said about Grant one time.

02:18:23.008 --> 02:18:26.928
Sherman's smarter than Grant, probably.
Sherman said, "I know more about strategy than

02:18:26.988 --> 02:18:30.708
Grant. I know more about tactics. I know
more about supply. I know more about

02:18:30.828 --> 02:18:34.808
everything than U.S. Grant does.
But where he beats me and where

02:18:34.868 --> 02:18:38.798
he beats the world is he don't give
a damn what the enemy is doing

02:18:38.888 --> 02:18:43.808
out of his sight, and that scares me like
hell." In other words, Grant could just

02:18:45.188 --> 02:18:49.058
let the background noise go
away, focus on his thing, and

02:18:49.128 --> 02:18:52.548
do what he was gonna do, and
Sherman couldn't do that. Grant

02:18:52.648 --> 02:18:56.328
liberated Sherman to become a great
general because he had Grant over him

02:18:56.448 --> 02:18:58.068
taking responsibility.

02:18:58.288 --> 02:19:03.948
It's really fascinating. And Washington,
whatever faults he had, he could take

02:19:03.988 --> 02:19:07.348
responsibility, and was
aware that he was setting

02:19:07.388 --> 02:19:11.228
precedents as the first president
of this new republic almost

02:19:11.268 --> 02:19:14.888
every time he did something. It's a
precedent. What did Washington do?

02:19:15.900 --> 02:19:18.790
Well, he gave up his uniform, and
then he gave up the presidency

02:19:19.440 --> 02:19:23.280
- Yeah, just from studying history,
understanding how difficult it

02:19:23.340 --> 02:19:27.300
is to give away power in the way that
Washington did, I have to put him as the number

02:19:27.440 --> 02:19:27.600
one.

02:19:27.780 --> 02:19:29.840
- Yeah, because without him,
the rest doesn't happen.

02:19:29.920 --> 02:19:33.720
I mean, that's my bottom line on it, and I really
do believe that. Without him, the rest doesn't

02:19:33.760 --> 02:19:37.710
happen. I don't know what... If they hadn't
had Washington to persuade people that

02:19:37.740 --> 02:19:41.680
a stronger central government might be
possible because Washington's gonna

02:19:41.720 --> 02:19:45.440
be president, who else would they
agree could be trusted? There's

02:19:45.480 --> 02:19:47.000
Washington, and then
there's everybody else.

02:19:47.620 --> 02:19:50.840
- And I think America's a truly
positive force in human history.

02:19:51.680 --> 02:19:55.180
So the fact that whatever ideas that
were created at that time, and that

02:19:55.240 --> 02:20:02.080
Washington was the foundation of
that, is a great gift to humanity.

02:20:02.540 --> 02:20:06.080
- Lucky to have him, lucky to have Lincoln.
We've been lucky in our leaders, Indy.

02:20:06.140 --> 02:20:09.110
We're lucky to have FDR in World War
II. I mean, we've been lucky to have-

02:20:09.240 --> 02:20:12.280
... people at moments of real crisis.

02:20:12.860 --> 02:20:17.060
- But we need the basement, too. We
need, we need the full variety.

02:20:17.320 --> 02:20:18.219
- Well, we've got them.

02:20:19.600 --> 02:20:23.590
And you never know when we might need
a new sub-basement. So it's just

02:20:23.700 --> 02:20:25.900
complicated and an ongoing story.

02:20:25.950 --> 02:20:29.840
- Man, it is a beautiful theater,
a beautiful drama, and

02:20:30.100 --> 02:20:34.080
I, like you, have faith in the
resilience of the people to

02:20:34.120 --> 02:20:37.240
deal with the full mess of it.
Quick bathroom break if it's okay.

02:20:37.280 --> 02:20:37.440
- Yeah.

02:20:38.100 --> 02:20:43.250
- I wanted to mention to you just as
a plug, but also as a big thank you

02:20:44.340 --> 02:20:48.040
that I don't know how many
hours, it must be maybe over 50

02:20:48.120 --> 02:20:55.540
hours I've enjoyed listening to your Great
Courses. So you have two on the American

02:20:55.620 --> 02:20:57.720
Civil War and on Robert E. Lee.

02:20:58.020 --> 02:20:58.180
- Right.

02:20:58.580 --> 02:21:01.980
- And so I just wanted to mention this
