notes are then transferred.

02:21:17.950 --> 02:21:18.960
- God, technology. Geez.

02:21:19.300 --> 02:21:22.980
- Yeah, this thing is great. So it's great for just...
It's been running this whole time, too, so it's

02:21:23.020 --> 02:21:25.050
transcribing automatically.
It's using AI to transcribe-

02:21:25.060 --> 02:21:27.130
- Oh, so it's doing every word,
everything that we say?

02:21:27.260 --> 02:21:29.550
- Yeah. You know, transcribing it into text.

02:21:29.740 --> 02:21:30.540
- Right. Right.

02:21:30.580 --> 02:21:34.200
- And then it can summarize it for you,
and then I organize and study the notes

02:21:34.340 --> 02:21:38.060
afterwards. This thing is great. Highly
recommend it. It's called Plaud.

02:21:38.140 --> 02:21:38.920
- Plaud.

02:21:39.120 --> 02:21:40.480
- Plaud. It's a clip.

02:21:41.060 --> 02:21:44.960
- I used to run around Town Lake or what
was, I guess it's Lady Bird Johnson

02:21:45.020 --> 02:21:50.440
Lake now. Is that what they call it?
We used to run six miles a day at noon

02:21:51.260 --> 02:21:55.580
for years when I worked at the LBJ Library.
We had a little group of us who went.

02:21:55.980 --> 02:21:56.460
- Just run?

02:21:56.940 --> 02:22:02.559
- Just ran. We'd do it in... We
would do six miles in 38 minutes.

02:22:02.960 --> 02:22:07.680
- It's actually a really hard problem
to solve, of how to not take memory.

02:22:08.600 --> 02:22:15.040
I'm just really happy this exists. Okay.
'Cause my smartphone was being a pain

02:22:15.140 --> 02:22:18.960
in the ass, like writing it down. It's been,
it's been a hard problem for me to solve.

02:22:20.280 --> 02:22:22.580
You listen to audiobooks
or no? Or you just read?

02:22:22.600 --> 02:22:23.580
- All the time on my w-

02:22:23.700 --> 02:22:27.680
I walk... I don't run anymore, but I
walk six or seven miles a day, and

02:22:27.780 --> 02:22:33.580
I listen to Audible all the time.
That's what I do. I love Audible.

02:22:34.180 --> 02:22:36.900
- Well, just out of curiosity,
just take that tangent.

02:22:37.700 --> 02:22:41.000
Do you listen to, like, history books?
Like what... Or do you wanna escape?

02:22:41.580 --> 02:22:45.500
- I listen to a mixture. I
listen to some history books,

02:22:46.860 --> 02:22:49.640
but I also listen to
some mysteries and some-

02:22:49.760 --> 02:22:50.529
- So fiction is also-

02:22:50.580 --> 02:22:54.480
- Oh, fiction, oh yeah. Yeah, and
some books that I think I should

02:22:54.520 --> 02:22:58.220
listen to and haven't ever gotten around
to reading. I did Moby Dick that way.

02:22:58.440 --> 02:23:00.260
- Mm-hmm. So the classics also?

02:23:00.360 --> 02:23:04.240
- Oh, yeah. But I've never
read War and Peace, and I

02:23:04.280 --> 02:23:08.180
don't think I ever will. I think... I
would've done it by now if I was going to.

02:23:08.440 --> 02:23:12.360
- We're making a statement today. I like a

02:23:12.440 --> 02:23:14.999
person who's not, not always,
but I like short books.

02:23:15.240 --> 02:23:19.100
- I do too. I write short
books. A lot of long books

02:23:19.160 --> 02:23:23.140
would make good short books, and a lot of
short books would make good essays, I think.

02:23:23.560 --> 02:23:27.240
- Yeah, can you... Ugh, I'm sorry to be taking
so many tangents 'cause you've written so many

02:23:27.280 --> 02:23:31.080
excellent books. How much of it is editing?
Like how-- or what does it take to

02:23:31.100 --> 02:23:34.439
write a good history
book? What's the process?

02:23:34.500 --> 02:23:36.020
- Well, for me, it's having

02:23:36.920 --> 02:23:40.620
either a question that you wanna
answer or having some strain in the

02:23:40.660 --> 02:23:44.480
literature that you think is wrong,
and you need to... For me, the

02:23:44.540 --> 02:23:48.420
Union War was trying to rescue something
that I thought people just don't understand

02:23:48.460 --> 02:23:52.160
anymore, is what union meant to
that generation. It makes more

02:23:52.200 --> 02:23:55.980
sense... A war fought to
end slavery makes sense to

02:23:56.060 --> 02:23:59.910
us now. That's, of course, it's a
monstrous institution, fight a war...

02:23:59.940 --> 02:24:03.880
but that's not what it was about
for most of the people. And to

02:24:03.900 --> 02:24:10.540
get at what it was about is a harder
pull, and explaining what union then

02:24:10.600 --> 02:24:16.180
meant to an era where it doesn't
mean anything. I mean, union as they

02:24:16.190 --> 02:24:20.130
understood it does-- it's not in
our vocabulary anymore. It has

02:24:20.160 --> 02:24:23.860
to do with labor relations now. But to say,
"What is-- but what does the concept of

02:24:23.960 --> 02:24:26.570
union mean?" Get crickets.

02:24:27.060 --> 02:24:33.260
- Is it basically a strong sense of nation?
So, like, what... Can we just kinda

02:24:34.160 --> 02:24:38.300
pull that apart? So there's
different states, and

02:24:39.960 --> 02:24:43.800
uh, Americans have always had a sense
of, like, "I'm from this state" pride--

02:24:44.400 --> 02:24:45.020
- Absolutely.

02:24:45.100 --> 02:24:45.200
- ... being from the state.

02:24:45.210 --> 02:24:49.070
- You have different loyalties.
You have a state pride and

02:24:49.120 --> 02:24:53.060
and a and maybe even a regional
pride. I think... I wrote

02:24:53.100 --> 02:24:57.000
a little book where I talked about Robert
E. Lee in that regard as part of it, where

02:24:57.060 --> 02:25:01.000
he has a sense of loyalty to
the nation, to the state, to

02:25:01.040 --> 02:25:04.580
the slaveholding South,
and so forth. But union,

02:25:05.400 --> 02:25:09.200
I mean, it's composed of individual
states which engender loyalties

02:25:09.240 --> 02:25:12.640
among the people who live in them,
but the parts make a greater whole,

02:25:12.720 --> 02:25:16.910
and that's what the union is,
and you have to have the whole--

02:25:18.164 --> 02:25:19.664
To get all the benefits.

02:25:19.724 --> 02:25:23.504
Because... And that is what people
thought then, and they tied it to the

02:25:23.544 --> 02:25:27.484
revolution, of course, and tied it to
earlier sacrifice, and tie- They had an eye

02:25:27.544 --> 02:25:31.464
on what's going on in Europe. I
mean, the Western world is, was

02:25:31.524 --> 02:25:35.374
their world. They didn't care that much about the
rest of the world. But the Western world, what's

02:25:35.404 --> 02:25:40.944
going on there, and I think they believed
things were going the wrong way in much of

02:25:41.004 --> 02:25:44.364
Europe, that the revolu-- the all the

02:25:44.424 --> 02:25:51.094
revolutionary fervor of the 1840s
had seemed to run into a cliff of o-

02:25:51.364 --> 02:25:54.424
oligarchy and aristocracy
and monarchy, and,

02:25:56.324 --> 02:26:00.664
and they saw the US as a bulwark
against that for small D democracy.

02:26:01.224 --> 02:26:03.824
- I think having a faith in nation

02:26:05.364 --> 02:26:09.464
is a beautiful thing. It's a
narrative that unites a people.

02:26:09.504 --> 02:26:13.404
And I think that in itself has power.
It's not ... You can make an economic

02:26:13.464 --> 02:26:17.204
argument, you can make a freedom
argument, all these arguments you can

02:26:17.264 --> 02:26:21.244
make, but I think what makes
a nation is a bunch of

02:26:21.284 --> 02:26:25.184
people believing in a narrative. It's
like what makes a great company. There's

02:26:25.244 --> 02:26:29.114
a mission. The power of humans
when you together believe in a

02:26:29.244 --> 02:26:33.154
thing is, is remarkable. And so that
belief in itself is really powerful.

02:26:33.584 --> 02:26:34.534
- That clearly was there,

02:26:35.614 --> 02:26:39.624
Among many, many, many, many. Not everybody,
of course, but it was widespread.

02:26:40.664 --> 02:26:45.054
And Lincoln articulated it- ...
perfectly, and he did it again and again,

02:26:45.904 --> 02:26:49.664
talking about give, give the ...
You wanna give a person a chance to

02:26:49.724 --> 02:26:53.504
rise, a person... And talking
about the last best hope of

02:26:53.564 --> 02:26:57.264
Earth, and talking... I mean, he
got it, and he distilled it, and he

02:26:57.384 --> 02:26:58.424
conveyed it. And

02:26:59.284 --> 02:27:02.764
it's not as if he's telling people
things they don't already know, but he's

02:27:02.904 --> 02:27:08.404
reaffirming things in ways that
are eloquent and memorable.

