All right. So we were talking about, uh-

02:27:25.844 --> 02:27:26.283
- Where are we?

02:27:26.313 --> 02:27:26.783
- ... Antietam.

02:27:26.984 --> 02:27:27.304
- Okay.

02:27:27.444 --> 02:27:32.224
- And how that was, uh, the
moment when The Union realized-

02:27:32.253 --> 02:27:33.494
- Preliminary Proclamation

02:27:34.224 --> 02:27:38.044
- ... Preliminary Proclamation, where it's
like, okay, slavery has to be a thing

02:27:38.144 --> 02:27:42.644
as a central part- ... of the Civil War.
And so where does that take us? Also

02:27:43.164 --> 02:27:48.154
very bloody battle. The generals, McClellan,
the end of the McClellan chapter,

02:27:49.284 --> 02:27:53.184
and that takes us to Fredericksburg.
So what happened at Fredericksburg?

02:27:53.224 --> 02:27:54.664
- Fredericksburg was a

02:27:55.784 --> 02:27:59.724
... It's another victory for Lee. It's an
easy victory for Lee. He has a very strong

02:27:59.784 --> 02:28:03.664
position on high ground across the
Rappahannock River from the Union

02:28:03.744 --> 02:28:07.564
forces, who are opposite Fredericksburg.
His opponent is a guy named Ambrose

02:28:07.604 --> 02:28:11.424
Everett Burnside, who's a very
affable guy, kind of a nice guy, who

02:28:11.484 --> 02:28:15.424
gave us the word sideburns. He
wore his whiskers that came

02:28:15.484 --> 02:28:17.284
down and came up and made, and-

02:28:17.524 --> 02:28:17.724
- Nice

02:28:17.764 --> 02:28:21.404
- ... it wasn't a beard, and they
were called burnsides at the time.

02:28:21.444 --> 02:28:24.764
The burnside cut, which was
transposed into sideburns.

02:28:25.844 --> 02:28:27.284
Everybody liked him. He just wasn't

02:28:28.104 --> 02:28:32.004
very bright in some ways. He's a
politician after the war, so he

02:28:32.044 --> 02:28:38.674
went into the right field, but His idea at
Fredericksburg- ... was to, in the end,

02:28:39.624 --> 02:28:43.424
it wasn't his original plan, but he ended up
launching frontal assaults one brigade at a

02:28:43.464 --> 02:28:46.864
time against very strongly entrenched
Confederates, and it was a butchery

02:28:47.904 --> 02:28:50.704
for ... And another horrible defeat
for the Army of the Potomac.

02:28:51.644 --> 02:28:55.514
Lincoln was really cast
down by it, and it just

02:28:55.584 --> 02:28:59.444
seemed that in this Eastern theater, he

02:28:59.504 --> 02:29:03.364
could not get any good news. He's getting
good news from the West. He's not

02:29:03.404 --> 02:29:07.033
getting any good news here, and he has
this revolving door of commanders.

02:29:07.104 --> 02:29:10.904
Burnside tried another
march in late January

02:29:10.944 --> 02:29:14.684
1863. He was gonna get around Lee's
flank, but he had horrible weather.

02:29:15.104 --> 02:29:18.044
His army literally got stuck
in the mud. Then it was

02:29:18.384 --> 02:29:22.294
lampooned as the Mud March,
and he removed him from

02:29:22.384 --> 02:29:26.344
command. So now he has another commander
in the Army of the Potomac. So you've had

02:29:26.404 --> 02:29:30.124
McClellan and Burnside, and
now a guy named Joseph Hooker.

02:29:31.484 --> 02:29:35.364
You have no continuity at the top in that
army. You have complete continuity at

02:29:35.404 --> 02:29:39.224
the top in the Army of Northern Virginia, which
is a huge advantage to the Army of Northern

02:29:39.244 --> 02:29:40.184
Virginia.

02:29:40.284 --> 02:29:44.214
- So this is where the, the famous
words of Robert E. Lee were...

02:29:44.254 --> 02:29:46.044
He was watching the slaughter.

02:29:46.054 --> 02:29:51.924
He said that "It is well that war is so
terrible, or we should grow too fond of it."

02:29:51.964 --> 02:29:52.904
What did he mean by that?

02:29:53.664 --> 02:29:58.264
- He was getting... I mean, he was
watching his soldiers butcher

02:29:59.084 --> 02:30:03.734
Union soldiers and, I guess,
kind of enjoying it. Uh,

02:30:04.784 --> 02:30:08.404
he's in a really strong position with
really guard artillery supporting his

02:30:08.464 --> 02:30:13.764
infantry, and the Union troops are
just attacking them and not having

02:30:13.844 --> 02:30:20.784
any success. And so there he is. He's up
there with James Longstreet and other

02:30:20.824 --> 02:30:28.344
people and watching it. And he, unlike
McClellan, was willing to risk,

02:30:28.524 --> 02:30:32.504
obviously, piling up casualties in every battle.
Didn't have that many at Fredericksburg.

02:30:32.524 --> 02:30:36.444
There were about 12 and a half thousand
US casualties at Fredericksburg and about

02:30:36.604 --> 02:30:40.184
5,500 Confederate casualties
because they were so

02:30:40.224 --> 02:30:42.424
well-protected in their defensive lines.

02:30:43.304 --> 02:30:49.824
- So you are working on a
manuscript on Chancellorsville.

02:30:50.204 --> 02:30:57.624
It's a legendary battle for many reasons.
Can you break down some of the military,

02:30:58.284 --> 02:31:02.224
details of what made up that
battle to give us a sense of,

02:31:02.284 --> 02:31:04.664
in general, what battles were like?

02:31:04.964 --> 02:31:08.724
- This is often described
as Lee's most successful

02:31:08.784 --> 02:31:12.524
battle. It's him against Joseph
Hooker. It's the last battle

02:31:12.764 --> 02:31:16.444
that Lee and Jackson fought as a team,
Lee and Stonewall Jackson, with Stonewall

02:31:16.544 --> 02:31:20.252
Jackson as his principal subordinate.
Hooker had replaced Burnside.

02:31:20.772 --> 02:31:24.652
Hooker had maneuvered to get
rid of Burnside. He wanted

02:31:24.692 --> 02:31:28.662
to be in command of the army. He, he had
made a statement that he said what the

02:31:28.662 --> 02:31:32.562
nation needed was a dictator to make
sure it won. And Lincoln, one of

02:31:32.592 --> 02:31:36.402
the best letters a president
ever wrote to a military

02:31:36.472 --> 02:31:39.342
commander, Lincoln wrote to
Hooker, and he said basically,

02:31:40.342 --> 02:31:43.452
"I know everything you're saying. You're
talking about all the things you're gonna

02:31:43.492 --> 02:31:47.372
do." And he said that, "The only
way you get to be a dictator is if

02:31:47.412 --> 02:31:51.372
you have victories, and I'm willing to
risk a dictatorship to get victories. And

02:31:51.512 --> 02:31:55.052
so I know I've got you figured out,
Joe," he's saying, and he did.

02:31:55.972 --> 02:31:59.812
Hooker made a lot of blustery talk.
He got a... He had a great start.

02:31:59.912 --> 02:32:03.832
He built the army into what he called
the greatest army on the planet, about

02:32:03.912 --> 02:32:10.392
135,000 men, beautifully supplied,
reorganized the medical service in it.

02:32:11.412 --> 02:32:16.912
He, he did everything that you
should do to get an army in shape,

02:32:18.052 --> 02:32:21.852
and then he started a campaign.
His plan, Lee was still at

02:32:21.892 --> 02:32:25.832
Fredericksburg. He was gonna get around
Lee's left flank, come in behind

02:32:25.852 --> 02:32:29.352
him, force Lee to either retreat to Richmond
or fight him on unfavorable ground.

02:32:29.392 --> 02:32:32.552
Everything went well with
Hooker. He got going in late

02:32:32.612 --> 02:32:36.332
April until the moment of truth came.
The moment of truth came on May

02:32:36.372 --> 02:32:39.332
1st, 1863, when Lee,
instead of retreating,

02:32:40.972 --> 02:32:44.912
left part of his army to watch one part
of Hooker's and turned around to meet

02:32:44.932 --> 02:32:48.422
Hooker head-on near a crossroads
called Chancellorsville

02:32:49.432 --> 02:32:53.412
a few miles west of Fredericksburg.
And as soon as the fighting

02:32:53.492 --> 02:32:55.802
started, McClellan lost his nerve-

02:32:55.932 --> 02:32:58.722
... pulled into a defensive
position. And the upshot was

02:32:59.872 --> 02:33:03.402
after Lee and Jackson carried off
a very successful flank attack,

02:33:04.112 --> 02:33:07.932
although it left Hooker still in a very
strong position, Hooker in the end

02:33:07.952 --> 02:33:11.912
retreated, and it was another victory for

02:33:11.992 --> 02:33:15.832
Lee in a battle he never, ever should
have won. He was outnumbered two to one,

02:33:15.952 --> 02:33:18.632
135,000 to about 64,000.

02:33:19.492 --> 02:33:23.292
And when the moment of truth came, Joseph
Hooker just lost his nerve. I mean, I

02:33:23.352 --> 02:33:26.272
really do believe that. I had fun
working on this book. I wrote a

02:33:27.271 --> 02:33:31.212
... A lot of books on campaigns are very long
and detailed. I wanted a short book that would

02:33:31.272 --> 02:33:36.472
introduce interested readers who didn't
really know that much perhaps to ...

02:33:37.472 --> 02:33:38.352
There's one of the maps.

02:33:38.812 --> 02:33:42.552
- So there are different, there's different
positions, the different troops' positions.

02:33:42.592 --> 02:33:43.172
There's Hooker.

02:33:43.552 --> 02:33:47.282
- Yes. That is the ... The battle
played out on May 1st, 2nd, and

02:33:47.492 --> 02:33:50.992
3rd. There's the opening
of the campaign. See the

02:33:51.032 --> 02:33:52.732
Rappahannock River, Fredericksburg.

02:33:53.392 --> 02:33:57.292
The armies are facing each other
there. Hooker's plan was to leave

02:33:57.452 --> 02:34:01.172
part of his army to hold Lee's attention
and move the rest up the river,

02:34:01.392 --> 02:34:05.362
up the Rappahannock, cross the
Rappahannock and then the Rapidan, and get

02:34:05.392 --> 02:34:08.912
behind Lee so that Lee
would be caught in a vise.

02:34:08.972 --> 02:34:12.742
He'd have Hooker in his rear and
the anvil of other Union troops at

02:34:12.792 --> 02:34:16.732
Fredericksburg, and he thought that that would
force Lee to retreat toward Richmond and that he

02:34:16.772 --> 02:34:21.552
could then harass him. They get around,
they get behind Lee. They do exactly what

02:34:21.612 --> 02:34:28.052
Hooker wanted to do. He got to
Chancellorsville. He had more than

02:34:28.292 --> 02:34:32.272
70,000 men at Chancellorsville,
and Lee doesn't find out till the

02:34:32.312 --> 02:34:35.872
very end of April what's going on, and
then he decides to divide his army,

02:34:36.712 --> 02:34:40.632
leave 10,000 men to watch 40,000
Federals at Fredericksburg, and he

02:34:40.652 --> 02:34:44.412
and Stonewall Jackson head back
toward Chancellorsville to

02:34:44.472 --> 02:34:48.392
meet Hooker. And they
meet on May the 1st. The

02:34:48.432 --> 02:34:52.092
fighting starts on May the 1st.
Everybody's upbeat in the Army of the

02:34:52.112 --> 02:34:55.692
Potomac. They've gotten around Lee's flank. They
know they're behind him. Everything seems to

02:34:55.732 --> 02:34:59.692
go very well. Hooker said,
"This is it. We've done

02:34:59.732 --> 02:35:03.412
it. We've done exactly what I wanted to do." And
then the fighting starts the next day on May

02:35:03.512 --> 02:35:03.772
1st.

02:35:04.632 --> 02:35:08.542
They march toward that place called Zoan Church.
You can see there, it's just a little bit

02:35:08.552 --> 02:35:12.372
east of Chancellorsville. And
the fighting, they collide about

02:35:12.412 --> 02:35:16.262
11:00 in the morning. 2:00 that
afternoon, Hooker orders a retreat.

02:35:17.032 --> 02:35:22.852
Everybody retreats back into this wilderness.
It's a kind of cut-over, woodsy area,

02:35:23.792 --> 02:35:27.482
that it has very few roads, and
he just hunkers down around

02:35:27.552 --> 02:35:30.952
Chancellorsville, hands
the initiative to Lee.

02:35:31.802 --> 02:35:35.432
The next day, Jackson
and Lee decide to send

02:35:35.492 --> 02:35:39.332
Jackson all the way around the
right flank, and you see the

02:35:39.372 --> 02:35:43.292
flank march there. They come,
they strike a Union corps

02:35:43.352 --> 02:35:47.332
that's exposed, commanded by a man named
Oliver Otis Howard. Howard University

02:35:47.372 --> 02:35:51.512
is named after him. He headed the
Freedmen's Bureau later in the war.

02:35:52.632 --> 02:35:56.472
They strike, they crush the 11th
Corps, which has a very high

02:35:56.532 --> 02:36:00.512
German element in it, and there's a lot of ethnic
prejudice that comes into play there by the

02:36:00.552 --> 02:36:06.012
other soldiers. But it ends up, the
day ends up with Lee actually in worse

02:36:06.412 --> 02:36:09.992
shape than he was before. He's got two
divisions over here under Anderson and

02:36:10.032 --> 02:36:13.762
McLaws on the lower right.
He's not even in touch with

02:36:13.812 --> 02:36:17.532
Jackson's corps. Jackson was
wounded that night by his own men,

02:36:17.772 --> 02:36:19.972
carried from the field.
He'd die a few days later.

02:36:21.112 --> 02:36:25.072
And Hooker still, although Hooker is in
between these two parts of Lee's army, he's

02:36:25.112 --> 02:36:29.082
in an ideal position. He's still just
thinking about the defense even. Where on

02:36:29.112 --> 02:36:30.292
the morning of May 3rd,

02:36:31.132 --> 02:36:35.262
the two parts of Lee's army fight their
way together around the Chancellorsville-

02:36:35.262 --> 02:36:38.952
... clearing, and Hooker then retreats
toward the north. You have this

02:36:39.012 --> 02:36:42.992
amazing moment on May the 3rd
where Hooker, who's been stunned

02:36:43.032 --> 02:36:48.292
by an artillery round that hit a pillar he
was leaning against at Chancellorsville-

02:36:48.372 --> 02:36:52.582
... he had a concussion. He was
vomiting. He retreated to the rear.

02:36:53.612 --> 02:36:57.492
And as the Union troops retreated from
that clearing near Chancellorsville,

02:36:57.532 --> 02:36:58.932
Lee's army

02:36:59.832 --> 02:37:03.682
had fought itself together, and Lee rode
into the middle of his troops. And there was

02:37:03.712 --> 02:37:06.912
this amazing moment when this spontaneous

02:37:07.012 --> 02:37:10.432
outpouring of emotion on the
part of the Confederates, that's

02:37:10.812 --> 02:37:14.492
the moment when Lee and his men finished
this period of bonding this year that

02:37:14.552 --> 02:37:18.962
was unmatched in the Civil War. See, there's
no way Lee should've won this battle.

02:37:19.032 --> 02:37:22.912
There's one more day of action a
little farther to the east. But in the

02:37:23.012 --> 02:37:26.582
end, Hooker retreats. He never should've
retreated. He was in an impregnable

02:37:26.612 --> 02:37:28.572
position, but he did retreat.

02:37:29.436 --> 02:37:33.416
And Lincoln was absolutely
beside himself after this. He

02:37:33.616 --> 02:37:37.186
just couldn't ... Everybody knew what the numbers
were. The newspapers had talked about it-

02:37:37.195 --> 02:37:40.316
... that Lee had only half as
many men as Hooker, and once

02:37:40.356 --> 02:37:44.306
again, the Army of the Potomac, the
largest and most important army of

02:37:44.316 --> 02:37:49.156
the Republic, has been defeated
by this smaller army. It,

02:37:49.996 --> 02:37:53.836
Lee's sort of legend in the
North grew and in the South.

02:37:54.156 --> 02:37:57.726
He became, by this point, he and his
army were the most important national

02:37:57.796 --> 02:38:01.656
institution in the Confederacy. People
looked to them to see whether indep- It's

02:38:01.716 --> 02:38:05.536
like Washington and the Continental Army
during the Revolution. If they're still in the

02:38:05.596 --> 02:38:08.466
field, there's still a
chance for victory. This

02:38:09.036 --> 02:38:12.566
cemented that position
for Lee, this victory at

02:38:12.596 --> 02:38:16.416
Chancellorsville did, in the minds of
Confederates, and made him a kind of

02:38:16.436 --> 02:38:18.275
bugaboo to people in the
North. They were just

02:38:19.166 --> 02:38:22.276
thirsty for someone to defeat
Lee and capture Richmond.

02:38:22.636 --> 02:38:25.906
- So this, in some sense, created
the legend of Robert E. Lee.

02:38:25.936 --> 02:38:29.296
- It certainly helped solidi-
Yes, it did. It went a long way

02:38:29.656 --> 02:38:31.836
toward doing it. A long way.

02:38:31.976 --> 02:38:35.196
- So you write about, you know, the
difference between sort of reality and

02:38:35.256 --> 02:38:41.936
memory. How does the North remember
this battle, and how does the South

02:38:41.976 --> 02:38:42.756
remember this battle?

02:38:43.176 --> 02:38:47.016
- It's really fascinating. The North
remembers this battle by not remembering it

02:38:47.096 --> 02:38:47.556
very much.

02:38:47.586 --> 02:38:47.676
- Yeah, ignoring it.

02:38:47.696 --> 02:38:52.276
- They sort of airbrush it out of their
memory of the war. The Confederates,

02:38:53.576 --> 02:38:57.326
in the iconography of the
Lost Cause, this ... Lee and

02:38:57.356 --> 02:39:01.316
Jackson had their last meeting ever here
just before the fighting on May 2nd got

02:39:01.416 --> 02:39:05.376
going, and there was a famous painting
of that done by an artist named

02:39:05.436 --> 02:39:13.256
Julio that was then reprinted as a
lithograph that hung in innumerable homes

02:39:13.296 --> 02:39:16.896
and offices and courthouses in
the South. It's Lee and Jackson

02:39:17.516 --> 02:39:20.976
at high tide, kind of. Gettysburg
hasn't happened, never mind

02:39:21.076 --> 02:39:24.976
Appomattox. These are the two greatest
Confederate soldiers at their greatest moment

02:39:25.016 --> 02:39:28.976
of success. It became hugely
important in the Lost Cause

02:39:29.816 --> 02:39:33.556
telling of the war. On the
Union side, they didn't

02:39:33.596 --> 02:39:37.335
wanna talk about Joseph Hooker that much. They
had... They'd rather talk about other things.

02:39:38.015 --> 02:39:41.245
- Yeah, it's probably one of the most humiliating
battles of the war from the Union side.

02:39:41.256 --> 02:39:44.066
- It, I think it's, it's really,

02:39:45.856 --> 02:39:49.696
it's just a, it's amazing that Hooker lost this.
It's just amazing. And it shows the complete

02:39:49.856 --> 02:39:53.896
dominance of one personality over
another personality, in my view.

02:39:54.716 --> 02:39:56.906
- So Stonewall Jackson was shot, killed.

02:39:56.936 --> 02:40:00.826
- Shot by his own men, out trying to
reconnoiter and keep the battle going

02:40:00.836 --> 02:40:04.496
and exploit his successes,
carried back to the rear, and he

02:40:04.536 --> 02:40:07.736
died on May 10th, died eight days later.

02:40:08.196 --> 02:40:13.016
- So what impact did that have
of Stonewall Jackson's death?

02:40:13.536 --> 02:40:17.456
- Well, that had a hu-- That,
there was unbelievable mourning

02:40:17.496 --> 02:40:19.416
across the Confederacy about that,

02:40:20.516 --> 02:40:24.476
and even since, really interesting, even
some positive comments in the North

02:40:24.556 --> 02:40:28.525
about how he'd been a sor-- He was misguided,
but a very religious guy and all of this

02:40:28.556 --> 02:40:32.426
stuff, but they're quietly very, "That's
really good news for the United States that

02:40:32.556 --> 02:40:36.326
Stonewall Jackson is dead, and really
bad news for the Confederacy."

02:40:37.196 --> 02:40:43.036
- Outside the myth, did it actually have
a practical impact on, on the war?

02:40:43.716 --> 02:40:47.696
- I don't think so, and the people who love Stonewall
Jackson would disagree with me, but I don't see

02:40:47.796 --> 02:40:51.576
where he would've ... He was a very
good soldier at what he did, and Lee

02:40:51.636 --> 02:40:55.376
used him very well, and they were
a good partnership, but I don't

02:40:55.476 --> 02:40:59.336
see how he would've changed the
larger contours of the war. I don't.

02:40:59.936 --> 02:41:03.636
- If we zoom out and look at Robert E. Lee,
somebody you've written about quite a bit,

02:41:03.716 --> 02:41:04.836
studied a lot,

02:41:05.816 --> 02:41:09.796
do you think we can use this battle to
try to understand the military genius of

02:41:09.836 --> 02:41:11.956
the man and, in general, who he was?

02:41:12.436 --> 02:41:15.756
- I do, and it shows both
why he was successful and

02:41:15.816 --> 02:41:19.585
why some of the reasons he was
successful opened the way for

02:41:19.656 --> 02:41:23.156
what should've been big defeats but
didn't. I mean, Lee's personality

02:41:23.256 --> 02:41:28.516
was, at heart, aggressive. His first
impulse was always to take the

02:41:29.336 --> 02:41:33.156
war to the opponent. He said, "They
outnumber us. If we just sit and wait

02:41:33.196 --> 02:41:36.936
for them to come to us, they're gonna have
all the advantages. We need to try to

02:41:36.956 --> 02:41:39.036
disrupt them and look for openings."

02:41:39.076 --> 02:41:42.266
What this campaign showed was
his audacity. He divided his

02:41:42.356 --> 02:41:46.266
army three times in the
course of this campaign in

02:41:46.296 --> 02:41:50.296
the face of a much more powerful
opponent, which showed his audacity.

02:41:51.275 --> 02:41:54.686
It also highlighted the overall

02:41:54.856 --> 02:41:57.736
culture that he created in his
army, which was a culture of

02:41:57.746 --> 02:42:01.736
risk-taking, of seeking to inflict
the greatest possible harm on your

02:42:01.776 --> 02:42:05.576
opponent. It's the antithesis of the
culture that McClellan created in the

02:42:05.656 --> 02:42:09.376
Army of the Potomac, which is a culture
of caution, of not taking risks,

02:42:09.876 --> 02:42:12.796
of not doing anything until
everything is just right.

02:42:13.116 --> 02:42:17.096
Any adult knows everything is never just right,
so if you're waiting for that, you'll never

02:42:17.106 --> 02:42:20.936
do anything. It's the, two
cultures are absolutely

02:42:21.016 --> 02:42:24.576
antithetical to one another,
and in the end, it shows

02:42:24.676 --> 02:42:28.896
Lee and how what he had
accomplished, he had created an army

02:42:30.716 --> 02:42:35.156
that was willing to absorb casualties
because it won all the time.

02:42:35.216 --> 02:42:38.996
And they realized that that was,
they believed, making it more

02:42:39.036 --> 02:42:43.016
likely that their whole war effort would
be successful. There are only a few

02:42:43.056 --> 02:42:46.266
times during the war where you get a scene
like you got on May 3rd, where this whole army

02:42:47.076 --> 02:42:50.876
sees the old man ride into their midst,
basically, and just has this ... I

02:42:50.896 --> 02:42:54.376
think they decided at
that point, the soldiers,

02:42:54.416 --> 02:42:58.276
that he was always gonna win, and Lee
looked at them and thought, "They can do

02:42:58.316 --> 02:43:02.196
whatever I ask them to do." I think if you
wanna understand Gettysburg, if you wanna

02:43:02.236 --> 02:43:03.636
understand Pickett's Charge,

02:43:04.476 --> 02:43:08.256
you need to look back to May 3rd at
Chancellorsville, when Lee just decided that his

02:43:08.316 --> 02:43:10.696
infantry could do anything
he wanted them to do.

02:43:11.136 --> 02:43:14.096
- But also from the infantry
perspective, they believed in the-

02:43:14.236 --> 02:43:18.216
- You asked me earlier about what did the
soldiers' letters say. The letters from

02:43:18.256 --> 02:43:21.616
Lee's army, they absolutely didn't even

02:43:21.716 --> 02:43:25.176
contemplate losing to the Army of the
Potomac. I mean, they just didn't think-

02:43:26.000 --> 02:43:27.580
They didn't think they would lose to it

02:43:27.940 --> 02:43:31.290
- What do you attribute that to? Is
that, does it, Robert E. Lee, to him?

02:43:31.500 --> 02:43:35.340
- It's to Lee and the fact that they won these
victories under Lee, and that they took

02:43:35.350 --> 02:43:39.200
these chances, and everybody tried. And they
always knew that they were outnumbered, and yet

02:43:39.240 --> 02:43:42.659
they were still winning. The closest
the numbers had ever been at the seven

02:43:42.740 --> 02:43:46.640
days when the armies were about the same
size, but after that, Lee is always at

02:43:46.700 --> 02:43:52.840
a significant disadvantage in numbers,
and yet he seems to be victorious.

02:43:53.240 --> 02:43:59.960
- So Lee's aggressiveness, like you've mentioned,
produced also staggering casualties.

02:44:00.240 --> 02:44:04.020
- Staggering casualties. He lost
13,000 men here, and Hooker about

02:44:04.060 --> 02:44:08.040
17,500. A much higher proportion

02:44:08.080 --> 02:44:12.020
of Lee's army than Hooker lost in
his army. But this should've been

02:44:12.060 --> 02:44:15.360
a disaster for Lee. Hooker
should have crushed Lee here.

02:44:16.260 --> 02:44:18.440
Absolutely should have
crushed Lee, but he didn't.

02:44:18.620 --> 02:44:22.320
- So there's two cases:
Lee's audacity being the

02:44:22.380 --> 02:44:26.360
South's best hope of
winning the war, but the

02:44:26.420 --> 02:44:29.580
other case is he was actually
bleeding the South dry.

02:44:29.920 --> 02:44:33.440
- Yes, and historians have criticized
him for not just being on the

02:44:33.500 --> 02:44:38.710
defensive all the time. I think Lee understood
better than many of his critics now

02:44:38.740 --> 02:44:42.160
understand that the key was
Northern civilian opinion.

02:44:42.200 --> 02:44:46.160
He said it more than once, "If we win
victories, it will depress their morale.

02:44:46.500 --> 02:44:50.400
If their morale is depressed, the Democrats
will do better. If the Democrats get into

02:44:50.500 --> 02:44:52.710
office, it's good for the Confederacy."

02:44:53.880 --> 02:44:57.860
And he also knew that he was ... It's a
race between, "Am I gonna run out of men

02:44:57.900 --> 02:44:59.470
before they run out of morale

02:44:59.520 --> 02:45:03.390
... in the United States?" I think
he understood that, and I think

02:45:03.460 --> 02:45:07.420
that was the best chance for the
Confederacy to win. And in the end, I'm

02:45:07.460 --> 02:45:10.980
very happy to report, the
Confederacy ran out of men

02:45:11.140 --> 02:45:15.100
before the Union ran out of
morale. And so the war went

02:45:15.110 --> 02:45:18.720
the way it did. But I think Lee was
very perceptive in that regard. He

02:45:18.780 --> 02:45:25.060
understood how important politically
what happened militarily was.

02:45:25.120 --> 02:45:29.000
Much better understanding of that than a
lot of people had at the time, and better

02:45:29.040 --> 02:45:33.000
understanding, I think, than a lot of
historians have now. And this is a horrible

02:45:33.800 --> 02:45:36.020
time for the US because

02:45:36.980 --> 02:45:40.900
behind the lines, people are in
ra- Democrats are unhappy with

02:45:40.940 --> 02:45:42.480
the Emancipation Proclamation.

02:45:43.500 --> 02:45:47.460
Just two months before this, Congress
passed the Conscription Act.

02:45:47.520 --> 02:45:50.960
Democrats are very unhappy about that.
They're saying that Lincoln is a tyrant.

02:45:51.120 --> 02:45:54.660
He's forcing people into a war that's
not the war they wanted to fight anyway.

02:45:55.560 --> 02:45:57.500
Union morale is very fragile.

02:45:58.460 --> 02:46:02.420
Morale, civilian morale at this
point, and this makes it much, much

02:46:02.800 --> 02:46:05.480
worse. The draft riots in New York City,

02:46:06.600 --> 02:46:10.440
the first draft numbers were pulled in
New York City in July. The draft riots

02:46:10.480 --> 02:46:13.840
come right after Gettysburg,
but that is symptomatic of what

02:46:13.880 --> 02:46:17.420
morale ... Desertions went up in the Army
of the Potomac after the Emancipation

02:46:17.500 --> 02:46:18.460
Proclamation.

02:46:18.580 --> 02:46:23.020
- So at this point, the Confederacy is
arguably very close to winning the war.

02:46:23.050 --> 02:46:26.420
- I think this is one of the
more optimistic points

02:46:27.160 --> 02:46:32.900
for the Confederacy, is in the late spring
and early summer of 1863. Yes, I do.

02:46:33.300 --> 02:46:37.020
- If Robert E. Lee is correct, in that it's
all about morale, which seems like a,

02:46:37.340 --> 02:46:42.880
a valid argument, then this feels, like,
very close to breaking the morale of the

02:46:42.960 --> 02:46:43.439
Union.

02:46:43.700 --> 02:46:47.560
- In the Eastern Theater, I think it is,
but in the Western Theater we have our

02:46:47.580 --> 02:46:52.210
boy Grant ... who's operating against
Vicksburg at this point. So there are two big

02:46:52.260 --> 02:46:55.880
campaigns kind of going on at this
time, and Vicksburg is the other one.

02:46:56.700 --> 02:46:59.380
- Can you speak to Vicksburg? This is '63.

02:46:59.440 --> 02:47:03.220
- Yeah, this is '63, and
Vicksburg is the last major

02:47:03.360 --> 02:47:06.980
Confederate citadel on the Mississippi
River. They've already lost New

02:47:07.020 --> 02:47:10.500
Orleans. They've lost ... So they've lost the
river. It's not a Confederate river anymore.

02:47:11.000 --> 02:47:14.580
But they still have a strong point at Vicksburg,
and another one at Port Hudson in Northern

02:47:14.620 --> 02:47:19.340
Louisiana. Grant has been operating
against Vicksburg since the winter of

02:47:19.350 --> 02:47:22.900
'62, and he's finally, through a
series of trial and errors and

02:47:23.020 --> 02:47:26.880
failures, he's finally found a way
that he thinks he can get to the

02:47:26.940 --> 02:47:30.780
city. He takes great risks.
He crosses the Mississippi

02:47:30.860 --> 02:47:34.680
River after having the Union
Navy run past all the batteries,

02:47:34.780 --> 02:47:38.720
the artillery batteries there at Vicksburg,
and he invests the city from the

02:47:38.800 --> 02:47:42.700
east. He's laying siege
to Vicksburg while he's

02:47:42.760 --> 02:47:47.320
just getting his campaign to do that
started when Hooker is at Chancellorsville.

02:47:48.640 --> 02:47:52.580
In May, he invests the city
and begins a siege that

02:47:52.590 --> 02:47:56.500
will end just as the Battle of
Gettysburg ends, and it's, it's one

02:47:56.540 --> 02:48:00.440
of the most impressive campaigns in
US military history, Grant's campaign

02:48:00.460 --> 02:48:04.360
against Vicksburg. Cuts loose from
his supply lines. Nobody understands

02:48:04.380 --> 02:48:08.160
what he's doing. Sherman doesn't get what he's
doing. Lincoln doesn't get what he's doing.

02:48:08.740 --> 02:48:12.600
And in the end they go, "Oh, that's
what you were doing." And it worked.

02:48:12.960 --> 02:48:18.840
And in the end, he captures Vicksburg,
which surrenders on July 4th, 1863.

02:48:19.680 --> 02:48:23.620
And if you don't think that a
kind of religious people didn't

02:48:23.680 --> 02:48:27.500
read divine intervention into
catching, having that happen on

02:48:27.540 --> 02:48:31.380
July 4th, 1863, you would be incorrect.

02:48:32.010 --> 02:48:34.950
That's the same day that Lee
retreated from Gettysburg. What?

02:48:35.990 --> 02:48:39.930
Really? God's not putting a thumb on
the scales here? Huge victories in

02:48:39.960 --> 02:48:43.400
the West, huge victory in the
East on the anniversary of the

02:48:43.460 --> 02:48:44.720
Declaration of Independence.

02:48:45.280 --> 02:48:46.940
- Well, that takes us to Gettysburg.

02:48:47.080 --> 02:48:48.400
- Here we are at Gettysburg.

02:48:48.510 --> 02:48:48.919
- Here we go.

02:48:48.940 --> 02:48:52.880
- Which everybody knows, Lex, is the
turning point of the Civil War.

02:48:53.400 --> 02:48:54.239
- Or is it? Okay.

02:48:54.300 --> 02:48:54.430
- No.

02:48:55.620 --> 02:48:55.690
- All right.

02:48:55.700 --> 02:48:56.320
- Short answer.

02:48:56.420 --> 02:49:00.340
- Well, that's first ...
Before we before we analyze

02:49:00.400 --> 02:49:02.920
the, its role as a turning point or not-

02:49:03.500 --> 02:49:03.530
- Yes, yes.

02:49:03.530 --> 02:49:05.780
- ... what is Gettysburg? July 1863.

02:49:05.840 --> 02:49:06.080
- Okay.

02:49:06.460 --> 02:49:08.460
- Walk me through the three
days of Gettysburg.

02:49:08.840 --> 02:49:12.500
- So Joe Hooker retreats across the
Rappahannock. Lee is frustrated because he

02:49:12.600 --> 02:49:16.400
hasn't dealt more damage to
Hooker, and this is what he

02:49:16.420 --> 02:49:19.830
decides to do. He decides to move
north for a second time. His

02:49:19.900 --> 02:49:23.880
army is strengthened. He builds
it up to 75,000. This is

02:49:23.920 --> 02:49:27.360
his army at its absolute apogee
as he gets ready to go north.

02:49:27.860 --> 02:49:31.780
It's confident. It's
well-supplied, well-led. And so

02:49:31.820 --> 02:49:35.780
they head north second week in June,
and here's what his goal is. He has two

02:49:35.820 --> 02:49:39.400
main goals. He wants to take the
war out of Virginia so that the

02:49:39.500 --> 02:49:43.340
agricultural sector isn't subject
to the presence of the army, so to

02:49:43.400 --> 02:49:47.280
let agriculture recover a little, and
he wants to provision his army off

02:49:47.320 --> 02:49:51.060
the rich Cumberland Valley in
Pennsylvania, siphon fodder and

02:49:51.160 --> 02:49:55.060
food and animals while he's
north of the Potomac, and he

02:49:55.100 --> 02:49:59.020
wants to stay up there for a long time to keep
the war out of Virginia. He doesn't wanna-- He

02:49:59.060 --> 02:50:03.000
has no... The movie Gettysburg, he
says he wants to capture Washington.

02:50:03.060 --> 02:50:06.920
No, he doesn't wanna capture Washington.
He knows it's too strong. He

02:50:06.980 --> 02:50:10.920
has no idea of doing that, but he knows he'll
pull the Union Army after him and simply

02:50:10.980 --> 02:50:15.000
reorient the war, and he knows it'll
be bad politically for Lincoln-

02:50:15.080 --> 02:50:18.740
... if he's actually operating in the
United States. So off he goes to the north.

02:50:19.180 --> 02:50:23.010
He gets north of the river. His
cavalry under Jeb Stuart is not

02:50:23.020 --> 02:50:24.800
doing its job because poor Jeb,

02:50:25.780 --> 02:50:29.240
who is a very good cavalryman but
has a sort of adolescent streak

02:50:29.780 --> 02:50:33.740
where he has to be praised all the
time, he fights a, the biggest

02:50:33.800 --> 02:50:37.520
cavalry battle of the war near
Culpeper, Virginia, Battle of Brandy

02:50:37.620 --> 02:50:40.140
Station on June the 9th,
and he almost loses,

02:50:41.240 --> 02:50:45.080
and it hurts his little feelings. And so
he decides that he needs to do something

02:50:45.160 --> 02:50:47.890
dramatic so the newspapers
will love him again.

02:50:47.950 --> 02:50:51.760
And he decides to ride all the way around
the Army of the Potomac, which he did when

02:50:51.820 --> 02:50:55.560
McClellan was in command. It's easier to do it
when McClellan's in command 'cause he's never

02:50:55.600 --> 02:50:58.720
moving. He decides he'll do it again here.

02:51:00.070 --> 02:51:03.980
And just as he gets east of
the Army of the Potomac, the

02:51:04.040 --> 02:51:07.820
Army of the Potomac starts to move
north, and so Stuart is trapped

02:51:07.860 --> 02:51:11.790
with... and he can't communicate with Lee. So
Lee doesn't know what's going on 'cause his

02:51:11.820 --> 02:51:15.200
cavalry isn't doing its job. He
spreads his army across southern

02:51:15.240 --> 02:51:19.320
Pennsylvania and then finds out
from a sort of civilian spy,

02:51:20.220 --> 02:51:24.000
guy named Harrison, that the Army of
the Potomac is actually following him

02:51:24.300 --> 02:51:28.240
and is much closer, and Lee at the
end of June orders his army to

02:51:28.280 --> 02:51:35.100
concentrate near Gettysburg and not to bring
on a battle, and they concentrate. Most

02:51:35.180 --> 02:51:36.140
of them come together.

02:51:36.940 --> 02:51:40.460
There's a little clash on June the
30th, but on July the 1st, the

02:51:40.539 --> 02:51:44.450
armies make contact to
the west of Gettysburg in

02:51:44.500 --> 02:51:48.450
a meeting engagement and more troops
coming on each side. It builds into what

02:51:48.480 --> 02:51:52.380
turns into the three-day biggest
battle of the Civil War. It's not

02:51:52.420 --> 02:51:53.500
something Lee had planned,

02:51:54.400 --> 02:51:58.370
And it wouldn't have happened if his cavalry
had been doing its job, but they weren't,

02:51:59.000 --> 02:52:02.440
and so here they come and there's
a battle that goes for three days.

02:52:03.120 --> 02:52:06.940
Lee has the-- He takes the
tactical aggressive every day. He

02:52:06.980 --> 02:52:10.940
does the attacking. The Federals
do the defending. Lee's army's

02:52:11.180 --> 02:52:15.240
75,000. The other army's
about 85,000, 88,000.

02:52:16.480 --> 02:52:20.320
And famous terrain, Little Round
Top, Round Top, Devil's Den, the

02:52:20.400 --> 02:52:24.320
Peach Orchard, all these famous places,
and it ends on the third day. Lee

02:52:24.360 --> 02:52:27.889
comes close on the second day, a huge
victory for the Confederates on the first

02:52:27.980 --> 02:52:31.460
day. Third day, Lee's frustrated

02:52:32.040 --> 02:52:36.000
and he just decides to let his infantry
take care of it for him and the

02:52:36.040 --> 02:52:40.020
result is what most people
call Pickett's Charge. Pickett

02:52:40.060 --> 02:52:41.560
was just a part of the charge.

02:52:42.540 --> 02:52:46.170
12 and a half thousand infantry
in a direct assault and it fails

02:52:46.200 --> 02:52:50.080
completely and he retreats
the next day. I think you

02:52:50.100 --> 02:52:51.820
can draw a straight line between

02:52:52.740 --> 02:52:56.640
May the 3rd at Chancellorsville
when his infantry fought

02:52:56.680 --> 02:53:00.540
their way together and July the 3rd
at Gettysburg. He admits it too as he

02:53:00.580 --> 02:53:04.560
writes letters to both his wife and Jefferson
Davis saying, "I asked too much of my infantry.

02:53:05.200 --> 02:53:08.000
No fault is on them. All
the fault is on me."

02:53:09.260 --> 02:53:12.820
And he was right. All the fault was on
him. He and Grant are alike that way.

02:53:12.860 --> 02:53:16.750
They accept responsibility, not like
McClellan. His first reaction is, "It's not my

02:53:16.780 --> 02:53:16.970
fault,"

02:53:17.780 --> 02:53:21.540
to pick a general at random who did this.
"It's not my fault. It must be somebody else's

02:53:21.620 --> 02:53:23.500
fault." Lee took responsibility.

02:53:24.860 --> 02:53:28.600
He expected too much from his infantry.
His subordinates had not been

02:53:28.660 --> 02:53:32.560
doing that good a job and they lost
the biggest battle of the war.

02:53:33.020 --> 02:53:36.780
- What was the military mistake there?
What could Robert E. Lee have done

02:53:36.820 --> 02:53:37.200
differently?

02:53:37.620 --> 02:53:41.609
- I think what he could have done, the most
perceptive Confederate soldier who wrote about

02:53:41.609 --> 02:53:45.600
the war was a guy named Edward Porter
Alexander who was the most gifted artillerist.

02:53:45.660 --> 02:53:47.840
He's in Lee's army. He
wrote after the war,

02:53:49.140 --> 02:53:52.890
Lee won a great victory on July
1st at Gettysburg, smashed two

02:53:53.060 --> 02:53:56.870
Union infantry corps, had a very
strong position. He should've just

02:53:56.900 --> 02:54:00.600
hunkered down and said, "Okay,
attack me. See what you can do."

02:54:01.920 --> 02:54:05.680
He didn't do that. He almost certainly
would've won the battle if he had done

02:54:05.760 --> 02:54:09.220
that, but he, his, this is where
his aggressiveness gets him in

02:54:09.260 --> 02:54:12.840
trouble. He was pushing for
a more aggressive, more

02:54:12.880 --> 02:54:18.400
decisive outcome right then and
he ended up costing... He lost

02:54:19.800 --> 02:54:23.560
probably 25,000 men at Gettysburg,
at least a third of his army.

02:54:24.140 --> 02:54:27.540
More than that, he went across the
river, went across the Potomac with

02:54:27.820 --> 02:54:29.840
52 generals in his army.

02:54:31.040 --> 02:54:34.800
17 of them became casualties.
He lost a third of his entire

02:54:34.860 --> 02:54:38.800
corps of generals killed, wounded, or
captured in the Gettysburg Campaign. It's a

02:54:38.920 --> 02:54:42.800
really bloody, costly campaign
for him and he ends up retreating

02:54:42.840 --> 02:54:48.300
back into Virginia. It comes to it, and
Lincoln is at first very happy about it.

02:54:49.700 --> 02:54:53.160
There's another, yet another general in
command on the Union side. Hooker's gone.

02:54:53.460 --> 02:54:56.950
A guy named George Meade
is in and Meade does what

02:54:57.500 --> 02:55:01.120
McClellan did after Antietam. He
lets Lee get away, doesn't really

02:55:01.140 --> 02:55:05.080
harass him, and Lincoln is beside
himself with disappointment. He

02:55:05.140 --> 02:55:09.090
writes a letter to Meade, thanks him for

02:55:09.120 --> 02:55:12.900
his great victory and then says,
"You've let him get away. The war is

02:55:12.980 --> 02:55:16.860
gonna continue indefinitely,"
says Lincoln. Then he doesn't

02:55:16.900 --> 02:55:20.260
send the letter to... He writes
it but doesn't send it to

02:55:20.320 --> 02:55:24.530
Meade, but he gets another guy to
tell Meade what's in it, so he-

02:55:24.530 --> 02:55:27.440
... a guy named Halleck who's the
general in chief at that point.

02:55:28.720 --> 02:55:31.780
Lincoln certainly did not think Gettysburg
was the great turning point of the war.

02:55:31.860 --> 02:55:33.620
He, he probably thought it should've been,

02:55:34.620 --> 02:55:40.420
but it wasn't because Lee got away with
his army intact. And after the battle, Lee

02:55:41.370 --> 02:55:45.510
said, "Well, you'll see." He said, "They
lost a lot too, and they're gonna be...

02:55:46.630 --> 02:55:47.960
There's not gonna be another big battle."

02:55:47.970 --> 02:55:52.080
He said, "They'll be as quiet as a
sucking dove- ... for six months."

02:55:52.490 --> 02:55:55.500
And in fact, it's 10 months before
there's another big battle in-

02:55:55.500 --> 02:55:57.200
... the Eastern Theater,
till Grant gets there.

02:55:58.090 --> 02:56:02.030
- Yeah, so how does Grant come from the, to
change the theater from the Western Theater

02:56:02.070 --> 02:56:02.680
into the Eastern?

02:56:03.050 --> 02:56:06.170
- So Gettysburg's a really important
victory for the United States.

02:56:06.250 --> 02:56:07.650
- Yeah, but it's not the turning point.

02:56:07.670 --> 02:56:11.530
- No, and it's not as important as Vicksburg,
because Lee gets away. Grant captured an

02:56:11.570 --> 02:56:15.460
entire rebel army at Vicksburg.
Pemberton's whole army surrendered,

02:56:16.590 --> 02:56:20.430
and that's the second time Grant had forced
an army to surrender. He forced one at

02:56:20.470 --> 02:56:23.330
Fort Donelson back in February of 1862.

02:56:24.430 --> 02:56:28.370
His victory is ... There
are no equivocations about

02:56:28.410 --> 02:56:32.350
it. It's a victory. The Mississippi
is now all controlled by the United

02:56:32.430 --> 02:56:36.350
States Navy, but Lee got away,
so that puts a little cloud

02:56:36.430 --> 02:56:37.970
over Gettysburg at the time.

02:56:38.910 --> 02:56:42.490
Grant wins one more victory in the
West after this. He wins the Battle of

02:56:42.530 --> 02:56:50.470
Chattanooga in late 1863. And that then
puts him in position for Lincoln to

02:56:50.510 --> 02:56:54.270
decide that he not only should be in charge
of everything in the West, he should be

02:56:54.330 --> 02:56:58.310
general-in-chief, and that's what
brings Grant to the fore. And in March

02:56:58.330 --> 02:57:02.100
'64, he's now general-in-chief
with the new rank of

02:57:02.130 --> 02:57:06.090
lieutenant general on his ... He's
got three stars on his shoulders

02:57:06.130 --> 02:57:07.590
now instead of two.

02:57:07.630 --> 02:57:11.210
- And then that where does that
take us? That takes us to

02:57:11.270 --> 02:57:16.010
'64 with the Overland Campaign. That
story plus Atlanta, Sherman's March.

02:57:16.450 --> 02:57:20.630
- That sets up ... Grant is now planning
for how the whole war is gonna go.

02:57:20.800 --> 02:57:24.790
And he envisions five components.
The two most important are he's

02:57:24.810 --> 02:57:28.790
gonna move against Lee, and his
plan is lock up with Lee, inflict

02:57:28.830 --> 02:57:32.470
the greatest possible damage on
Lee, prevent Lee from sending

02:57:32.510 --> 02:57:36.430
reinforcements anywhere else, and if all goes
well, Richmond would fall. But he's not mainly

02:57:36.450 --> 02:57:38.260
interested in Richmond. He's
interested in Lee's army,

02:57:39.310 --> 02:57:42.990
which is what Lincoln has been trying
to get his generals to be interested in

02:57:43.570 --> 02:57:46.770
the whole time. Destroy Lee's army,
and of course Richmond falls.

02:57:47.050 --> 02:57:50.370
- So how is he thinking about the
clash with Lee? Grant versus Lee.

02:57:50.750 --> 02:57:53.910
- Grant versus Lee is just confront him.

02:57:54.339 --> 02:57:57.930
And they're still near Fredericksburg, where
they, on the Rappahannock River frontier,

02:57:58.010 --> 02:58:01.190
engaged with him and
what they ended up with

02:58:02.130 --> 02:58:05.450
was a period of six weeks
of continuous combat that

02:58:05.490 --> 02:58:08.360
began with the Battle of the
Wilderness on May the 5th and

02:58:08.650 --> 02:58:12.590
6th. The old pattern in the Civil
War, Lex, was you fight a battle,

02:58:12.690 --> 02:58:16.130
one side retreats. It might be weeks,
might be months before you fight again.

02:58:16.730 --> 02:58:20.560
What happened here is Lee and Grant fought
the Battle of the Wilderness. 12,000

02:58:20.630 --> 02:58:24.550
Confederate casualties, 18,000
Union 30,000 casualties at the

02:58:24.570 --> 02:58:28.490
Wilderness. They march for one day.
They start another battle, Spotsylvania

02:58:28.590 --> 02:58:33.440
Courthouse. 12,000 More Confederate
casualties, 18,000 more Union casualties,

02:58:33.550 --> 02:58:37.230
so now you've had 60,000
casualties in two battles with no

02:58:37.270 --> 02:58:41.250
interval. That continues through May. Cold

02:58:41.330 --> 02:58:44.630
Harbor, they end up near Richmond
at Cold Harbor in early June.

02:58:44.950 --> 02:58:48.710
Grant launches frontal assaults
there. They come to nothing. His men

02:58:48.830 --> 02:58:52.650
are, many of them are butchered.
The bottom line is that in the

02:58:52.710 --> 02:58:56.670
six-week campaign, Grant
suffers 65,000 casualties,

02:58:56.790 --> 02:58:59.890
which is as big as Lee's whole
army had been. Lee suffers

02:58:59.970 --> 02:59:03.950
35,000, which is a higher percentage
of his army than Grant lost in, or

02:59:03.970 --> 02:59:07.910
about the same percentage,
actually. But it is a kind of

02:59:07.950 --> 02:59:11.850
continuous campaign no one had seen
before, and it shatters both armies.

02:59:12.030 --> 02:59:15.690
It eviscerates the officer
corps in both armies.

02:59:15.770 --> 02:59:18.270
armies. A lot of soldiers just break down.

02:59:19.190 --> 02:59:23.150
We would talk about PTSD now probably.
They just hadn't been used to something

02:59:23.230 --> 02:59:23.580
like this.

02:59:23.630 --> 02:59:28.570
- And this kind of continuous warfare,
that's ... We can attribute that to Grant.

02:59:28.970 --> 02:59:33.090
- Yes, this is ... And this is where
the Grant the Butcher image comes in.

02:59:33.130 --> 02:59:40.370
- But also probably, as hard as it is to say,
it's an act of military genius, right?

02:59:40.430 --> 02:59:44.370
- Well, it's ... He's doing what he wanted
to do. He's bleeding Lee's army and

02:59:44.410 --> 02:59:48.930
at the same time pushing it back toward
Richmond, and what it ends up in

02:59:49.850 --> 02:59:53.830
is the siege at Petersburg, Virginia, which
is just south of Richmond and controls

02:59:53.870 --> 02:59:57.570
the communications into Richmond, and
it's a siege of actually Petersburg and

02:59:57.610 --> 03:00:01.230
Richmond, and that is the thing that

03:00:01.370 --> 03:00:04.960
Lee feared the most, and Grant had
put him in exactly that position.

03:00:05.330 --> 03:00:10.660
But the perception behind the lines in
the United States is endless casualties.

03:00:11.630 --> 03:00:15.480
Who's winning? We can't even tell who's
winning. We have these casualties,

03:00:15.530 --> 03:00:17.700
and so Union morale drops

03:00:18.590 --> 03:00:22.580
to its lowest point of the entire
war in July and August 1864. Because

03:00:22.610 --> 03:00:25.220
while this is going on,
Sherman is moving against

03:00:25.290 --> 03:00:29.120
Atlanta, and he is taking
his time, not fighting these

03:00:29.170 --> 03:00:32.950
big battles, but he's not capturing Atlanta
either. The whole summer goes by. Atlanta

03:00:32.990 --> 03:00:36.980
hasn't fallen. Richmond hasn't fallen.
There's an election coming up in the fall in

03:00:36.990 --> 03:00:40.850
the United States, and Lincoln
decides ... The morale

03:00:40.870 --> 03:00:44.790
gets so bad he calls his
cabinet together in late August

03:00:45.990 --> 03:00:49.590
and makes them sign what's been called
the Blind Memorandum, and what the

03:00:49.610 --> 03:00:52.650
memorandum said is, "We're
not gonna be reelected,

03:00:54.050 --> 03:00:57.910
and we're gonna have to win the war before the Democrats
come in, because we know they won't win the war."

03:00:57.970 --> 03:01:01.730
Lincoln did not think they would win.
That's in August. And so I think

03:01:01.740 --> 03:01:05.610
that United States civilian morale,
the lowest point of the war for the

03:01:05.630 --> 03:01:09.530
United States is in July
and August 1864. And

03:01:09.570 --> 03:01:13.290
if we'd been able to stick our microphones
in people's faces and said, "Well, but what

03:01:13.370 --> 03:01:17.290
about Gettysburg? That was the turning point
of the war," they would've said, "What are you

03:01:17.350 --> 03:01:22.570
talking about?" "That was a year ago.
This is now, and we're not winning, and

03:01:22.610 --> 03:01:26.230
it's not worth it, and we're
just about ready." McClellan,

03:01:26.310 --> 03:01:30.070
incidentally, was the Democratic nominee for
president that year, so he's running against

03:01:30.110 --> 03:01:30.430
Lincoln.

03:01:30.820 --> 03:01:33.530
- So first of all, if Lincoln
didn't win, do you think-

03:01:34.010 --> 03:01:37.940
- Well, we know it would've been good for the
Confederates if Lincoln didn't win, but just, uh ...

03:01:37.970 --> 03:01:41.790
McClellan still ... It's interesting.
McClellan was running kind of

03:01:41.930 --> 03:01:45.370
against the Democratic platform. What
the Democratic platform called for

03:01:45.450 --> 03:01:48.200
was basically negotiations,
an armistice and then

03:01:48.210 --> 03:01:52.159
negotiations. And many people
believed if they ever stop fighting,

03:01:52.199 --> 03:01:56.090
they're not gonna start fighting again.
And so some negotiated end to the war

03:01:56.130 --> 03:01:59.490
that might even yield a confederacy.

03:01:59.530 --> 03:02:03.190
McClellan, to his credit, and I hate to
give him credit, at least said that he

03:02:03.290 --> 03:02:08.110
wasn't up for that. He said, "We need to win
the war, and then we'll have negotiations."

03:02:08.130 --> 03:02:11.790
But what's clear is... And it would've been,
would've been the end of emancipation.

03:02:11.990 --> 03:02:18.100
The, 'cause emancipation, what became the
13th Amendment had passed through the

03:02:18.190 --> 03:02:21.290
Senate in April of '64, but
the House voted against it.

03:02:21.590 --> 03:02:24.980
That doesn't, isn't gonna
happen till early '65.

03:02:25.750 --> 03:02:30.790
- How close was it that Lincoln won/lost?

03:02:31.230 --> 03:02:34.550
- Well, Lincoln won. Grant didn't
reelect Lincoln, 'cause he,

03:02:35.210 --> 03:02:39.150
Grant against Lee would not reelect Lincoln.
But other Grants, other things that

03:02:39.250 --> 03:02:45.019
Grant, part of his overall plan as
general-in-chief, Sherman captured Atlanta-

03:02:45.070 --> 03:02:48.770
... in early September. And
that just changed everything.

03:02:48.930 --> 03:02:50.010
- That was the real turning point.

03:02:50.050 --> 03:02:53.989
- That was a huge military turning point,
and then on the heels of that, Philip

03:02:54.070 --> 03:02:57.610
Sheridan won a series of crushing
victories in the Shenandoah Valley,

03:02:58.230 --> 03:03:02.110
the last of which came just before the
election, and it was Sherman, and to a

03:03:02.170 --> 03:03:05.990
lesser degree Sheridan, that
reelected Lincoln, and not just

03:03:06.050 --> 03:03:08.370
Lincoln, but huge Republican majorities-

03:03:08.430 --> 03:03:10.950
... in both the House
and the Senate in 1864.

03:03:11.560 --> 03:03:15.420
- And then the thing that broke the
back of Confederacies, Sherman's

03:03:15.510 --> 03:03:20.790
March, 60,000 men across Georgia to
the sea, burning the infrastructure.

03:03:20.830 --> 03:03:24.970
- Yes, as he said, "I'm gonna go across and
make Georgia howl," is how he put it.

03:03:25.890 --> 03:03:29.690
He added a psychological twist. What Grant

03:03:29.770 --> 03:03:33.710
envisioned his doing, and Sheridan actually
did it in the Shenandoah Valley before

03:03:33.750 --> 03:03:37.230
Sherman did it in Georgia,
is go into an agriculturally

03:03:37.310 --> 03:03:41.840
productive area, burn the mills, kill
the livestock, destroy the crops,

03:03:42.690 --> 03:03:46.250
lay waste to it so that it can't
be a logistical support of the

03:03:46.290 --> 03:03:50.270
Confederacy anymore. Sheridan did it in
the Shenandoah Valley, and then Sherman

03:03:50.310 --> 03:03:53.930
did it in Georgia. But Sherman said,
"But I'm also going to break them

03:03:53.950 --> 03:03:57.710
psychologically." That's what he said,
that, "I'm gonna show them that my

03:03:57.789 --> 03:04:01.450
army can go wherever it wants to go,
whenever it wants to go, and there's nothing

03:04:01.510 --> 03:04:05.430
Jefferson Davis can do about it." He said,
"When they decide that, they'll give up."

03:04:05.910 --> 03:04:09.370
Didn't exactly work that way, but
that's what he-- so he marched first to

03:04:09.430 --> 03:04:13.250
Savannah, and then the final campaign for

03:04:13.270 --> 03:04:17.090
Sherman was up through the Carolinas. He
went up through South Carolina and into

03:04:17.130 --> 03:04:21.510
North Carolina. And Grant laid siege to

03:04:22.570 --> 03:04:26.450
Lee, and the siege lasted nine months,
the Siege of Petersburg in Richmond,

03:04:27.210 --> 03:04:34.750
and finally Grant broke Lee's lines on
April 1st, 1865. Lee started a retreat.

03:04:35.950 --> 03:04:39.810
Grant just gave him no
pause, and he ran him to

03:04:39.850 --> 03:04:43.859
ground at Appomattox after about
a week, and Lee surrendered.

03:04:44.600 --> 03:04:47.310
- What do we know about the
relationship between the two men?

03:04:47.350 --> 03:04:48.170
- Grant and Lee?

03:04:48.230 --> 03:04:49.630
- Yeah.

03:04:49.670 --> 03:04:53.570
- Well, it's really interesting. I mean, they
basically operated in totally different

03:04:53.610 --> 03:04:55.690
worlds. They were both
in the war with Mexico,

03:04:56.530 --> 03:05:00.320
but they weren't with
each other in Mexico, and

03:05:00.790 --> 03:05:04.690
they had some awkward kinda small talk
at Appomattox before they got down

03:05:04.730 --> 03:05:08.579
to business. One said he remembered the other one,
the other one said he didn't remember him at all, and

03:05:08.730 --> 03:05:10.079
it's so ... And

03:05:10.890 --> 03:05:15.930
it... But Grant was very generous. He
gave 25,000 rations to Lee's army,

03:05:17.170 --> 03:05:20.930
and to survive. Lee began the
retreat with about 60,000

03:05:20.990 --> 03:05:24.850
men, only fewer than 30,000. His army
disintegrated in a week. He only

03:05:24.890 --> 03:05:30.910
surrendered 28,000 at Appomattox. They
were allowed to sign a parole and go home.

03:05:31.490 --> 03:05:35.470
- But at the peak of their,
you know Grant versus Lee,

03:05:36.070 --> 03:05:37.990
surely they were thinking
about each other.

03:05:38.050 --> 03:05:41.799
- They were, and I've always found this
interesting. They tended to be very

03:05:41.850 --> 03:05:44.660
generous toward other opponents.

03:05:44.950 --> 03:05:50.280
But Grant pretended that Lee wasn't
the best opponent he ever faced.

03:05:50.630 --> 03:05:54.350
And Lee pretended that Grant
wasn't the best opponent he

03:05:54.410 --> 03:05:57.340
ever faced, and it's ludicrous that
they would've actually thought that.

03:05:57.340 --> 03:05:59.850
They clearly found out
that that wasn't true.

03:05:59.870 --> 03:06:03.090
- It's interesting when people,
when people do that. I sometimes,

03:06:03.150 --> 03:06:05.790
like, wonder, how is it possible,

03:06:07.410 --> 03:06:10.870
um... There's just a million
examples of this. For example,

03:06:10.990 --> 03:06:15.170
Hitler just completely dismissed
America as a military force.

03:06:15.250 --> 03:06:18.810
- Oh, it's noth- they're a bunch of... Yes,
they're soft. They'll never do anything.

03:06:18.950 --> 03:06:22.050
- I have to say, it's probably
some silly psychological

03:06:22.090 --> 03:06:26.070
things. You s- you make up your
mind for some stupid reason.

03:06:26.090 --> 03:06:27.010
- And then you stick to it.

03:06:27.050 --> 03:06:30.940
- And you stick to it, and you almost,
like, against... I mean, with a lot

03:06:30.990 --> 03:06:35.740
of great generals and leaders, you have
to be stubborn in the face of facts

03:06:36.870 --> 03:06:37.420
sometimes-

03:06:38.030 --> 03:06:38.410
- You do, yeah

03:06:38.790 --> 03:06:42.620
- ... because, like, all the rational people
will tell you, you get this with Napoleon,

03:06:43.110 --> 03:06:47.070
like, "What are you doing?" Like, the
generals, the wise, smart people around

03:06:47.130 --> 03:06:48.250
you will say, "Don't do this."

03:06:48.350 --> 03:06:51.670
- Grant's a perfect example of that,
Lex, at Vicksburg, where he,

03:06:52.790 --> 03:06:56.610
he, his problem was to get his army on the same
side of the river as the Confederates, where he

03:06:56.630 --> 03:07:00.410
could come against Vicksburg, and the
only way to do it was have the Union

03:07:00.470 --> 03:07:04.390
Navy run p- and he said to do it. "No, don't
do it," said the Navy. "No, don't. No.

03:07:04.700 --> 03:07:10.350
Sherman, no. Don't..." Everybody said no,
and Grant said, "Do it." And it worked.

03:07:10.410 --> 03:07:14.290
- I mean, it has to do with the, with
I guess you've criticized once or

03:07:14.350 --> 03:07:16.150
twice M- this guy named McClellan.

03:07:16.370 --> 03:07:17.310
- I did, once or twice.

03:07:18.490 --> 03:07:22.590
- That you know, you do have to take
risk. You have to take risk in order-

03:07:22.600 --> 03:07:23.310
... to win battles.

03:07:23.450 --> 03:07:27.310
- And I'll finish w- I can't resist
getting one more shot. Lee is often

03:07:27.350 --> 03:07:31.110
quoted as saying that McClellan was the
most talented general he ever faced.

03:07:31.230 --> 03:07:32.070
- Really? Hmm.

03:07:32.160 --> 03:07:35.770
- Yeah, but that's, but what he actually
said, and the quote started with his

03:07:35.870 --> 03:07:39.710
nephew, Fitzhugh Lee, who wrote it
in a book after the war. Lee called

03:07:39.770 --> 03:07:43.668
McClellan the most intellectual
General he ever faced.

03:07:43.668 --> 03:07:47.578
And I think that is not a compliment.
I think Lee is saying, "Yeah, he's

03:07:47.598 --> 03:07:51.748
an intellectual. He thinks about this, he
thinks about ... He never does anything."

03:07:51.798 --> 03:07:55.778
"He thinks about things." So I think it's
actually a slight, not a compliment to

03:07:55.838 --> 03:07:57.178
McL- I really do.

03:07:57.378 --> 03:08:01.278
Because he couldn't have possibly thought
he was the most talented one that he,

03:08:01.478 --> 03:08:02.098
that he faced.

03:08:02.578 --> 03:08:04.978
- I think it's a polite wink
and nod to criticize.

03:08:05.198 --> 03:08:06.078
- It's a wink and nod.

03:08:07.098 --> 03:08:11.008
- Uh, all right. How does the
war end? So clearly there's

03:08:11.038 --> 03:08:13.998
the turning point with
Atlanta and Sherman's March.

03:08:14.237 --> 03:08:17.858
- And the election of '64 is the that
drives the dagger into ... I mean,

03:08:18.138 --> 03:08:18.948
there's no question

03:08:19.798 --> 03:08:23.718
but that the war will be pushed to the end
because Lincoln and Grant are in charge

03:08:23.758 --> 03:08:25.898
and they are not gonna
take their foot off the-

03:08:25.908 --> 03:08:29.758
... the pedal. They're gonna push it.
So it's a question of time. It drags

03:08:29.838 --> 03:08:31.498
on, still drags on another six months.

03:08:32.998 --> 03:08:37.438
Lee surrenders on April 9th. They
lay down their arms on April 12th.

03:08:38.398 --> 03:08:42.278
There are still tens of thousands
of Confederate soldiers under arms

03:08:42.358 --> 03:08:45.998
in different places, but it's a
measure of how important Lee and his

03:08:46.078 --> 03:08:50.598
army were that for everybody, virtually
on both sides, that's the end of the war.

03:08:50.878 --> 03:08:54.798
Lee surrendered. There's no reason to push
this any farther, and other surrenders came

03:08:54.818 --> 03:08:58.718
pretty quickly. Jefferson Davis was one
of the few people who didn't figure

03:08:58.798 --> 03:09:02.598
that out. He thought something was still possible,
but it wasn't. That was the end of the war.

03:09:02.658 --> 03:09:06.618
The final surrenders are over in June,
and that's it, and everybody gets

03:09:06.658 --> 03:09:10.617
the same terms that Grant offered to
Lee. His friend Sherman tried to do

03:09:10.698 --> 03:09:14.178
more, and they had to reign Sherman
in and say, "You can't ... No.

03:09:14.698 --> 03:09:17.318
You're a general, not a
politician. You can't do this."

03:09:17.858 --> 03:09:21.838
- So can we talk about April 9th? I mean,
this is a really important moment of

03:09:21.858 --> 03:09:27.338
what Grant did in terms of how
he treated the opposing army.

03:09:27.778 --> 03:09:31.568
- As I said earlier, he treated him
the way that he knew that Lincoln

03:09:31.638 --> 03:09:35.628
wanted them treated and, and it may be
that Grant thought exactly the same

03:09:35.738 --> 03:09:39.298
thing. But I think this is a
measure of how Grant was a

03:09:39.478 --> 03:09:43.158
very ... He's a soldier who
knew his place. He has civilian

03:09:43.198 --> 03:09:46.958
superiors. Other generals
didn't act that way. I know I'm

03:09:46.998 --> 03:09:50.758
beating up on McClellan. He's one. Joseph
Johnston on the Confederate side is one.

03:09:51.118 --> 03:09:54.238
They didn't really defer to their
civilian superiors. US Grant did,

03:09:55.198 --> 03:09:59.118
and he ... And this is one of those
cases. He sent the rations. He,

03:09:59.338 --> 03:10:02.958
he didn't know that Confederates
supplied their own horses. If you're a

03:10:02.998 --> 03:10:06.938
Confederate cavalryman, the government
didn't buy a horse for you. You had to

03:10:06.958 --> 03:10:10.888
supply your horse, and if a horse got
killed, you had to buy another one

03:10:11.418 --> 03:10:15.278
or they'd make you an infantryman.
When Grant found that out, he let them

03:10:15.378 --> 03:10:19.318
take their horses home with them. He said
they'll need them for the planting and so

03:10:19.438 --> 03:10:20.978
forth. He did everything

03:10:21.958 --> 03:10:25.478
in a generous way that you could
reasonably think might happen in a

03:10:25.518 --> 03:10:29.017
situation like ... It's still an unconditional
surrender. You are surrendering.

03:10:29.078 --> 03:10:32.088
You're going home. You're
not an army anymore. But

03:10:32.918 --> 03:10:34.678
nobody's going to prisoner of war camps.

03:10:35.738 --> 03:10:39.438
Nobody's doing those things. You lay your arms
down, you lay your flags down, and you lay your

03:10:39.478 --> 03:10:41.618
ammunition down, but that's it.

03:10:41.878 --> 03:10:45.778
- Yeah, but there's certain symbolic actions
like this that can lay the groundwork

03:10:45.838 --> 03:10:47.218
for long-term peace.

03:10:47.638 --> 03:10:51.598
- Yes. He didn't demand that Lee give
him his sword. He didn't do that, none

03:10:51.638 --> 03:10:53.688
of those kinds of things
that you could have done.

03:10:54.308 --> 03:10:58.028
- Sorry to linger on it, but there's
certain elements of humiliation that

03:10:58.058 --> 03:11:01.688
prevent long-term peace. I
have to go back to Hitler

03:11:02.738 --> 03:11:05.598
and what Germany experienced
at the end of World War I.

03:11:05.838 --> 03:11:08.858
- You mean Versailles, the terms from
Versailles. No question about it.

03:11:08.898 --> 03:11:12.838
- And some of it is economic, some of
it is geopolitics, but some of it

03:11:12.898 --> 03:11:15.918
is just on a basic human
level humiliation.

03:11:15.998 --> 03:11:19.818
And people are just not good
with humiliation. They remember.

03:11:19.878 --> 03:11:23.198
- No, and they can turn that around
into something you don't want it to

03:11:23.238 --> 03:11:27.138
be to get back national
pride. But Lincoln's

03:11:27.198 --> 03:11:31.098
bottom line was the point is to
put the nation back together

03:11:31.258 --> 03:11:34.898
and you ... The good way to start that is
not by imprisoning tens of thousands of

03:11:34.938 --> 03:11:38.707
Confederate soldiers. That's not the way to
do it. Send them home. Make them accept the

03:11:38.738 --> 03:11:42.338
terms, but then send them home
and leave them alone. The

03:11:42.378 --> 03:11:46.118
president wanted to go after Lincoln's
death. Andrew Johnson wanted to go

03:11:46.238 --> 03:11:49.998
after Lee, and Grant said, "I'll
resign if you do..." 'Cause I

03:11:50.818 --> 03:11:54.088
... His term ... We extended the
terms. He's abiding by the terms.

03:11:54.158 --> 03:11:55.638
You can't change the rules here.

03:11:55.878 --> 03:11:59.818
- But by the way, like it should be said, is
that that's not easy to do. You have just

03:11:59.978 --> 03:12:02.538
fought an extremely bloody
battle. There's hatred.

03:12:02.758 --> 03:12:06.578
- There were lots of people who wanted
Davis to swing and Lee to swing. "Kill

03:12:06.638 --> 03:12:10.338
them. Execute the rebels. They're rebels
and traitors who almost destroyed the

03:12:10.398 --> 03:12:13.818
nation. Why are you
being so easy on them?"

03:12:13.978 --> 03:12:17.798
Thaddeus Stevens was on board that
train. Lots of radical Republicans

03:12:17.858 --> 03:12:21.378
were. "You need to punish them for what ... You haven't
punished them enough. You're not punished them.

03:12:21.718 --> 03:12:22.278
Yes, you took

03:12:23.338 --> 03:12:27.238
slavery away from them, but that's not enough.
They should be punished more for what they

03:12:27.278 --> 03:12:31.898
did." "Confiscate their lands.
Take their wealth away from them."

03:12:32.878 --> 03:12:35.898
- So what happened next in
terms of reconciliation?

03:12:36.158 --> 03:12:39.558
- Well, Lincoln got assassinated
just a few days after-

03:12:39.758 --> 03:12:41.338
... Lee's troops laid down their arms.

03:12:41.498 --> 03:12:46.328
- Can you talk to the assassination? He was
shot five days after the surrender, right?

03:12:46.378 --> 03:12:47.018
- Yes, he was.

03:12:47.438 --> 03:12:50.218
- So who was Booth? Who
was John Wilkes Booth?

03:12:51.238 --> 03:12:54.798
Why did he assassinate Lincoln,
and what impact did that have?

03:12:54.888 --> 03:12:56.718
- Well, he was the youngest

03:12:57.538 --> 03:13:01.438
of the most famous acting family in
the United States. Junius Brutus

03:13:01.618 --> 03:13:05.248
Booth and Edwin Booth, his father
and older brother, were very famous

03:13:05.398 --> 03:13:09.358
actors, and he was a very successful actor,
too, John Wilkes Booth was. He was good at

03:13:09.418 --> 03:13:12.398
jumping around the stage,
and he was very athletic.

03:13:12.978 --> 03:13:17.068
Um, and an ardent pro-Confederate.

03:13:17.098 --> 03:13:18.528
- That's the more important- ... aspect

03:13:18.538 --> 03:13:21.678
- Not ardent, not ardent enough to put on a

03:13:21.778 --> 03:13:26.028
uniform for the Confederacy,
but ardent enough to, you know-

03:13:26.138 --> 03:13:30.118
... argue for the Confederacy. He
claims ... I mean, not claims. He,

03:13:30.278 --> 03:13:33.938
he heard Lincoln make a statement about
maybe Black soldiers getting the vote,

03:13:34.478 --> 03:13:38.378
and he said that was it for him, he ...
that somebody needed to kill the tyrant.

03:13:38.998 --> 03:13:41.318
This is very ... This is
after Lee had surrendered.

03:13:41.558 --> 03:13:44.438
- So he was on the Union side.

03:13:44.578 --> 03:13:45.558
- Oh, yeah. Oh, yes, he-

03:13:45.598 --> 03:13:48.758
- But Lincoln was a tyrant in his eyes.

03:13:48.958 --> 03:13:52.638
- Lincoln was a tyrant because of his sta-
because of his treatment of the white

03:13:52.698 --> 03:13:57.758
South. And he had plotted earlier
to kidnap Lincoln and hold him for

03:13:57.838 --> 03:14:01.758
ransom, and that didn't work out.
So then he got this little group of

03:14:01.818 --> 03:14:05.548
losers around him to help. I
mean, real misfits. Their,

03:14:05.658 --> 03:14:09.578
their plan was to kill Seward,
Andrew Johnson. They were gonna

03:14:09.998 --> 03:14:12.778
cut off the top leadership
of the United States.

03:14:12.898 --> 03:14:12.978
- Yep.

03:14:13.178 --> 03:14:16.518
- Kill the vice, kill the president, kill the
vice president, kill the secretary of state.

03:14:16.788 --> 03:14:18.138
That's what they wanted to do.

03:14:18.178 --> 03:14:20.498
- On the Johnson side, they chickened out.

03:14:20.578 --> 03:14:24.507
- That guy chickened out. A guy named Lewis
Powell was sent to kill Seward. Seward had

03:14:24.518 --> 03:14:27.578
been in an accident and had
his neck in a big brace,

03:14:28.518 --> 03:14:32.327
and he slashed at Seward, huge
scars on the side of his face,

03:14:32.418 --> 03:14:36.238
but the knife hit the brace and didn't
kill Seward. So Seward was disfigured. His

03:14:36.318 --> 03:14:37.078
son was wounded.

03:14:38.108 --> 03:14:42.358
The guy who was supposed to kill Johnson
chickened out, and Booth killed Lincoln.

03:14:42.718 --> 03:14:46.538
And there was a manhunt for Lincoln,
or for Booth. It took a while, and he

03:14:46.598 --> 03:14:50.118
was shot in a barn rather than
captured and put on trial.

03:14:51.058 --> 03:14:54.948
But four of the conspirators were
put on trial, including one woman, a

03:14:54.958 --> 03:14:58.858
woman named Mary Surratt, who ran a boarding house
where some of them stayed, and they were all

03:14:58.918 --> 03:15:04.577
hanged at Old Capitol Prison.
And the combination of th-

03:15:04.698 --> 03:15:08.398
that hanging, they were tried
by a military commission and

03:15:08.458 --> 03:15:12.448
hanged, but they also put on trial
and hanged the commandant of

03:15:12.518 --> 03:15:16.338
Andersonville Prison, which was the most
infamous of the Confederate prison of war

03:15:16.458 --> 03:15:20.268
camps. They hanged him as
well. And it seemed that

03:15:20.298 --> 03:15:24.198
those hangings sort of took
the edge, the sort of we

03:15:24.238 --> 03:15:28.148
need some retribution edge off
attitudes in the North. I think

03:15:28.198 --> 03:15:32.808
those things helped offset Lincoln's
assassination in a sort of odd way

03:15:33.698 --> 03:15:37.478
because the temperature
went down after that.

03:15:37.958 --> 03:15:40.488
- You talk about, as maybe
a bit of an aside,

03:15:42.078 --> 03:15:47.658
But that we Americans like to spin up
conspiracy theories about assassinations-

03:15:47.738 --> 03:15:48.018
- We do

03:15:48.098 --> 03:15:53.778
- ... of our presidents. So that's certainly
very true, maybe truer than most about

03:15:53.818 --> 03:15:55.018
Lincoln's assassination.

03:15:55.698 --> 03:15:59.498
- There was a long literature actually.
It began with a chemist named

03:15:59.578 --> 03:16:05.118
Otto Eisenschiml many, many decades ago
that implicated Secretary of War Stanton-

03:16:05.178 --> 03:16:08.838
... and blamed the radicals were
unhappy with Lincoln for not being

03:16:08.998 --> 03:16:12.858
harsher on the former Confederates,
and what they wanted to do was

03:16:13.118 --> 03:16:16.868
get him out of the way. They thought they
could manipulate Andrew Johnson if they got

03:16:16.938 --> 03:16:20.798
Andrew Johnson in charge. So they,

03:16:21.038 --> 03:16:24.878
then they would pose these questions. How
come no one was guarding Lincoln that night?

03:16:24.918 --> 03:16:28.558
How come the only bridge over the Potomac
that wasn't guarded is the one that Booth

03:16:28.618 --> 03:16:30.538
escaped across? All of these.

03:16:31.718 --> 03:16:35.638
How come Grant didn't go to the play
with Lincoln? The Lincolns asked them.

03:16:35.698 --> 03:16:39.578
Well, they didn't 'cause Mrs. Lincoln didn't like...
I mean, the, Mrs. Grant didn't like Mrs. Lincoln.

03:16:39.678 --> 03:16:43.398
I mean, you, there are answers to all the
questions. But they put all these together,

03:16:43.578 --> 03:16:47.328
added two and two and got
five, and said that it was,

03:16:47.618 --> 03:16:51.488
it was a plot on the part of the radical
wing of the Republican Party to get rid of

03:16:51.518 --> 03:16:54.738
Lincoln 'cause Lincoln didn't wanna
be harsh enough on the South.

03:16:55.558 --> 03:16:58.978
- Yeah, it's difficult to
believe that dramatic,

03:16:59.658 --> 03:17:01.988
sometimes catastrophic things happen for

03:17:02.118 --> 03:17:06.618
seemingly... That, that like
the random, as you said, loser

03:17:07.518 --> 03:17:11.468
- It's not satisfying. You want a more
satisfying, and that is a big conspiracy

03:17:11.498 --> 03:17:15.388
is better. There are all these forces at
work to get rid of Lincoln, not just that

03:17:15.438 --> 03:17:19.018
the one guy assigned to guard
Lincoln, there's no Secret Service

03:17:19.078 --> 03:17:22.638
then, went across the
street to get a drink.

03:17:22.778 --> 03:17:26.618
And Booth had played in that theater many
times. He knew exactly the layout. He'd

03:17:26.678 --> 03:17:30.038
come in earlier. He'd arrange... I mean,
it, it just, that's the way it worked out.

03:17:30.318 --> 03:17:35.438
- And I have to say, it, it's kinda interesting.
I have to be honest with my own mind

03:17:36.398 --> 03:17:39.097
that, like, I love a good
conspiracy. My brain does.

03:17:39.518 --> 03:17:40.818
- Every, almost everybody does.

03:17:40.858 --> 03:17:44.838
- I think it's, what is it?
It, it's maybe depressing or

03:17:44.938 --> 03:17:50.718
heartbreaking to know that so much
destruction can be caused by random events.

03:17:52.118 --> 03:17:55.798
You want there to be a larger

03:17:58.238 --> 03:18:05.078
system at play that it makes
you feel like there's a meaning

03:18:05.137 --> 03:18:11.638
to this whole thing as opposed to the tides
of history can turn on a stupid actor

03:18:11.998 --> 03:18:16.158
shooting one of the greatest, if not
the greatest president in the history

03:18:16.168 --> 03:18:19.328
- That's correct. Killing
him, jumping out of

03:18:19.378 --> 03:18:23.238
the presidential box, breaking
his leg when he hit the stage,

03:18:23.438 --> 03:18:25.898
limping off, and getting away. Yep.

03:18:26.058 --> 03:18:29.958
- Over 700,000 people dead, these epic

03:18:30.078 --> 03:18:34.918
battles, all of that. Lincoln
survives all of it, and then there's

03:18:36.178 --> 03:18:40.148
- When they had the grand review in Washington,
when Sherman's armies and the Army of

03:18:40.178 --> 03:18:43.598
the Potomac marched over
two days, this amazing

03:18:43.638 --> 03:18:47.518
display of the troops that had won the war,
and troops who had run the war, one of the

03:18:47.598 --> 03:18:51.498
most common comments was, "Lincoln's
not here. Of all people who

03:18:51.538 --> 03:18:54.858
should've been here, Lincoln should've been
here, and he doesn't get to watch this.

03:18:54.918 --> 03:18:57.078
He's not in the presidential
box watching this."

03:18:58.178 --> 03:18:59.598
And the soldiers talked about it too.

03:19:00.878 --> 03:19:04.638
- It's heartbreaking, really, especially
I would've loved to see his evolution

03:19:05.158 --> 03:19:13.108
as a leader, as a human being, and the
words he may have, he may have found

03:19:14.278 --> 03:19:18.398
to direct us as a country, you know,
in the, in the Reconstruction.

03:19:18.618 --> 03:19:22.118
- Yep. Would've been different.
We can't tell how different.

03:19:22.158 --> 03:19:26.438
But it would've been different, and I
think it would've worked more smoothly.

03:19:27.218 --> 03:19:31.498
- What was Reconstruction? How rocky was it?

03:19:32.238 --> 03:19:36.168
- The white South liked to talk about the
occupation of the South and how they were

03:19:36.198 --> 03:19:39.518
under the boot heel. There was
never an occupation of the former

03:19:39.578 --> 03:19:43.358
Confederacy 'cause there never were
enough troops available to do it. The

03:19:43.438 --> 03:19:45.588
most that were ever in
the former Confederacy

03:19:46.610 --> 03:19:50.230
Which is three quarters of a
million square miles in size, was

03:19:50.410 --> 03:19:53.630
30,000 men, and that was
for a very short period. By

03:19:53.650 --> 03:19:57.590
1870, there were only about
10,000 men left. And the reason

03:19:57.610 --> 03:20:01.350
there wasn't an occupation is because
the Northern population wasn't

03:20:01.390 --> 03:20:05.270
willing to either pay for it
or keep men in uniform long

03:20:05.290 --> 03:20:09.220
enough to make the White South do something
that they didn't really care about, which was

03:20:09.370 --> 03:20:11.410
give full equal rights

03:20:12.250 --> 03:20:16.010
to formerly enslaved Black people. The
White North just wasn't, they weren't

03:20:16.050 --> 03:20:18.810
willing to do that. They were willing,

03:20:20.010 --> 03:20:22.950
largely because of Andrew
Johnson's intransigence

03:20:23.770 --> 03:20:28.270
in the summer of 1865 when he started to
act like the South hadn't even quite lost,

03:20:29.250 --> 03:20:32.870
they were willing to push far enough
to get the 14th Amendment and the

03:20:32.910 --> 03:20:36.730
15th Amendment, and that to me is sort
of miraculous considering what the

03:20:36.830 --> 03:20:40.790
attitudes among white people were in the
United States. You got the vote given to

03:20:40.850 --> 03:20:44.530
African American men, and you
got the 14th Amendment. So it's,

03:20:44.650 --> 03:20:48.590
that's kind of amazing. And
those don't go away. They're

03:20:48.600 --> 03:20:52.210
still there on the books to be
interpreted correctly down the line

03:20:52.290 --> 03:20:55.930
somewhere, even though Jim
Crow is a long and ugly

03:20:55.990 --> 03:20:59.520
episode of what comes. I mean the
Confederates just, they weren't

03:20:59.550 --> 03:21:03.330
confused about being defeated.
They were crushed, and

03:21:03.390 --> 03:21:06.950
so they vastly scaled back what
they wanted. They had wanted a

03:21:06.990 --> 03:21:10.910
slave-holding republic. They
scaled back for just having white

03:21:10.950 --> 03:21:14.870
supremacy and the North, and
that's what happened. You

03:21:14.910 --> 03:21:18.560
ended up with white supremacy
and Jim Crow intact--

03:21:18.690 --> 03:21:21.690
... which is a lot different
than keeping slavery intact.

03:21:22.070 --> 03:21:28.590
- And that set off the whole process of the
Civil Rights Movement that lasted decades.

03:21:29.250 --> 03:21:33.910
- I mean, Jim Crow is the clearest
expression of the White South's

03:21:34.650 --> 03:21:38.570
reaction to defeat at
Appomattox. "Okay, what can

03:21:38.690 --> 03:21:41.330
we get out of this? We
can't get what we wanted.

03:21:42.170 --> 03:21:46.070
This is the next best thing, and that's
what we'll get. We'll get Jim Crow."

03:21:47.350 --> 03:21:50.850
- You know, when you walk the ground of
some of these battles, Gettysburg,

03:21:51.470 --> 03:21:54.230
is there something you can only
experience when you're there?

03:21:54.970 --> 03:21:58.630
- In my experience, you cannot replicate
in a classroom what you can do at a

03:21:58.670 --> 03:22:00.990
historic site, I mean a
really historic site.

03:22:01.790 --> 03:22:05.589
You can... You get people to a
place like that, I've given untold

03:22:05.630 --> 03:22:08.350
tours on battlefields and
other historic places.

03:22:09.230 --> 03:22:12.930
You can talk about so much more than just
what you can s- First of all, you can

03:22:13.230 --> 03:22:17.030
you can understand what happened on the battlefield
better than reading about it or looking at

03:22:17.050 --> 03:22:19.489
maps 'cause the terrain
makes it much clearer.

03:22:19.720 --> 03:22:23.690
But you can also talk about, okay, what
are the men like in the armies who

03:22:23.730 --> 03:22:27.570
are here? Why are they in the ser- A lot of the
things we've talked about today, what's the

03:22:27.690 --> 03:22:31.530
status of the war now? What are the
political situations behind the lines? What

03:22:31.570 --> 03:22:35.150
difference will this battle
make? What will happen if the

03:22:35.290 --> 03:22:38.970
Federals prevail or if the
Confederates prevail? Or you can

03:22:39.050 --> 03:22:42.620
use, at a place like Gettysburg,
the memorial landscape as a

03:22:42.690 --> 03:22:46.330
wonderful way to talk about memory from
the war. Look at the inscriptions on the

03:22:46.410 --> 03:22:50.290
monuments. What was important when they put
these up? And the later monuments, it's ...

03:22:51.370 --> 03:22:58.050
The opportunities for a teacher on one of
these sites are just almost unlimited.

03:22:58.570 --> 03:23:02.190
- And I don't want to be too woo-woo,
but I also believe from some of the

03:23:02.230 --> 03:23:06.570
experience I've had that the land has a
kind of memory. I don't know what it is.

03:23:06.630 --> 03:23:10.170
- They have a speech in the
cemetery at Gettysburg on the

03:23:10.230 --> 03:23:14.190
anniversary of Lincoln's Gettysburg
Address every year, and I was asked to do

03:23:14.250 --> 03:23:18.080
that a few years ago, and it
was just... It was amazing

03:23:18.310 --> 03:23:21.030
to me. I mean, there have been
very few things in my career

03:23:21.910 --> 03:23:25.890
that are like that, and we
want... We talked, we stopped in

03:23:25.930 --> 03:23:29.420
different parts of the cem- There were a
thousand people there. It was an extremely

03:23:29.430 --> 03:23:35.989
cold, windy November 19th, and it was
just... Talk about feeling the ghosts

03:23:36.070 --> 03:23:39.990
of the past. It was really something
that I've only felt a few times

03:23:40.030 --> 03:23:43.070
in my life. It was really
interesting, really interesting.

03:23:44.010 --> 03:23:47.990
- You've mentioned the Lost Cause a few
times. What was the Lost Cause? Who

03:23:48.050 --> 03:23:49.830
created it, and what did it claim?

03:23:49.870 --> 03:23:53.810
- The Lost Cause is the
Confederate memory stream of

03:23:53.850 --> 03:23:57.720
the war, and let me just say very, very
briefly, I think there are four main

03:23:57.750 --> 03:24:01.450
ways the generation that experienced
the war remembered it. The one

03:24:01.490 --> 03:24:05.400
remembered by the most people would be
the majority of white Northerners is what

03:24:05.550 --> 03:24:09.300
I call the Union Cause and that the greatest
outcome of the war was putting the nation back

03:24:09.310 --> 03:24:13.300
together. It's also good that slavery
was killed. That's important, but

03:24:13.330 --> 03:24:17.210
the most important thing was putting the
nation back together. The Emancipation

03:24:17.330 --> 03:24:21.310
Cause memory of the war is, yes, it's a good
thing that the Union was saved, but the

03:24:21.370 --> 03:24:24.970
most important thing is you killed
slavery. You got rid of this monstrous

03:24:25.030 --> 03:24:27.810
institution that had mocked
that language in the founding

03:24:27.850 --> 03:24:32.010
documents. So those are both the
winners' versions of the war.

03:24:32.970 --> 03:24:36.790
The Confederate version, the Confederates are
in a tough spot. How do we make something

03:24:37.190 --> 03:24:42.510
palatable out of this catastrophic
decision we made in 1861?

03:24:43.390 --> 03:24:47.310
We'll do this to save slavery, and four years
later, slavery's dead, and a third of all

03:24:47.350 --> 03:24:52.130
our white military-age boys are
dead. So here's what we're gonna do.

03:24:53.070 --> 03:24:57.050
First of all, it wasn't about slavery. Let's
pretend it wasn't about slavery. It was about high

03:24:57.170 --> 03:24:59.330
constitutional principles.
It was about states' rights-

03:24:59.510 --> 03:24:59.760
- States' rights

03:24:59.760 --> 03:25:02.330
- ... as opposed to central
authority oppressing people.

03:25:02.510 --> 03:25:03.620
- Right, right, right.

03:25:03.650 --> 03:25:07.430
- And second, we never could have
won. It was a hopeless fight

03:25:07.490 --> 03:25:10.730
against long odds, and so
it was a gallant effort in,

03:25:11.970 --> 03:25:15.930
in pursuit of constitutional
rights. Okay, that gets us off the

03:25:15.970 --> 03:25:19.730
hook there. And who are we gonna look to?
Should we talk about Jefferson Davis?

03:25:19.830 --> 03:25:23.770
No, we didn't even like him during the war. I know
who we'll talk about. Let's talk about Robert E.

03:25:23.850 --> 03:25:27.810
Lee. He won victories against
the odds. So these are kind

03:25:27.850 --> 03:25:31.610
of the main tenets of the Lost Cause. It
wasn't about slavery. We never could have

03:25:31.670 --> 03:25:35.590
won. There's no loss of honor in losing a
battle you never could have won, but that

03:25:35.670 --> 03:25:39.510
you waged for high constitutional principle.
And let's talk about Lee 'cause we don't

03:25:39.550 --> 03:25:43.370
wanna talk about Jefferson Davis, and there's
almost no one else to talk about except maybe

03:25:43.430 --> 03:25:46.569
Stonewall Jackson. Those will be our
two boys that we talk about the most.

03:25:47.410 --> 03:25:48.790
And that is basically

03:25:49.654 --> 03:25:53.594
The short form of the Lost Cause. The fourth
way that people remembered, and this came

03:25:53.614 --> 03:25:56.334
a little bit later, was the
reconciliation cause where you...

03:25:57.174 --> 03:26:00.944
This is kind of white Northerners and white
Southerners agreeing not to talk about slavery.

03:26:00.994 --> 03:26:04.734
Don't talk about emancipation. Let's
talk about shared American virtues of

03:26:04.834 --> 03:26:06.674
courage and so forth.

03:26:07.354 --> 03:26:11.114
- So some of that still persists
with us today. We look at the

03:26:11.194 --> 03:26:16.634
Confederate flag. So some historians argue
for the long, quote, "long Civil War."

03:26:16.734 --> 03:26:20.334
- Sure, and it's really long. It's the
Civil War that never ends kind of,

03:26:20.774 --> 03:26:24.623
and I certainly think that there
are ramifications from the Civil

03:26:24.694 --> 03:26:28.594
War that have never ended. But I think the Civil
War ended, and I think it ended in the early

03:26:28.674 --> 03:26:32.494
summer of 1865 with the destruction
of the Confederate government,

03:26:33.094 --> 03:26:36.854
the absolute dissolution of all
the Confederate military forces.

03:26:37.374 --> 03:26:41.294
I think that is the end of the Civil War.
People at the time thought it was, too. What

03:26:41.414 --> 03:26:45.334
comes afterward, the long coda,
Reconstruction is a 12-year

03:26:45.434 --> 03:26:49.254
coda to a four-year war that works
out some of the difficulties of

03:26:49.314 --> 03:26:51.564
how you put the nation back together.

03:26:52.794 --> 03:26:56.374
And then beyond that, you have these
different memory streams that have

03:26:56.414 --> 03:26:59.794
been contending with one another
that still... Some of which still

03:26:59.834 --> 03:27:03.454
reverberate today, and including
what are you gonna do with the

03:27:03.494 --> 03:27:07.374
Confederate flag, which most of... There are
a lot of nitwits around. They don't even

03:27:07.414 --> 03:27:10.254
know what the Confederate
flag was. They fly a

03:27:10.294 --> 03:27:13.974
rectangular version of
the St. Andrew's cross.

03:27:14.294 --> 03:27:18.154
That is not the Confederate flag.
They call it the Confederate flag.

03:27:18.194 --> 03:27:21.774
The Confederate flag that had a St.
Andrew's cross wasn't a rectangle, it was a

03:27:21.914 --> 03:27:25.614
square, and the actual
Confederate flag had three big

03:27:25.754 --> 03:27:29.814
bars and then stars up in... It looks
kind of like the United States flag.

03:27:30.754 --> 03:27:34.674
They don't even know that, and they go
around flying it everywhere. It's a

03:27:34.714 --> 03:27:38.394
symbol and pretend that it
represents all kinds of things. The

03:27:38.434 --> 03:27:42.294
Confederate flag cannot be divorced
from all the times, that version of it,

03:27:42.394 --> 03:27:45.784
the St. Andrew's cross version.
Every Ku Klux Klan group flew it.

03:27:45.794 --> 03:27:48.474
Every... It's... You just
can't sanitize it. And

03:27:49.374 --> 03:27:52.714
I mean, I saw Confederate flags in Britain

03:27:52.774 --> 03:27:58.174
and Ireland. I mean, it's become a sort of
up yours symbol I think in some places-

03:27:58.193 --> 03:28:01.854
... of just sort of rebellion, not
necessarily- ... tethered specifically-

03:28:01.894 --> 03:28:03.344
- Rebellion against inst- institutions

03:28:03.344 --> 03:28:05.434
- ... against authority and institutions-

03:28:05.444 --> 03:28:09.354
... and the ruling class and the... But
that's... But here it has a very specific

03:28:09.434 --> 03:28:12.814
meaning, and you just can't separate
it. It's... People should quit trying.

03:28:13.694 --> 03:28:19.374
- So you taught at UVA and lived
through Charlottesville in 2017.

03:28:20.334 --> 03:28:24.254
Can you tell me what Charlottesville
was? What did it mean?

03:28:25.234 --> 03:28:30.894
- It's really interesting because the
day just before that, I did a long

03:28:31.834 --> 03:28:35.514
interview with Katie Couric,
who was working on a s-

03:28:35.974 --> 03:28:38.543
on a documentary about
Confederate monuments.

03:28:38.584 --> 03:28:42.414
And she and I spent two hours
talking, walking around the

03:28:42.494 --> 03:28:46.194
UVA campus, talking about the Confederate
monuments were there. She was a

03:28:46.274 --> 03:28:47.594
student at UVA.

03:28:47.634 --> 03:28:51.294
She said, "I didn't even know these were here." I
said, "Well, most people don't know they're here.

03:28:51.354 --> 03:28:55.154
They're not aware of this, of this
part of the landscape." We had

03:28:55.234 --> 03:28:59.034
no idea. Her film crew went out
the next day and filmed what was

03:28:59.114 --> 03:29:02.834
going on when they actually
had the violence and the

03:29:02.934 --> 03:29:08.114
crowd at the Lee statue, which was downtown.
I didn't go down to watch any of that.

03:29:08.814 --> 03:29:16.494
- What's your view on Confederate monuments?
You know, the monuments that capture

03:29:17.034 --> 03:29:19.934
these periods, these complicated
periods of our history.

03:29:20.474 --> 03:29:24.354
- Well, I have complicated views,
but my main vi- But my, my

03:29:24.414 --> 03:29:28.204
first reaction to that is they're not all the
same. They've all... They're treated as if they're

03:29:28.274 --> 03:29:32.154
all exactly the same, and
they're not. There's some...

03:29:32.494 --> 03:29:36.334
There are the Silent Sam
mo- which was on the

03:29:36.854 --> 03:29:40.744
campus at UNC for years. It
had... Some of the rhetoric

03:29:40.774 --> 03:29:44.314
when they dedicated that is
really horrific. It's horrible

03:29:44.434 --> 03:29:48.164
rhetoric, and I can understand why that needed to
go. There's the... what was called the Battle of

03:29:48.274 --> 03:29:53.614
Liberty Place monument in New
Orleans that was a white supremacist

03:29:53.674 --> 03:29:56.134
monument very definitely that.

03:29:57.214 --> 03:30:01.114
I think that local places should
decide these. Wherever the

03:30:01.154 --> 03:30:04.754
monument is, I think that's who should decide,
if the local... And I think there should be a

03:30:04.794 --> 03:30:08.453
process how they decide
it. Let people have a

03:30:08.494 --> 03:30:11.914
public hearing. But they did that in
Charlottesville actually, had a public hearing.

03:30:12.034 --> 03:30:15.934
I went and talked at it. Lots of other
scholars and local people talked at it,

03:30:16.834 --> 03:30:20.153
and then they had a commission that
they had set up, and the commission

03:30:20.874 --> 03:30:27.634
voted to keep the monuments in place
but re-examine them with text that put

03:30:27.654 --> 03:30:31.394
them in context and then put
up monuments, new monuments to

03:30:31.414 --> 03:30:33.534
previously ignored groups.

03:30:35.794 --> 03:30:39.694
The city council then came in and overrode
that and decided they took them all down

03:30:39.754 --> 03:30:43.594
in Charlottesville. They also took down the
Lewis &amp; Clark monument, and there's a Geo-

03:30:43.744 --> 03:30:47.444
They took down all the monuments
that had been put up. I think

03:30:47.514 --> 03:30:52.394
many of the Confederate monuments, the kind
of ones on courthouse lawns and so forth

03:30:53.914 --> 03:30:57.474
that were put up by the Confederate
generation, that is the veterans

03:30:57.494 --> 03:31:00.894
themselves and the women who were
alive during the war, tend to be

03:31:01.034 --> 03:31:04.994
monuments to the common soldiers,
that just kind of lost generation

03:31:05.034 --> 03:31:11.144
of young men. The later
wave of monuments that came

03:31:11.934 --> 03:31:15.734
are... were put up by other groups that were
not... They're not really Confederate monuments.

03:31:15.814 --> 03:31:19.694
They're put up by... The ones that were put up
in the '20s and '30s. A third wave were put

03:31:19.754 --> 03:31:23.314
up in reaction to the civil rights
activities in the '50s and early

03:31:23.374 --> 03:31:26.974
'60s, and then a fourth wave
went up after the movie

03:31:27.034 --> 03:31:30.894
Gettysburg and Ken Burns' series. But
they're complicated, and I see them

03:31:30.934 --> 03:31:31.844
as historical...

03:31:33.134 --> 03:31:36.874
And I understand the arguments on both sides. I
understand people who just say they're hateful.

03:31:36.914 --> 03:31:40.454
They bring up hateful remembrances. We should
get rid of all of them, every one of them.

03:31:41.044 --> 03:31:44.634
I used them as teaching tools in
Charlottesville for years with groups of

03:31:44.694 --> 03:31:48.094
teachers and students. There were
five monuments in Charlottesville,

03:31:49.014 --> 03:31:52.708
Two that were put up by
veterans—two that were put up by

03:31:52.708 --> 03:31:57.228
groups later, the Lee and Jackson statues
were those, and then there were two

03:31:58.078 --> 03:32:01.678
bronze plates on the side of
the rotunda that just listed

03:32:01.738 --> 03:32:05.618
UVA graduates who died in Confederate
service. Had about 500 names on them.

03:32:05.658 --> 03:32:09.278
Those were all of the Confederate
statues there, and it— I used

03:32:09.318 --> 03:32:13.178
them to talk about different
strains of memory and used

03:32:13.258 --> 03:32:17.138
recon- They're— The Lee, the main
tenor of the Lee statue when it

03:32:17.178 --> 03:32:21.138
went up was this
reconciliationist Lee. He, Lee

03:32:21.238 --> 03:32:25.198
wanted the nation back together. Lee at Appomattox,
that kind of stuff. Didn't have anything

03:32:25.238 --> 03:32:29.078
to do with Jim Crow or white supremacy, although
all the people who put them up probably would've--

03:32:29.598 --> 03:32:33.138
Clearly, we would consider them racist.
They were in favor of white supremacy.

03:32:34.358 --> 03:32:37.818
But you don't need statues
in the post-war South

03:32:38.218 --> 03:32:42.158
and the Jim Crow South. You have
the police force, state law,

03:32:42.298 --> 03:32:46.198
local law, state troopers. That's
what enforces white supremacy,

03:32:46.278 --> 03:32:49.238
not statues. The historian in me

03:32:50.478 --> 03:32:54.198
likes to use them as part
of the historical record,

03:32:54.778 --> 03:32:58.248
interpret them, use them to explain
different parts of U.S. history.

03:32:59.398 --> 03:33:03.118
That's how I used them. Having
said all that, I did, I do,

03:33:03.258 --> 03:33:07.218
did, and still do believe that it should be a
local issue. If a locality decides they don't want

03:33:07.238 --> 03:33:09.458
them up anymore, then don't
have them up anymore.

03:33:09.878 --> 03:33:13.718
- I think if we ignore history, we're
gonna be doomed to repeat it. I think

03:33:13.758 --> 03:33:17.498
that's... We should never
censor books. We should

03:33:17.598 --> 03:33:20.258
read books. We should read
Mein Kampf. We should read,

03:33:21.398 --> 03:33:25.138
Books that capture a certain moment
in history, even if it's a hateful

03:33:25.198 --> 03:33:28.678
moment in history, even if it's a
destructive moment in history. We need to

03:33:28.718 --> 03:33:29.438
understand-

03:33:29.778 --> 03:33:35.198
- Or have them read texts written at
the time that have language that we

03:33:35.238 --> 03:33:39.078
wouldn't use now. It helps you
understand the ti- It's, it's-

03:33:39.458 --> 03:33:43.218
There are a lot of hard edges in
the past. There are a lot of nice

03:33:43.278 --> 03:33:47.218
edges too that make you feel good, but there are a
lot of really hard edges in the past, and if you're

03:33:47.238 --> 03:33:51.218
gonna be serious about coming to terms
with history, you need to accept

03:33:51.298 --> 03:33:55.118
that and deal with them and talk
about them and not just try

03:33:55.198 --> 03:33:59.108
to erase them, I think. There are
a lot of books that people won't

03:33:59.158 --> 03:34:01.937
read now. I taught a graduate
seminar at one point

03:34:02.898 --> 03:34:06.818
on foundational 19th century
texts, and one of the texts

03:34:06.998 --> 03:34:10.878
I was gonna— that I assigned in
the class was Huckleberry Finn.

03:34:12.398 --> 03:34:16.138
And a lot of the students said they wouldn't
read Huckleberry Finn because they knew it had

03:34:16.178 --> 03:34:18.688
language in it that they... And
they didn't wanna see those words.

03:34:18.838 --> 03:34:23.598
- Even just reading about slavery,
American and otherwise, is hard.

03:34:24.598 --> 03:34:28.218
It's really difficult. But,
like, we're capable of that. We

03:34:28.358 --> 03:34:32.058
humans are capable of that.
And this doesn't somehow go

03:34:32.198 --> 03:34:36.138
away because we agree for it to

03:34:36.178 --> 03:34:40.058
go away. We can always return back
to that, maybe in other forms,

03:34:40.578 --> 03:34:44.548
in other ways. We can do evil
things unto each other. And I,

03:34:44.858 --> 03:34:48.458
I personally believe all of us are
capable of doing evil things unto each

03:34:48.538 --> 03:34:54.498
other. And the dark
reality of that is we too

03:34:54.958 --> 03:34:57.738
readily convince ourselves
that we're doing good.

03:34:58.358 --> 03:35:00.938
- Yeah, because if you're doing
good, you can do anything-

03:35:00.988 --> 03:35:05.567
... and it's justified if it's,
if it's good. If the end is good-

03:35:05.618 --> 03:35:08.338
... all bets are off. You can
do anything that you wanna do.

03:35:08.398 --> 03:35:14.488
- And reading history to realize that,
uh, we need to think deeper and more

03:35:14.538 --> 03:35:18.507
critically about our actions and,

03:35:18.838 --> 03:35:23.028
and also that history is complicated,
and there's a bunch of different forces,

03:35:24.218 --> 03:35:29.168
Is really important. And
also to realize that

03:35:29.218 --> 03:35:35.178
individuals can, can turn the
tides of history, can like,

03:35:36.178 --> 03:35:39.958
can lead to progress, can
stop evil doing when it's

03:35:39.998 --> 03:35:43.478
happening. All of those things. Like,
the power of the individual is immense.

03:35:44.298 --> 03:35:47.558
- Yes. I mean, coming back to the
monuments for a minute, it, it's also

03:35:48.118 --> 03:35:51.798
worth talking about that even at the time
there were people who... A lot of Union

03:35:51.838 --> 03:35:55.758
veterans were against putting up Confederate
monuments. Frederick Douglass had

03:35:55.818 --> 03:35:59.658
a problem with the Emancipation
Monument that was in Emancipation Park

03:35:59.718 --> 03:36:03.558
on Capitol Hill and talked about the reasons
why he had a pro- "Why is this the image

03:36:03.567 --> 03:36:07.498
that you chose for that monument?"
Union veterans, they had a

03:36:07.537 --> 03:36:10.028
hard time with Confederate monuments, and

03:36:10.998 --> 03:36:14.818
I just think that it is... Almost
everything can be a teaching tool,

03:36:14.958 --> 03:36:22.418
un- but it bothers me that certain books
would be proscribed because they have some

03:36:22.538 --> 03:36:26.518
language that might bother somebody.
And, and, and it... You need to...

03:36:28.118 --> 03:36:30.318
I mean, the language
should bother you. So-

03:36:31.178 --> 03:36:36.758
- I think, I think history
truly should bother you.

03:36:38.198 --> 03:36:41.048
It, it, the, it should be difficult.

03:36:41.058 --> 03:36:44.058
It should be a painful process
to study human history

03:36:44.858 --> 03:36:48.758
because if it's not painful,
you're not really understanding

03:36:48.778 --> 03:36:51.958
the full range of what we're
capable of. But anyway,

03:36:54.338 --> 03:36:58.218
you know, people in the modern day,
I have to ask you about this, kinda

03:36:58.298 --> 03:37:02.698
casually talk about the
new Civil War in America.

03:37:03.198 --> 03:37:06.618
As somebody who studied the Civil
War, do you think our country

03:37:06.658 --> 03:37:10.558
today is divided to a
degree that's on the verge

03:37:10.618 --> 03:37:14.498
of something like the Civil
War of the 19th century?

03:37:14.598 --> 03:37:18.378
- No, I really don't. And I'm not pr-
And I don't mean to play down the

03:37:18.418 --> 03:37:23.678
degree to which... that we are divided.
We are divided. We, we do resemble the

03:37:23.698 --> 03:37:27.598
1850s now in that I think
the first impulse on

03:37:27.618 --> 03:37:31.158
the part of people on either side
of the political spectrum is to

03:37:31.198 --> 03:37:34.978
attribute the worst to the
other side and to, and to not

03:37:35.038 --> 03:37:38.478
acknowledge any, any good on the
other side, to attribute the worst

03:37:38.578 --> 03:37:41.048
possible characteristics
to the other side,

03:37:42.158 --> 03:37:46.018
and to have inflammatory
rhetoric coming from

03:37:46.058 --> 03:37:50.018
various directions that, that
really seeks to sort of stir

03:37:50.098 --> 03:37:53.758
up as much unhappiness and controversy
as possible. I think we're like the

03:37:53.778 --> 03:37:57.728
1850s in that way. But I-- But in terms of

03:37:57.758 --> 03:38:01.608
whether we're really at the point
where the kind of actions that I've

03:38:01.638 --> 03:38:04.138
dealt with in my study of the Civil War

03:38:05.678 --> 03:38:09.578
I, I don't think we're anywhere near
that. And I mean, I really don't. If

03:38:09.618 --> 03:38:13.398
we get there, that will be heartbreaking,
but I just don't, I just don't see it.

03:38:14.098 --> 03:38:17.958
I don't see physical assaults
against members of Congress on the

03:38:17.978 --> 03:38:24.958
floor of the Congress. I don't see...
It's, States talk about seceding if things

03:38:25.058 --> 03:38:28.958
don't go... Texas talked about seceding
when Obama was elected. California

03:38:28.998 --> 03:38:32.558
talked about seceding when Trump was elected.
But you know what? Neither one of them called a

03:38:32.618 --> 03:38:36.558
secession convention and had
delegates go to it. When Lincoln was

03:38:36.598 --> 03:38:40.378
elected, they called conventions, they
voted to secede, and they left the

03:38:40.538 --> 03:38:44.338
they left the nation. So that's
different to me than talking

03:38:44.418 --> 03:38:48.958
about it and threatening it and
posing different scenarios.

03:38:49.698 --> 03:38:53.458
- Are there genuine structural
parallels between that time and now,

03:38:53.818 --> 03:38:54.858
if you can linger on that?

03:38:55.178 --> 03:38:59.078
- Well, I mean, the states
structurally, a state can call a

03:38:59.118 --> 03:39:00.558
convention if it wants to,

03:39:01.838 --> 03:39:05.818
and to talk about whatever it
wants to talk about. So I suppose

03:39:05.878 --> 03:39:08.138
that structure is still in place,

03:39:09.058 --> 03:39:14.538
but I haven't seen any serious movement
toward doing something like that.

03:39:15.578 --> 03:39:20.778
- Yeah. And I think there's a real
cohesion and resilience to the Union,

03:39:21.698 --> 03:39:25.538
as we've talked about. I think people are,

03:39:25.598 --> 03:39:31.598
despite the discourse and the
bickering, there's a real

03:39:32.598 --> 03:39:39.518
love of nation that maybe is not
often expressed- ... explicitly. But

03:39:40.658 --> 03:39:43.238
there is a sense that we're
all in this kinda together,

03:39:44.198 --> 03:39:47.398
and that we're yelling at each other. Kinda
like you have a family Thanksgiving dinner

03:39:48.578 --> 03:39:51.118
where there's very passionate

03:39:52.438 --> 03:39:56.418
yelling and arguing, but there's a kinda
assumption that we're still a family.

03:39:57.138 --> 03:39:58.038
At least that's the hope.

03:39:59.918 --> 03:40:05.578
You know, one of the questions that the
Civil War asked is, What is America?

03:40:07.638 --> 03:40:11.318
So I have to ask you from over 50 years of

03:40:11.378 --> 03:40:16.547
studying the Civil War- ... like what,
what do you think? What is this country?

03:40:17.718 --> 03:40:23.838
What is this union? What is the
core that makes us who we are?

03:40:24.838 --> 03:40:28.678
- Well, I think in a lot of ways
it's much of what it was in the

03:40:28.718 --> 03:40:32.658
mid-19th century. I still think it's
a magnet for... I mean, we're in an--

03:40:32.758 --> 03:40:36.018
we're far more diverse now as a people
than we were during the Civil War

03:40:36.998 --> 03:40:40.678
because we continue to
act in the same way as

03:40:40.958 --> 03:40:44.718
it did in the Civil War, only they're not
Germans and people come from everywhere here

03:40:44.728 --> 03:40:47.498
now. I mean, they literally
come from everywhere here now.

03:40:48.338 --> 03:40:52.278
And everybody doesn't find a nice
easy road here by any means,

03:40:52.358 --> 03:40:53.867
and there are lots of things wrong

03:40:54.718 --> 03:41:00.898
with the United States. But I still
think the United States acts... Has a

03:41:01.718 --> 03:41:05.658
role in the world that's unlike, I
won't say any other place, but I can't

03:41:05.668 --> 03:41:09.587
think of a place that's quite like it, that
has so many people coming from so many places,

03:41:10.158 --> 03:41:14.138
who somehow manage to fit into
this mosaic. And then I think

03:41:14.198 --> 03:41:18.038
most people do have a way to fit into
the mosaic, and then they become part of

03:41:18.098 --> 03:41:21.918
the old mosaic when the next
group of people who are not like

03:41:21.978 --> 03:41:24.798
them come in, and those are the ones
who are the new ones fitting in.

03:41:25.538 --> 03:41:28.908
- Yeah, sometimes I think about,
I'm sorry to romanticize it,

03:41:29.178 --> 03:41:34.198
but because I do think that Barack Obama
was one of the great speech makers

03:41:35.118 --> 03:41:42.058
of my lifetime. And I sometimes imagine
what Lincoln would have thought

03:41:42.858 --> 03:41:48.858
if he could attend an Obama speech.
If he saw that Obama became

03:41:48.938 --> 03:41:52.908
president, that an African American
became President of the United States of

03:41:52.978 --> 03:41:57.258
America. It's, it seems like
such a trivial thing to say,

03:41:58.458 --> 03:42:02.418
But I think it's not trivial at

03:42:02.498 --> 03:42:04.638
all. It's one of the great
accomplishments of our country.

03:42:04.878 --> 03:42:11.338
- I think one of Lincoln's great attributes
was his humanity comes through

03:42:12.218 --> 03:42:15.998
so many things that he wrote.
His real appreciation for

03:42:16.038 --> 03:42:19.988
people who faced long odds,
worked against the odds, didn't

03:42:20.038 --> 03:42:23.358
begin with advantages in
their favor, and so forth.

03:42:23.578 --> 03:42:27.557
I think he and his ability to grow, I
think he would have handled that actually

03:42:27.658 --> 03:42:31.038
pretty well. Much better than
many other people I can think of

03:42:31.638 --> 03:42:37.328
from the mid-19th century. I think
he had genuine humanity. I think,

03:42:37.418 --> 03:42:41.218
I don't think you can read all the
things he wrote and not come to

03:42:41.278 --> 03:42:44.938
that conclusion. He also... But,
so does, does he check all our

03:42:45.018 --> 03:42:48.998
boxes now? Of course he doesn't. Was he
prejudiced by our standards in some ways?

03:42:49.038 --> 03:42:52.818
Of course he was. But I think that he,
I think he would have handled that

03:42:52.898 --> 03:42:54.138
pretty well, and he would have been,

03:42:55.038 --> 03:42:59.618
still been really tall. Even in our time-
... he'd be way taller than most people.

03:43:01.198 --> 03:43:05.498
- Yeah. What you know, 250th birthday,

03:43:07.238 --> 03:43:10.338
What gives you hope about
the future of America?

03:43:10.898 --> 03:43:14.878
- What gives me hope? I'm, and I'm,
and I'm a grouchy, not optimistic

03:43:14.998 --> 03:43:16.018
person in a lot of ways.

03:43:16.078 --> 03:43:16.088
- Are you?

03:43:16.158 --> 03:43:18.018
- Yeah, I am. I am.

03:43:18.078 --> 03:43:19.938
- You had some moments of optimism today.

03:43:20.238 --> 03:43:24.218
- I was just gonna say, but for, I do have
moments of optimism, and one of them

03:43:24.338 --> 03:43:32.078
I have is in the resiliency of this republic.
I really do have... And it's genuine

03:43:32.118 --> 03:43:35.948
optimism. I'm not just... And I know a lot of
people will think that it's naive, but I really...

03:43:36.088 --> 03:43:38.888
Because I'm, see what it's been
through, and it's emerged-

03:43:39.078 --> 03:43:42.818
... and it's gone forward. And the
trajectory, and this is something O-

03:43:43.058 --> 03:43:46.858
that Obama has commented on, and as have
many others, the trajectory is in the

03:43:46.918 --> 03:43:48.958
right direction.

03:43:49.078 --> 03:43:53.038
With lots of things you wish were different,
but the overall trajectory is in the right

03:43:53.078 --> 03:43:56.058
direction. I really do
believe that, and that's why

03:43:57.158 --> 03:44:00.258
I tend to be, in that regard, optimistic.

03:44:00.998 --> 03:44:02.518
- I think there's a few folks

03:44:03.338 --> 03:44:07.228
on the interwebs that talk about
the decline of the American empire,

03:44:07.918 --> 03:44:10.768
even using the word
empire. I'm one of the f-

03:44:12.138 --> 03:44:16.018
One of the folks who thinks that
America's flourishing. It's, it

03:44:16.078 --> 03:44:18.578
is a beacon of hope and
freedom in the world,

03:44:19.418 --> 03:44:21.738
and there's a lot to
criticize, the militarism,

03:44:22.658 --> 03:44:26.988
And a lot of different aspects
that are hypocritical,

03:44:28.578 --> 03:44:34.318
and is not always living up
to the great ideals that it

03:44:34.378 --> 03:44:38.078
supposedly represents, but at
least is striving towards that.

03:44:38.978 --> 03:44:42.828
And it is still a home
for the great diversity

03:44:42.878 --> 03:44:46.838
of humans, as you've mentioned.
That, I think, is one of the great,

03:44:46.858 --> 03:44:50.518
beautiful aspects about this country.
And the full mess of it, the drama,

03:44:50.678 --> 03:44:54.318
the arguments, the heated passion, the

03:44:54.398 --> 03:44:57.898
chaos, the thing that feels
like chaos but I think

03:44:57.998 --> 03:45:01.858
ultimately will lead to progress.
I love all of it. I think it

03:45:01.898 --> 03:45:04.358
is the greatest nation
on Earth. So on this

03:45:05.238 --> 03:45:09.478
250th birthday, I hope
there's 250 years more, and

03:45:10.358 --> 03:45:14.038
I'm truly grateful that
there's somebody like you

03:45:14.158 --> 03:45:18.958
studying our great history, and I'm grateful
for this wonderful conversation today.

03:45:18.988 --> 03:45:21.018
- I appreciate the
invitation. It's been fun.

03:45:21.118 --> 03:45:21.638
- Thank you, Gary.

03:45:22.758 --> 03:45:26.298
Thank you for listening to this conversation
with Gary Gallagher. To support this

03:45:26.358 --> 03:45:30.258
podcast, please check out our sponsors
in the description, where you can

03:45:30.318 --> 03:45:34.158
also find links to contact me, ask
questions, give feedback, and so

03:45:34.278 --> 03:45:37.818
on. And now, let me leave you with
some words from William Tecumseh

03:45:37.938 --> 03:45:41.158
Sherman, a United States
Army general in the Union

03:45:41.278 --> 03:45:45.378
Army during the Civil War.
"I am tired and sick of war.

03:45:46.358 --> 03:45:50.138
Its glory is all moonshine.
It is only those who have

03:45:50.198 --> 03:45:54.078
neither fired a shot nor heard the
shrieks and groans of the wounded

03:45:54.618 --> 03:45:58.758
who cry aloud for blood, for
vengeance, for desolation.

03:45:59.878 --> 03:46:06.798
War is hell." Thank you for listening,
and hope to see you next time.

